      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Origin of Surnames. I have long been interested in this subject, and my reading and observation have led me to the conclusion that, while there areltve sources from which English surnames may be said to have been derived, the great majority are place names. I was reminded of it by the appearance of Sandbach in last week's advertisement re- specting the Quarter Sessions to be opened at Knutsford on Monday next. I have an old friend named Sandbach. Wherever you meet a Butterworth, depend upon it his forefathers be- longed to a little place of that name by Roch- dale. Bracewell and hosts of others originated in like manner. When strangers with Un- familiar names came into a district in which I was living for the time being, I set to work to find out where they got them, and almost in- variably they were place names. There was one called Beer; turning the leaves of a little book on pillow-lace making, I accidentally discovered thal Beer is a Devonshire village. Many of my readers may have known that all along, but I have to confess my ignorance of it previously. My own name, the derivation of which puzzled me for some time, is that of a village in the county of Durham.</p>
        <p>" Baby's Bones." The paragraph thus headed in last week's ladies' column reminded me of little Paul .Pombey, who used to talk about his bones. The item in question warned those having the care of babies against careless nursing likely to de- velop curvature of the spine, with dire results. I know a little girl who on this account was en- cased in plaster of Paris corsets for two years, and who still suffers from,her back. It behoves mothers to be careful about such things, and I commend to them the diligent perusal of " Mar- jorie's" weekly contribution to these pages. Dickens.</p>
        <p>The foregoing incidental allusion to one of the Master's immortal creations suggests the remark that he has many more admirers among old, fellows like myself than he has in the later generation. Most of us who remember Dickens in the flesh have his books pretty well by heart. We have read and re-read, and return to them again and again. Several years ago I read the whole of them aloud to my wife. The other day a minister called on me for the first time, and as he cast a glance at my books his eye was ar- rested by an edition of Dickens, and he revealed the kindred spirit. "I should not like to tell you," said he, "how often I have read 'these. Would that I could forget it all, and begin again at the beginning !" To think that some young reader who may be favouring me with his atten- tion at this moment has all that joy and felicity in prospect. "Lucky Jim; how I envy him !"</p>
        <p>The Cactus. "In the Garden" I read last week of select- ing cactus dahlias, and the word " cactus " sent my mind back to a wonderful nursery in the village of Sowerby, in the West Riding of York- shire (there are other Sowerbys-A-one no farther from this than Thirsk). The proprietor, who is widely known as the Cactus Bing, takes pride in showing visitors every variety of cactus under the sun. The neighbouring industrial districts have their branches of the Workers' Educational Association, Adult Bible Classes, and Young Men's Mutual Improvement Societies—or had when I was there in pre-war times—and in rela- tion to these it used to be said of the Cactus Gardens, which were on a considerable emi- nence, "Thither the tribes go up." Like the famous Duke of York, their leaders "marched them up a great big hill and marched them down again," but they were repaid for their exer- tions.</p>
        <p>A Bit Depressing." Last week's extract with this headline re- minded me of an old registrar of deaths whom I may possibly have referred to before. Day by day we met going in opposite directions along the same road, and I always stopped to have a word with him, for ,a reason which will pre- sently appear. Once I noticed that he looked rather sad, and I asked if he was unwell. "No," said he, "thank you; I'm well enough in health; but when it comes to business, seems to me I'm heading for bankruptcy. There never were such times, I think; people in my district will not die!"</p>
        <p>The Apt Quotation. A lover of poetry, with a tenacious memory, this entertaining old acquaintance of mine would generally round off a conversation with an appropriate tit-bit from the classics, Shah- spere being prime favourite (my old edition of the imperishable bard 4o spells his name, and, so far as I have seen, Sir Walter Scott invtiri- ably renders it thus). As we parted, my inter- esting friend would bonclude: " Ah, well, as Coriolanus says," and the quotation pat would follow. When I referred to his health, he apos- trophised, _ _</p>
        <p>The chiefest good Bestowed by Heaven, but seldom understood</p>
        <p>If I twitted him with money-grubbing, he treated me to Johnson's lines on the fallacious- ness and Young on the vanity of wealth. A chance mention of adversity, ambition, friend- ship, or conscience would yield me apt quotations from Addison, Gray, Thomson, Rogers, Milton, Ben, Jonson,. Smollett, Joanna Baillie, Dryden, and, of course, Shakspere—all these and others 1 had from time to time until, on a black day for me, my oracle passed away with an appropriate verse on his lips. I cannot hope e'er to look upon his like again. The Jokers. _ _ .</p>
        <p>_ The " braking " of carts on the kerb stones deprecated in that same "Gossip," reminde I me how, for a period during my residence in Halifax, carts bringing stone down the :deep roads from quarries on the neighbouring heights had their.brakes so tightened as to emit a ter- rible din that could be heard far away. It came to be known as the "Corporation organ," but eventually the scandalised Corporation re- pudiated this; they adopted a bylaw forbidding the nuisance and threatening persistent perpe- trators with pains and penalties. This was quite justified, too, in the sequel, for the joker; who had been guilty of the outrage on every downhill journey easily found—to employ the apostolic phrase—a more excellent way than the ear-splitting performance to which they had been accustomed. And so will the local carters now that the Paving Committee have 'deter- mined to "go for" offenders.</p>
        <p>Those " Zepps." The reference to the Lymm Volunteers' Zepp drill in last week's district news leads me to remark that I have just had an opportunity of comparing notes with a friend who lives in the town that suffered most severely in the last air raid. He is a special constable, and he said that, although he had not been summoned, he inferred that there was something amiss, and that it was his duty to make for the place to -which he was appointed in. case of emergency. "So," Said he, "I tumbled out of bed, turnecl up the light, and was off in quicksticks." Turned up the light !" Fancy that! No wonder the German bounders managed to do them a mischief. They passed over a neighbour- ing town in which I happened.to be that night, but it was so enshrouded in darkness that they utterly failed to locate it. Having notice of their approach, the local authorities cut off the supply of gas and electricity at the source. I had imagined that to be the rule, but ray friend, the special constable, evidently considered it quite reasonable and ordinary that he should be able to turn on his bedroom light during an air raid.</p>
        <p>• Anonymous Letters. I once had a conversation on this subject with a police superintendent now living in retire- ment at Southport. I presumed that he would not condescend to notice an anonymous letter. "Yes, indeed,' said he; "not a little informa- tion of real valuehas been conveyed to me anonymously. For various reasons which one could appreciate, the writers did not care to dis- close their names, but their letters were often of considerable service." It appears, then, that there are anonymous letter-writers and anony- mous letter-writers—that the class should not come under wholesale and indiscriminate vn- demnation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 14, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 14, 1916.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED IN THE PiDVI4NeE.</p>
        <p>SERGEANT J. HEACOCK.</p>
        <p>Eldest Of Four Soldier Brothers Wounded In The Thigh.</p>
        <p>Sergeant James Heacock, of the Machine Gun Corps, who, before enlisting, lived, with his married sister at 13, Marsh House-lane, has been wounded in the right thigh, and is now in hospi,tal at Aberdeen. Sergeant Heacock, who is thirty-seven years of age, is the eldest of four soldier brothers. The others are Herbert, of the Machine Gun Section, South Lancs. Regiment,/ who was recently wounded for the second time and is at present in hospital; Thomas, of the A.S.C., and Bert, of the South Lancs. Regiment, in France. Percy, also of the South Lancs. Regi-</p>
        <p>SERGEANT J. HEACOCK. ' Wounded.</p>
        <p>ment, was discharged a few weeks ago owing to foot trouble.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Heacock, who was formerly employed at the Longford Wire Works, served his time in the old Volunteers, and later in the Territorials, and is the proud poSsessor of the long service medal. He was mobilised with the local battalion of the South Lancs. Regiment, and served in the Machine Gun Section, but has since transferred. _</p>
