      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>PRIVATE H. lIILL.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE H. lIILL.</p>
        <p>Youngest Of Three Patriotic Brothers</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. John Hill, 4, St. Mary-street, Latchford, have been informed by their son, Private Harold Hill, South Lancs. Regiment, that he is wounded and at present lying in Northumberland War Hospital, Newcastle-on- Tyne. -Writing from there, he says he is re- ceiving every attention, and that his thir.l finger is fractured _near the knuckle. _</p>
        <p>Private Hill enlisted in November of last year, and had been in France a month when he was hit. He is 18 years of age, and was employed by the St. Helens Cable and Rubber Co., Ltd., being an old boy of St. James' School, and a member of the Bible Class at that church. He was interested in football, playing for his works team. He has two</p>
        <p>PRIVATE HAROLD HILL.</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>brothers" serving, Private John Hill with the. New Zealand Contingent, and Albert Hill, now in training at the crystal Palace for the Nav•,-, with the intention of becoming a signaller in that service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>LANCE-CORPORAL E. HODGETT.</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL E. HODGETT.</p>
        <p>"Getting Every' Attention And Going On Well," Writes Chaplain.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Ernest Hodgett, son of Mr. T. Hodgett, 12, Appleton-street, has been wounded. Canon R. A. Adderley, chaplain, writing to Mr. Hodgett under date September 26th, said :—" Your son,. Lance-Corporal E. Hodgett, asked me to /et you know that he is in hospital suffering from shell wounds in the back and legs. He is getting every attention, and is going on as well as possible, but is very poorly. He hopes you will not. worry about him."</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Hodgett, enlisted in Septem- ber of last year, going to France six months ago. He is 19 years of age, and on leaving St. Ann's School, entered the employment of Messrs. R. A. Naylor, Ltd., but later trans- ferred his services to the Castle Rubber Co., where he was up to his enlistment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>CORPORAL A. JONES.</head>
        <p>CORPORAL A. JONES.</p>
        <p>Full Of Fight When Wounded A</p>
        <p>Second Time.</p>
        <p>Wounded for a second time, Corporal Arthur Jones, Cheshire Regiment, and who in peace times resides with his wife and two children at 7, Cornwall-street, is now in hospital in Eng- land. k</p>
        <p>, A tine-expired Reservist, on the outbreak of war Corporal Jones re-enlisted immediately and went to the Front with his old battalion in the Cheshire Regirpent. At the time he was work- ing for Messrs. Lever Bros., Port Sunlight, Previously he spent seven years in the Army. five of which were spent in India. He was in India during the Boer War. When in War- rington he was employed by the Richmond Gas Stove and *Meter Co., Ltd. He was on, leave twelve months ago for a period of three .days, soon after he had been wounded the first tim..,</p>
        <p>Corporal Jones has the professional soldier's temperament in excelsis, only a fortnight ago</p>
        <p>he wrote saying he was "interested in this campaign, and am •full of fight as the Devil !"</p>
        <p>RIFLEMAN JAMES WELSH.</p>
        <p>Farmer Moulder 'Wounded In The Knee.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. J. Welsh, 6, Sparling-street, have received a letter from their son, Rifleman James Welsh, of the Rifle Brigade, informing them that he is wounded in the left knee, and in hospital at Whalley, near Blackburn. Rifleman Welsh is twenty-two years of age. He is a moulder at Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co.'s works, for whose football ;team he played. He enlisted .in September, 1914, and has been at the Front fifteen months. He was educated at Our Lady's School, Latchford, and also attended that church, where he was an altar boy for six or seven years. His elder brother, Peter, of the Connaught Rangers, was reported wounded 'and missing in April, and no news has been received of him since, while the two solcliers—botbh Latchford Men—who enlisted with Rifleman Welsh, have both been</p>
        <p>RIFLEMAN J. WELSH.</p>
        <p>Wounded</p>
        <p>killed, viz., Riflemen A. Thompson and D. Walsh.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE E. XVELLINGS.</p>
        <p>Not Many Yards From The Huns' Trenches When Hit.</p>
        <p>"Just a. line to let you knoivi your husband has been wounded. You have no need to worry as I can assure you it is not serious. 1 dr,pssed him myself, and mpect he will be in England in a few days' time. The bullet passed through his forearm. We have all had a lively time, as no doubt you will see by the paper. At thz time 'Ted' got hit we were working in front of our firing line, not many yards from the Huns' trenches." The preceding letter was written by Private E. Bracegirdle, a stretcher-bearer, who himself recently died of wounds, sustained while engaged. in his work of mercy on the battlefield, to Mrs. Wellings, 48, Earl-street, concerning her husband, Private Edwin Wellings, South Lanca- shire Regiment. At present Private Wellings , is in hospital in Perth, Scotland. He enlisted in November, 1914, and was drafted across the English Channel in June of last year. Formerly in the employment of the , Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co.. Ltd., he is an old boy of Holy Trinity School, and is 37 years of age. Two brothers are serving, ! Private John Thomas Wellings, South Lanca- shire Regiment, and Private William Wellings, who emigrated to New Zealand six years ago, and joined a New Zealand contingent. Mrs. Wellings's brother, Private John Savage, has been with the South Lancashire Regiment in IFrance for fifteen months.</p>
        <p>r 1111$111,. 1101:11 • e.""'"•-••:' • ri • . . •••••• •••••• - .• :,,?tfmt,lorer.• • • , • . • . *</p>
        <p>CORPORAL A. JONES. • Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>PRIVATE H. CLAYTON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE H. CLAYTON.</p>
        <p>Son Of Former Liberal Agent</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>Private Harold Clayton, South Lancs. Regi- ment, and son of Mr. John Clayton, 80 Padgate- lane, a former secretary and agent to the Liberal Club for seventeen years, has been wounded in action.</p>
        <p>Private Clayton was taking part in the cap- turing of a German trench on Saturday, September 30th. On the Sunday he was hit in the left hand. At five on Monday Morning a shell burst near a machine gun of which he was in charge, and killed the five lads who were assisting him. A piece of the shell hit his</p>
        <p>Wounded'.</p>
        <p>water bottle and blew it to smithreens. It was the bottle which, undoubtedly, saved his legs. As it was, he was wounded in the right thigh and left knee. Writing from a hospital in Newcastle-on- Tyne, Private Clayton says he is getting on quite well, and can move about with the help of a stick.</p>
        <p>Enlisting under the Derby Scheme on the 7th April of this year, Private Clayton had only been in France since September 14th. He was formerly a clerk in the Warrington Corpora- tion Gas Offices, and is an old boy of Beamont School. He is 21 years of age, and his wife and one child are at present residing with her parents at 153, Marsh House-lane.</p>
