      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>PRIVATE S. MILLET.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE S. MILLET.</p>
        <p>Second Time Wounded Necessitates</p>
        <p>Amputation Of Arm.</p>
        <p>Private Stanley Millet, South Lancashire Regiment, who was wounded in June of last year, sustaining shrapnel wounds in the head, has been wounded again. News to this effect has reached his home, 92, Orford-lane. He is badly wounded, this necessitating the amputa- tion of one of his arms while in hospital at Rouen. He now lies in Netley Hospital. Private Millet, who is 27 years of age, for-</p>
        <p>Farm Work de Luxe. "The ploughman homeward plods his weary</p>
        <p>way." The reader put aside his volume of poems. "Times change," he commented. "I see in Kansas they are taking hired hands to the har- vest fields in taxicabs."</p>
