      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>LIVELY MEETING.</head>
        <p>A LIVELY MEETING.</p>
        <p>Trade Unionists Fall Out And Then Agree.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>DUKES' SUCCESSOR.</head>
        <p>DUKES' SUCCESSOR.</p>
        <p>After fifty minutes' speech-making and cross- talking, about as many Trade Union delegates assembled in the Town Hall on Monday night, selected Mr. J. Hamblett, the Trades Council nominee, to replace Private Charles 'Dukes, who had been suggested without official accept- ance as it Labour representative on The War Pensions Committee.</p>
        <p>The meeting was interesting, provocative and illuminating as regards the mutual animosity felt by non-affiliated unions and the Trades Council. The Mayor (Alderman Peter Pea- cock) first explained that Private Dukes had been ruled out, and they were now met to select a nominee in his stead. They could either send one, two or 'three names for the Town Council to select from. "Now choose your own chairman," said the Mayor, pre- paring to vacate the chair. Mr. H. Harrison (General Labourers) : I pro- pose Mr. Edwin Plinston (chairman of the Trades Council). A seconder was "on time,'' Up jumped Mr. Hodson s (of the Dock Labourers' Union), "I propose the Mayor.", Mr. Joseph Elwell (Iron and Steel Workers): I second.</p>
        <p>The Mayor replied tha" he could not allow his name to be put to the vote and would, there- fore; withdraw. "Before Igo have you any- thing to ask me?" he inquired. "Yes," said one of the delegates. "Why was Dukes ruled out?"</p>
        <p>"I can't tell you anything," rejoined his Worship. "If you have not sufficient intelli- gence to read the papers and take notice of them I cannot tell you. Moreover, I don't think it is fair of you to ask me to say anything fur- ther." So saying, he bowed his adieux, and Mr. Plinston ascended the dais. This gentleman then gave a lucid review of the situation, re- marking that 'a previous meeting of trade unionists had nominated 'Messrs. Baker, 'Hod- son, Dukes, Seabury and himself as members of the committee, and the Town Council had accepted all but Messrs. Dukes and Seabury. These names had been submitted to the Statutory Committee in London, and the Trades Council had no representative there for the simple reason that the notice given was too short. The objection to Mr. Seabury was not sustained and, according to the communi- cation received, Mr. Dukes had been dropped because he was in the Army and not because they had anything against him as /being an un- fit person to serve on the local committee. The Trades Council had further considered the matter and had agreed that Mr. Dukes' name would have to be withdrawn. While having the utmost confidence in him as a person able, ready: and willing to act on that committee, they had decided to let his name go for the time being. He (Mr.. Plinston) thought they could now decide on one name in Mr. Dukes' place, and that ought to put an end once and for all to the recritninations which had taken place, and let them set to work to do their duty on the Pensions Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>NAMES SUGGESTED.</head>
        <p>THE NAMES SUGGESTED.</p>
        <p>In the name of the Trades Council, Mr. F. Berry then nominated Mr. J. Hamblett, who was the railwaymen's delegate on the Council. This having been seconded, Mr. Hodson opposed, remarking that the Trades Council did not represent half of the trade .unionists in Warrington and, therefore, had no right to have two or three representatives on the committee. He proposed Mr. Charles Wozencroft, of the Railwaymen's Union. Mr. George Chamberlain claimed that the nominations from the Trades Council had qualifications not possessed by any other organisation, for those from the latter only represented themselves, whereas those from the Trades Council were on behalf of the majority of trade unionists in the Town.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wozencroft, as secretary of the Railway- men's Union in Warrington, said that the name of Mr. Hamblett, also a railwayman. was brought before the branch meeting on Satur- day night, and they decided that they were not hound to support' his nomination, but were given an absolutely free hand.</p>
        <p>Messrs. C. Bradshaw and H. Hewitt said that their branch of the A.S.E. had requested them to name Mr. Thrvid Plinston. This gentleman did not happen to be present, and the Chairman, although related to him, thought it unwise to accept his nomination, as delegates ought to have an opportunity of see- ing those for whom they were voting. Mr. Hewitt objected, stating that he and Mr. Bradshaw had been sent there to sabmit the name of Mr. David Plinstckn, and it was their duty to do sp. The Chairman replied that that was not the principle . upon which they worked in their branch meetings; only officials present were elected. However, he would put the matter to the vote. The meeting supported him by 33 to four.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. E. Seabury, secretary pro tern. of the Trades Council, pointed out that at a previous meeting of the delegates it was agreed upon that non-affiliated societies with the Trades Council should have a third of the representa- tion. More than that they already had and, therefore, it was a matter entirely for the Trades Council as to who should take Mr. Dukes' place. There was sufficient evidence from the Labour representatives on the Statu- tory Committee that had Mr. Dukes been free from military service they would not have recognised the Town Council's. objection what- soever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>STORM BEGINS-</head>
        <p>THE STORM BEGINS-</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Elwell retorted that it was all right for them to enter into an agreement, but they hadllot then consulted their members. Since then they had talked it oyer with them anl found .that the majority were against delegat- ing to the Trades Council the right to say how many affiliated or non-affiliated members were to go on to the committee. There might be a greater number of societies in the Trades Coun- cil, but he denied that there were more trade unionists. Many of the statements made by the Trades Council were absolutely false. If the trade unions were consulted they would be found to be absolutely against the Trades Council doing their business for them. The workers as a body were working for a Trades Council which should represent trade unionists and not a party political organisation. While Mr. Elwell was speaking a ragged fusilade of interruptions was indulged in. The Chairman resented the disorderly procedure re- marking, "We ought to listen to those opposed to us and not chirp in. I hope you will not do it any more. As Chairman of the Trades Coun- cil I am glad to bear any criticism and, now that Mr. Elwell has started the subject, I shall allow ample opportunity for reply afterwards." Order regained, Mr. Elwell proceeded to say that he and others had consulted their mem- bers since the previous meeting ad he had been instructed to carry out their request. That was his apology for making a departure from the arrangement entered into on that occasion.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain remarked that it was not very flattering to Mr. Elwell to have his pre- vinus action over-ruled. Mr. Thomas Howard caused amusement by saying that he did not know which was going to finish first, the war or the election of the Laboui representatives on the Pensions Com- mittee! Both had been unduly prolonged. Mr. Iroward then commenced to reply to Mr. Elwell, and that gentleman , intervened with the observation that he was not prepared to stop there all night. He was out of bed at four o'clock that morning to go to work, and had got to he up at the same time next day. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>-,-RAGES---</head>
        <p>-,-RAGES---</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I am sorry that Mr. Elwell opened out an important point, laying stress on it, and that he now runs away from his guns. Mr. Elwell, he said, had gone out of his way to send darts, which he hoped would be poisonous, to the effect that the Trades Council was a partisan body. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Elwell: All right. Go on then. I'll stay all night if you like. Mr. Howard went on: He denied the allega- tion that the Trades Council did not represent the working men of Warrington, and added that when those who were not affiliated held a meeting they got about 30 people present. "We, at any rate, did get a hall full, even if we get chucked out," he admitted, amid laughter. He thought the agreement with the Trades Council regarding the representation should be cairied out. There ought not to be</p>
        <p>Continued in next column,</p>
        <p>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>PRIVATE L. HOUGHTON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE L. HOUGHTON.</p>
        <p>Arrives' Home XVith Wound In His</p>
        <p>Private Levi, Houghton, of the South Larcs Regiment, whose parents reside at 36, Pickmere- street, was recently wounded in the neck, and has now arrived _home on a short leave.</p>
        <p>Private Houghton, who is twenty-one years of age, joined the Army at the Outbreak of war, and had been at the Front twenty-one months.</p>
        <p>Natural. Customer: "Barber, why did you drop that boiling towel on my face?" Barber: "Because it was too hot to hold."</p>
