        <p>PRIVATE LEVI HOUGHTON. Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>Employment Of War-broken</head>
        <p>Employment Of War-broken</p>
        <p>Men.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Plinston presided at the meeting of the Trades and Labour Council on Tuesday evening. A proposal from the Blackburn and District Council suggested the formation of an associa- tion amongst discharged soldiers and sailors by which they could protect their rights in the matter of employment. Mr. Seabury (secretary pro tern.) said they had now instituted a 28s. minimum wage for the labourers and they were asked if that sum was to apply to the inefficient men. They were going to be met with the return of hundreds of maimed men, and they should see that the Government should take steps and provide those men who were discharged and unable to get into the factories with the good things of life.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain knew of men coming back incapacitated who were taking the place of men getting 28s. a week, their pension being taken into consideration. It was not tight that pen- sions should be considered, and that a man should be paid 10s. or 12s. a week wages.</p>
        <p>The question was sent on to the new Execu- tive Committee. •</p>
        <p>PEACE TIME SETTLEMENTS.</p>
        <p>A request was received from the Union of Democratic Control, asking the Council to par- ticipate in a forthcoming special conference with regard to the position of the working classes after the war. The Chairman said there could be no harm in attending a conference and listening to the suggestions made.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain understood the U.D.C. to be a body of people out to bring about a reason- able peace at a reasonable time, and it was up to the working man to take an interest in matters affecting his own class when peace was declared. He moved they fall into line in call- ing the conference. Mr. Stringfellow seconded. Mr. Seabury said that whether they agre?d with the war or not, ninety-five per cent of the working class were fighting the battle to-da, and they should have a voice in the settlement. They should see that the working men should derive some benefit at any rate at the conclusion of hostilities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>HOUSE SHORTAGE.</head>
        <p>THE HOUSE SHORTAGE.</p>
        <p>In the matter of the housing problem in Warrington, the Town Clerk, in replying to a letter from the Council, said tilt* nothing could be done in this direction until the Government provided the means and powers to act. On the motion of Mr. Harrison, seconded by Mr. Chamberlain, a resolution was adopted protesting against the continued inactivity of the Town Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WAR PENSIONS COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WAR PENSIONS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Ilamblett reported the result of the appointment of a representative on the War Pensions Committee the previous evening, and paid a special tribute to the fair way in which the Chairman at that meeting (Mr. E. Plinston) had conducted the proceenlngs and allowed everyone to express their views. (Applause:) He congratulated the Council on the result, and said it was his determination to represent the working man on that committee and to see that the men who came back from the war broken and maimed and wounded would have their just rights. Mr. Seabury complained of the lack of prin- ciple of some Members attending the confer- ence. If any section of the Labour movement in Warrington—no matter what section it was —was desirous of obtaining representation on the War Pensions Committee a report should be submitted of what took place at such meetings. -Unless that was done the Labour movement was going to be put back further than it had been. They must tell the rank and file what was going on. Mr. Hamblett's report was received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>NEW EXECUTIVE.</head>
        <p>NEW EXECUTIVE.</p>
        <p>The following officers were elected on the new executive:—President, Mr. E. Plinston; Secre- tary, Mr. Charles Dukes; Secretary (pro tern.), Mr. A. Seabury. Executive Committee: Messrs. Berry. Shim- min. Chamberlain, Stringfellow, Whithy and Boyle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>LIVELY MEETlNG.—Continued.</head>
        <p>A LIVELY MEETlNG.—Continued.</p>
        <p>a split in the Labour movement for their factions were only made use of by those opposed to them. It was all right to say that the Trades Council was a political body. They did not deny it or apologise. That was its function, for they were not going to hesitate to use the political arm any more than they were the industrial. They could not afford to dis- pense with either. Several delegates rose to continue the dis- cussion in apparently a cantankerous vein, and the Chairman requested them to "have done with the abominable stuff you are talking." Societies, he said, which had sent delegates to the Trades Council in the past were responsible for them, and if the Wiredrawers sent one of the strongest Socialists in the town (Mr. John Haley) to represent them that was not the fault of the Trades Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>-AND ENDS.</head>
        <p>-AND ENDS.</p>
        <p>That was sufficient. The storm received its quietus,- the traffic of business proceeded on its. way unassailed by the "U" boats of innuendo and aspersion, and Mr. J. Hamblett reached harbour with 30 votes, Mr. Charles Wozencroft 12, and Mr. T. Rigby ,(Bricksetters' Union) 3. Mr. Hamblett's name was then submitted for unanimous acceptance and agreed to. Mr. Hamblett replied that it would be his aim to serve the interests of all those who carat back maimed from the Front. In the vote of thanks to the Chairman there was a general chorus of approval, Mr. Wozen- croft remarking that it was about time that the working men of Warrington ceased to be sectional anil became united. (Loud applause.) The Chairman was pleased with the words of Mr. Wozencroft. "If there has been an unclasping of the hands in the Labour ranks and a grabbing at one another's throats, please desist," he said in conclusion, and, the dele- gates dispersed, the Mayor coming to Mr. Plinston for the news, which he will convey to the Town Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</head>
        <p>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</p>
        <p>'Alderman Tinnion, Messrs. F. A. Frost, J• J. Whitley, Joseph Elwell and W. BradbarY formed the Military Tribunal sitting at the Town Hall on Wednesday to hear appeals. Four Sons In The Army.</p>
        <p>A local butcher appealed on behalf of his 90?-7 23, who acts as slaughterman and also buys in the market. Four sons are already in the ArnlY —two in the Australians, one in the Canadians, and the other in the Royal Fusiliers, at Pre' sent in Scotland.</p>
        <p>The case was adjourned, the Tribunal admit- ting the importance of applicant's business- They had to consider, however, that 'the young man was 23 and single. The military representa- tive might be able to arrange for the services of various slaughter men—some may have more than they require.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Girl Got Married.</head>
        <p>Girl Got Married.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. L. Davenport (Messrs. Eustance and Co., jewellers, Sze., Sankey-street), applied for exemption for his assistant, a man who had been already granted two months. He explained that ,he had engaged a young woman anti was teach- ing her the business, but she got married ar fortnight ago. "That upset my apple cart,'. said Mr. Davenport, amidst some laughter. The appeal Was refused, Captain Mathains, military representative, promising to let the man stay out as long as possible. Medical Re-examination.</p>
        <p>In several cases it was mentioned that raOn passed for general service or for garrison dutY abroad had been form_erly rejected. Captain Mathams• said that anyone not satis- ,fied with the result of their medical eiamiaa" tion could be re-examined if they so desired. Milk Dealer's Application.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Mary E. Roberts, a widow, represented by Mr. Steel, appealed for exemption for her son. • Applicant is a milk dealer, who distributes sixteen dozen quarts of milk per day amol 250 customers. She has four girls, none 0 whom is able to carry on the business, and this son is the only person capable of doing so. Then bulk of the milk is supplied from a cart or which the son is in charge. The young raft had been at first rejected as medically un' on account of rupture, but he had been recently: passed as fit for active service: The eldest L her four daughters is only 15 years of age. Two months' exemption was granted, no fur- ther appeal except by leave of the Tribunal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>'HIE ALPINE CURE AT HOME.</head>
        <p>'HIE ALPINE CURE AT HOME.</p>
        <p>The Peps Breatheable Remedy For Coughs, Colds, Sore Throat, And Bronchitis.</p>
        <p>It is here again—the season of chilly nights and mornings, and sudden weather changes. Coughs and colds are the order of the daY, and bronchial sufferers dread the coming of winter unless they are prepared with a box of PePs' These unique tablets are happily described 11 bringing all the chest-strengthening virtues ° A Pine Forest into every home. The origin of Peps is interesting. When it was proved that the pine forest air of Switzer:. land was so beneficial for throat and chest, troubles, scientists endeavoured to find a waY extracting the medicinal elements from thet pine trees and embodying them in a conveniel: tabloid form. It was comparatively easy empress the medicine into a tablet which coult be gradually swallowed into the stomach as lt dissolved in the mouth. But that was net sufficient, because the medicine would reach the bronchial tubes and lungs, which at' the parts affected by coughs, colds, bronchitis, &amp;c, t 5 It's the pine air they breathe in that bene- and. cures weak-chested visitors to Switzerlann; and keeps the natives so free from coughs .an":. colds. The problem which the inventors Peps solved was how to give British 13°131'3 in their own homes this valuable throat an chest medicine in a natural, breatheable fosg; You place a Peps tablet in the mouth, removing the silver wrApper, and as it dissolve; the medicine becomes air-like or volatile, ana mingles with your breath. The result revelation. Breathing becomes easy, an° nice warm glow creeps down the breathint tubes right into the lungs, which then Icer without any sign of soreness or Obstruction. „ It's astonishing what one or two Peps tablet' rniwill do—how soon they stop that nasty Ine ing cough, how quickly they soothe that throat irritation, and how easily they cut and eige that thick,- suffocating phlegm which is. _ _</p>
        <p>terror of bronchitis sufferers.- -Peps get there direct. They are the breill•P'; able medicine which goes right down to the ver' root of coughs, cold, sore throat, and br°„ chitis. Apart frpm their • soothing medicinal qualities, Peps are also germietne and an invaluable protection against in°''t infectious germs that are the cause of throlle and chest epidemics. If every member of tilt family takes Peps, coughs and colds will n° spread through the house. the It's a good habit to .always keep Peps in house, and to carry a few in the pocket out or at work.</p>
        <p>What's The Use. Little Lemuel: "Say, paw, this hook sit' Nature never wastes anything." Paw: "I guess that's right, son." Little Lemuel: "Then what's the use of a C having two horns when she can't even Pl9l one?"</p>
        <p>IF ht so m 131 ac</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Itttrrstithr Ttlitar</head>
        <p>Itttrrstithr Ttlitar</p>
        <p>PENSIONS : HFiLP FOR. SAILORS</p>
        <p>AND SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Owing to the lamented breakdown in health of Sir Frederick Milner, the work that he was engaged upon on behalf of sailors and soldiers who were being unfairly treated in the matter of their pensions, has been undertaken by the British Workers' National League. In addition to assisting applicants who are in diffi- culty with their pensions, the League assists and advises in cases of delay in .the payment of separation allowances, or men's final settle- ments, and deals with any inquiry in connec- tion. with service in the war. It will also assist in cases of threatened eviction by landlords of soldiers' wives or deriendents, of which there are too many cases in various parts of the country. I shall be glad, therefore, if your readers with grievances or in difficulties will -communicate with me, when' all bona-fide cases will be inquired into free of charge, by the League. FREDERICK HODSON. 121 a, Sankey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>MEN OF 11IE FUTURE.</head>
        <p>THE MEN OF 11IE FUTURE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—When the time comes for hostilities to Cease and terms of peace are signed, what will then be the state of affairs? We cannot return to the status qua ante, for that would mean, a renewal of industrial strife amongst ourselves, and the flooding of our home markets with cheap foreign products. Capital and Labour will have to organise on parallel Lines; their policy mutual, not antagonistic; trustful in each other, working in combination for the good of the country. Hew is this to be done? The time has come, after what we have seen of the exploitation of our country, for Capital and Labour to, try to work harmoniously to- gether. In the past we have always been at sixes- and sevens; we shall have to give up the Old Ishmaelite policy of the past, of every Man's hand against every man. Master and man will have to coalesce for the mutual good and the Common benefit, showing t united front to .the enemy. In the past it has been "every man for himself and the devil take the hindmost," which ho,s led up to another striking phrase, "Blessed is the man that expecteth nothing, for he shall not be disappointed." These two phrases Sum Up the character of the past. What is going to be done to alter matters? A young officer at the Front, writing home on this subject, says: "They (the soldiers) will never again be content to live as they lived before the war. They hate the war itself, but it has filled them with an immense hope. Every- body is beginning to have .a purpose in life. The wheel cannot be moved back again.- Every- body who has been out here, whether he wanted to or not, has been obliged to think. He has got some definite ideas on certain principles of life, and has made for himself, unconsciously, ,perhaps, a certain code of morals which he will follow. Let not all this be in vain. Let the public mind at home be opened and prepared for the soldiers when they come home. After this life, will they be content to return to the slums again? Oh, no." These things are thought by thousands at the Front, and to them the ultim- ate enemy is not the German, with whom they 4are now fighting, but an ,aimlessness in life we all led before the war. The life of our whole society now seems to them like aimless dreams.</p>
        <p>What was-i1;;;;;;se—Of this aimlessness, and why has the war with all its waste and long- seeming futility, driven it out of the mind of Our soldiers? The soldier of to-day is the same 'Simple ordinary man of pre-war times, with this differeneethat many of:his natural fine quali- ties, which were latent in the humdrum of his</p>
        <p>ordinary life, have come out. In the firing line the Philistine, the arrogant Pharisee, the pedant, and even: the prig, find themselves on one level; the man of wealth and ,the man of poverty are equals there; there is no variation in status, every one counts. The man with his thousands is of no more value than the man who possesses nothing; They are all united in one common fellowship tolace the foe, each one helping his neighbour; and in facing the risk of death together they are like men under the. sway of a great artist, or joined 'together in a common act of worship. The " Times " corre- spondent thus describes them: "There have been thick-set men getting on to middle age, hard and weather-beaten, and tanned by ex- Posure and battle; and there have, been slim youths with downy cheeks and wistful eyes, and soft hair fit for a mother's stroking; and they were fighting men all." "It is a wonder that never palls, but is always new; the spirit which these men of ours possess, from no matter what part of the Empire they may come. One won- ders where the grumblers, the cowards, the mean people. whom one thought one met in ordinary life, have gone. They are not herer Or, if they are, they are uplifted and transfigured." Again he Proceeds "More than one officer has told me that it humbled him to think he was in com- mand of, and expected to set an example to, such men as his. One said he did not know how his men did it. 'For an officer,' he said, 'it's com- paratively easy, because he knows that if he fails everything will break. But how the individual men, who haven't the same responsibility to sustain them, do it, is what I shall never under- stand.' Almost every battalion, every brigade, every division is the best in the Army. I know it, because the officers have told me, so here's to them. But I am not sure that the best testi- mony; after all, was not that of an officer high up in the Department of the Chaplain-General, who has seen much. He said.: It, is God-in- spired. I can find no other explanation for it. The behaviour of the men is directly God- inspired.'" _</p>
        <p>Such are the men we have sent oat, and of such is the Army composed: Men</p>
        <p>With flame _of freedom in their souls,</p>
        <p>And light of knowledge in their eyes</p>
        <p>"Greater love hath no man than this, that he lay down, or risk, his life for his friend." These are our friends. What are we going to do for them on their return? They are an Army now; they will be an Army on their re- turn. At the end of this war militarism will be dead and the returning Army will be one of peace. They will have had a surfeit qf ,strife, and will have no desire for contentiousness. Shall it be peace and goodwill, or a renewal of the sordid squabbles of the past? All our efforts at home must be to settle eontrovergy and to work unitedly for the best interests of not only these small islands, but the whole Empire. To endeavour to build up our society and trade and commerce on a basis of true honesty and fair dealing, not only , between man and man, but also between employer and employe, working for the country in its truest and most patriotic sense. As Lord Northcliffe recently pointed out: "The bravery and camaraderie of the officers and men in the field have broken down all class feeling, and the millions of men abroad are changed communities, of whose thoughts and aims we know but little. Just as Grant's sol- diers, the Grand Army of the Republic, domin- ated the elections in the United States for a quarter of a century, so will the men in the trenches and the ambulances come home and demand by their votes the reward of a very changed England—an England they will fashion and share, an England that is likely to be as much a surprise to the present owners of Capital and leaders of Labour, as it may be to the owners of the land.'</p>
        <p>PHIL SQUOD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
