      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Law</head>
        <p>Law</p>
        <p>FREE CONCERTS.</p>
        <p>Co-operative Society Starts Musical Camppign.</p>
        <p>The Education Committee of the Warrington Equitable Industrial Co-operative Society having arranged a series of free concerts, to take place during the season, the first was given on Saturday evening by Mr. J. Graham's popular party. The hall was taxed to its utmost limit, Mr. J. Upson (president of the society) presiding over the gathering, and with him being Mr. W. E. Dudley, a C.W.S. director, who, during an interval, spoke to the audience on the income tax question. The programme opened with a chorus entitled, "Ladies and gentlemen," sung with fine gusto by the party, which, during the even- ing, also gave "My Kentucky home," "When the angelus is ringing," "Who bashed Bill Kaiser ?"—one of the, latest, this—" All Scotch," and "Come underneath my umbrella." Mr. Graham had the audience in uproars with his humorous selections, both vocal and otherwise, which included "Little Willie wonders why," "Is it likely?" and "A gay old dog." He was repeatedly encored, ,and, for one of these, he gave an imitation of the night wind. It was a realistic effort. Mr. K. Leigh delighted v.ith his songs, "Mountain lovers " and "Until as also did Miss L. Vickers with her "Moorish maid." Miss M. Edge rendered in fine style, " In an old-fashioned town," and also took part with Miss Vickers in a duet, "Life's dream is o'er." All the items were rendered in splendid style, and the artistes were repeatedly encored. Mr. A.*Trow was the accompanist. Mr. J. Upson said that, on behalf of the Board of Management, he had a most pleasing an- nouncement to make, viz., that on and after Monday, the 9th inst., best butter would be sold at Is. 11d. alb., but no dividend would be paid. The commodity having reached such a high price, it was thought that the interests of the members would be best served by selling at net price.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WHEN LIFR WAS SIMPLER LIFE WAS LONGER.</head>
        <p>WHEN LIFR WAS SIMPLER LIFE WAS LONGER.</p>
        <p>When the organs begin to weaken, whether early or late in life, the hardworking kidney, usually tire out first, and should have first con_ sideration.</p>
        <p>Failing eyesight, stiff, ,achy joints, rheumatic pains, backache and distressing urination are often clue only to weak kidneys. At the first sign of weakness give the kidneys prompt attention. Drink water freely to flush the kidneys, and use Doan's Backache Kidney Pills to strengthen them. Go - back to the Simple Life, to the sensible habits of your boy- hood days. Eat less meat, avoid over-work, excesses and worry, and take more outdoor exercise, rest and sleep.</p>
        <p>Everybody dreads kidney trouble, but this sensible treatment will keep the kidneys in con- dition, lengthen life, make it easier, aild per- haps avert altogether the more serious kidney diseases. • - -</p>
        <p>_ Local people have recommended Doan's Backache Kidney Pills to their friends and neighbours for over 15 years. The good they do and the continual use of home testimonials inspires an ever increasing confidence in THIS SPECIAL KIDNEY MEDICINE.</p>
        <p>• All dealers, or 219 a bog! from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells St., Oxford St., London, W.</p>
        <p>Failure's Privilege.</p>
        <p>Justice Hughes, at a Baptist supper in Wash- ington, once rebuked a back-biter neatly. The man had been talking about the tainted money of this millionaire and that millionaire. All millionaires, according to him, were thieves and hypocrites. Hughes , nodded and said quietly:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
