      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>: u WOMANS RL</head>
        <p>: u WOMANS RL</p>
        <p>fore the tide turned in favour of the useful receptacles would have, had at the most one placed inconspicuously inside the garment. Sometimes curiously shaped pockets form the basques of blouses and corsages, others the draperies, whilst still others are cut in one with the dresses. One of the very, newest pockets and most sensible when not overdone, has been nicknamed the "flower pot," because it stands out from the sides of the coat or dress to which it is attached, as would the half of a pot if placed in the same position.</p>
        <p>Nightwear and Raids. Perhaps no department of ftminine attire has undergone greater change during the last ten years than that devoted to underwear. The materials now used would cause the greatest surprise to early Victorians, as would also the shapes of the nightdresses and sleeping suits of to-day. The frequent Zeppelin raids have brought a new night garment into prominenv modelled on the suit designed in America for women camping out. It buttons up to the throat, is shaped like a princess robe, has big pockets at the sides, and near the hem—for the feet—also a hood, and is made of fine flannel, thick crepe de chine, or some very warm material. A much more frivolous and decora- tive nightdress is of delaine in a floral design, and in shape very like a coat frock. It is trimmed with ribbons and lace, and is furnished with pockets. The Empire nightdress still re- mains first favourite for ordinary" wear, and is made of nun's veiling, vyella, and thin woollen materials. A touch of black is considered smart, and is, achieved by means of a satin ribbon threaded at the waist through embroidery.</p>
        <p>Breathing and Health. Breathing is one of the involuntary motions of the body, yet it appears that even here it is possible to injure one's health through ignor- ance of the correct mode of taking in the air. Most people take too short a breath, with the result that the lower lobes of the lungs are not filled as they ought to 'he. Swimming, rowing, tennis, riding, all sports in fact, develop the muscles, and to" a certain extent the lungs; vocal exercises not only build up the lungs, but by introducing fresh air into the lobes, they also expand the air vesicles, and cause new ones to form. Cheese Straws.</p>
        <p>Mix together two tablespoonfuls of flour, a pinch of salt, a very little cayenne pepper, and three ounces of grated Permesan c,hecCse. Add the beaten yolk of an egg, and then enough iced water to make a very stiff paste. Roll the paste out on a board into a sheet an eighth of an inch thick. Cut the paste into strips an eighth of an inch wide and five inches long, and bake them about ten minutes in a very hot oven. They should be a very light brown; If you have any scraps of paste left, roll them into a sheet, and cut-into small rings. Bake them, and slip little bundles c f the straws through them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Save Coals and Light. Housewives can, through the autumn and winter, help to win the war by exercising the greatest possible economy in the use of coal, gas and electric light. Coal is not for luxury this winter. It is vital for the requirements</p>
        <p>of the Navy, the munition works, and the factories engaged in the production of war material. Then we have to supply the needs of our Allies and to keep up our exports to neutrals. Coal is used in the household directly to produce heat, indirectly to give light, in the form of gas. It has been computed that if each consumer riduced the usual quantity only one-tenth, some three or four million tons of coal would be saved for the Navy, and for the needs of the war. One ton saved represents a miner's work for one day; it also means money which could be lent‘to the nation. It is quite worth while to economise in coal.</p>
        <p>Home Dried Fruits and Vegetables. Not only have we paid vast sums to foreign countries for eggs and butter, but also for dried fruits and vegetables. Yet by taking trouble much of this money could have been kept at home. Germany started the drying of vegetables as a paying industry, and her ex- ports of these provisions were in demand in almost every country in the world. Beginning with the drying of fruit and potatoes, she ex- perimented successfully with vegetables of every kind, and then proceeded to include roots of various descriptions. Thus work was found for the stoves and drying chambers, and the employes, all the year round. The process of drying is very simple, and can be carried out in any household if a little care be taken. An inexpensive machine has lately been invented for home use. The flavours are in every case preserved, and the dried goods, when properly cooked, are hardly to be distinguished from fresh-grown vegetables.</p>
        <p>The Rage For Pockets. Pockets are having an extraordinarY vogue, and tailors and dressmakers vie with each other in placing them in the most incongruous 'posi- tions. Their shapes are as various as their curious hiding places, although the majority are by no means invisible—on the contrary they form the most prominent features of tailor- mades, coats and day and evening gowns. The pocket that depends from the belt, and in some cases from the shoulder, is perhaps the most popular shape, and admits of greater variety than others, hence possibly its success. Four large and a couple of small pockets are con- sidered by no means an excessive number for a fashionable wrap, or travelling coat, that be-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>••:••:••:••:••.1.4:•+•:•+++.:••:•••••••••• • • • + 4. Children' s Corner..) • •</head>
        <p>••:••:••:••:••.1.4:•+•:•+++.:••:•••••••••• • • • + 4. Children' s Corner..) • •</p>
        <p>Conducted by UNCLE JACK. 4'.• •4.:.+++++++++++++++++++++</p>
        <p>WHAT YOU THINK OF ZEPPELINS.</p>
        <p>My Dear Children, You are all well up on the subject of Zeppelins. If I had to print all your letters this week I am afraid the Editor would have no room for his news of the war or of the town. What I have done has been this: I have given one letter in full and then points from letters not dealt with by other writers. So you must not be disappointed if only a few words appear of your particular letter. All of them are good, but they naturally cover the same ground, and it is no use repeating, is it? If you read all the quotations I have made from all the letters you will know what all your nieces and nephews are thinking abbot .the Zepps. I am pleased to think that you for the most part care as little for Zepps as our soldiers care for German soldiers. We shall beat them both in the long run. ' The prize this week goes to 445 Niece Jda Whitby, 5, Green-street, who attends 'Evelyn-street School, and is thirteen years of age, so that the nieces have again won.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ADVICE FOR THE BOYS.</head>
        <p>ADVICE FOR THE BOYS.</p>
        <p>Last week's successful competitor, Niece Lily McLachlan, 8, Ash Grove, Latchford (Bolton Council School), gives the boys some advice. She says— " Dear -Uncle Jack,—l was delighted to re- ceive my second prize on Saturday for my essay on the 'Battle of the Somme.' It in- terested me to read your remarks about the boys. Let the boys try hard to beat the girls, and we shall have to do better to beat them. I took my prizes and 3,6ur paper to school on Monday, and the Bead Mistress, Miss Place, and the teachers were very pleased."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>HOW TO JOIN THE CORNER.</head>
        <p>HOW TO JOIN THE CORNER.</p>
        <p>As you will see, new members are still roll- ing in; but there appears to be some doubt as to what to do to enter. If all wishing to join Uncle Jack's big family of nieces and nephews will write a letter like one I got from Lymm this week, they will do exactly the right thing. The letter is a model, so I reproduce it in full "20, Newfields, Lymm. "Dear Uncle Jack,—Will you please make me a member of your Children's Corner? I should dearly like to become one of your nieces, as I think this particular Corner of our favourite paper is very educational, and ought to be taken advantage of.—l remain, yours truly,</p>
        <p>" GLADYS ELLEN MOSTON, "Age 12; Lymm School."</p>
        <p>DRAWING COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>We will have another drawing competition this week. Draw, but do not colour, any sub- ject you prefer yourself, an send to me not later than Wednesday. Your loving, UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>THE PRIZE ESSAY. By Niece Ida Whitby (No. 445). 5, Green- street</p>
        <p>"A Zeppelin is a German airship invented by Count Zeppelin. There have been at least a dozen types of dirigibles produced by German airmen,- and three of these are in use by them to-day. These are named the Parsevall the " Schutte-Lanz, and the 'Zeppelin,' and each differs greatly from the other two. The Parseval,' which is the earliest surviving type, is a non-rigid airship, is a large bag filled with gas. Its two propellor blades are made of cloth and hang limp when not turning. It has only one car, which, is made of steel tubes,, and will hold from four to seven men. The Schutte-Lanz ' is semirigid, and its frame- work is mainly wood, and it has two or more open cars, and will carry a crew of from twelve to sixteen men. The 'Zeppelin,' which is an all-rigid type, has an aluminium frame- work covered over with an outer skin of rubberised cotton stretched tightly over it. In- side are the balloonets, which are filled with hydrogen gas. Some of the latest Zeppelins are nearly 800 'feet long,, with several covered- in cars, and carry a crew ,of from '• thirty to thirty-five men. They can fly at eighty miles an hour, and are able to journey three thousand miles out and back in one trip. Three machine guns are placed, one at each end of the bow and stern, and one on the top of the envelope, and they are able to carry five tons of bombs. Nowadays Zeppelins are known to be clever, but dangerous things. There have been a great many Zeppelins brought down in flames whilst over London, but this time we have brought one down undamaged, and are able to take a copy from it if we wish."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</head>
        <p>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</p>
        <p>"There is a secret in them which no other coun- try can find out. When the crew •get high up where the air is thin, they have a room in whia they go." .</p>
        <p>Niece Amy. Reading,. 26, St. Barnabas'-place (Evelyn-street School).—" Some people said, 'Birds can fly; why can't we, of' make some- thinc, to fly in?' They began to make aeroplanes, which could only get the height of 50 yards. Count Zeppelin tried and succeeded in getting them to go about twice as high." Niece Gladys Pennington, 168, Wellfield-street (Evelyn-street School).—" The objective of the Zeppelins is to destroy our armament works and military and naval bases. But, sad to say, many innocent lives are lost through the raiders." Niece Amy Berry, 83, Algernon-street (Fair- field Girls' School).—" I hope they do not arrive at Warrington or Liverpool to wreck our house or any other."</p>
        <p>Niece Gladys Ellen Moston, 20, Newfields, Lymm (Lymm School).—" Zeppelins are a‘ hor- rible weapon of war. They seem only to achieve one aim—murdering defenceless women and children. Before long Count Zeppelin, the in- ventor of these infernal machines, will have to invent something new to take their place, for if we go on as we are doing he will have none left to carry out his lord and master's desire."</p>
        <p>Nicce Lily McLachlan/8, Ash Grove Latek foi d -(Bolton Council School).—' They invade our coast when we are at slumber, and when our gallant aviators ascend•to engage them in combat the cowards are only too eager to turn back to their Fatherland. We should all thank Lieutenant Robinson and Lieutenant Brandon, who, assisted by some of our anti-aircraft gunners, have brought three Zepps down within ten days. That is a fine record to our Royal Flying Corps and gunners. It iS a shame that so many innocent lives should be lost through these cruel people who command the ZepPs, and certainly no punishment is too great for the Germ-huns who have broken the laws."</p>
        <p>Niece Ivy Odell, 1, Myrtle Grove, Latchford (Bolton Council School).—" The Allies have in- vented an airship similar and as good as the Zeppelin, so that we are now equal to the Germans."</p>
        <p>Niece Emily Marshall, 183, Winwick-road.— •` A long time ago it wasn't Zeppelins 'they were frightened of, but sea robbers or pirates. The Zeppelins are as long as a football field. The people generally give the Zeppelins the name of Baby Killers,' because the Kaiser has told his aviators to kill all the children they can."</p>
        <p>Nephew Harry Heywood, 8, Matthews-street (Parochial School).—" One Zeppelin is about two hundred yards long, so you can imagine how much money it would take to make a fleet of twenty Zepps." • Nephew John Berry, 83, Algernon-street (Parochial School), hopes the Zeppelins do not reach Warrington or Liverpool. - Nephew Fred Rathbone, 112, Algernon-street (Parochial School).—" The Zeppelins are the length of a street. In Warrington there is a searchlight which will shine on the Zeppelins when they come."</p>
        <p>Niece Maud Alice Lunny, 108 a, Bridge-street (Arpley-street School).—" Zeppelins are very destructive implements of war. It is an awful sensation when they come over, as they make everyone frightened. It has been said that Count Zeppelin never made them for destruc- tive purposes. They were originally ,built for carrying cargo, but are now used for every evil purpose." Niece Eva M. Morrall, 47, Price-street.— "Perhaps the Germans think to terrify us and make us give in, but we are more determined than ever after every raid. I would like to see a Zeppelin, but from a very safe distance." Nephew Frank Overend, 74, Lythgoes-lane (Hamilton-street School)." The number of Zeppelins damaged, broken to pieces, 'r(r brought down since the war began is thirty- two. The milk the districts which have been bombarded has not been up to the standard because the huge monstrous air enemy has frightened the cows." Nephew s Jas. Darbyshire, 35, Tillev-street (Hamilton-strek School).—" The Zeppelin can carry several tons of high explosives, such as bombs and shells,"</p>
        <p>Nephew Jos. Spencer, 89, Laira-street (Hamil- ton-street School).—" Zeppelins are Germany's best air machines, and are England's greatest enemies. They fly at a great height and are, not easily brought down. A Zeppelin is more vulnerable when hit- from above than from below."</p>
        <p>Nephew John H. Singleton, 24, Gandy-street.— "The framework of the Zeppelin is made oe aluminium, a very light but strong metal. In the gas bag there are a number of balloons filled with gas, and several of them have to he bursted before it will fall to earth when fly- ing."</p>
        <p>Nephew, Denis Farrar, 46, Willis-street (Parochial. School).—" When the huge thing is fetched ,down and all the burnable part is burnt the steel part looks like a huge Meccano broken up. The speed of a Zeppelin is about a hundred miles an hour, which is a lot faster than an aeroplane. At tee present time a Zeppelin is the largest filing to go in air, but I do not think it will be after the war." Niece Fanny Osborne, 31. Leicester-street (Evelyn-street School).—" No doubt the</p>
        <p>111'14.111: 1,1!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>a • e*• `." 141124 .4 4 . • „, 44. • . • • le1( . te. '4A So** 74." .7 .3.</head>
        <p>a a • e*• `." 141124 .4 4 . • „, 44. • . • • le1( . te. '4A So** 74." .7 .3.</p>
        <p>0• . • 1 ' ' ..,..., ;.... *-.., ~.. . . ......., Meat . ..dr , ,dt. ;I,‘ --ri,/- f..-oPlb : ..' 5: „ • r4# 4 , ..., ...:1, • . , v• ' .t 11 •, 4 ) "...• I— s 414: yilr ',. ~,, ' i'll ' '''‘44''' . ?''....tr'' •11 4t, 1 '' :7,... ' ' -, .' , , -44- Itw • ?", ..... - ' \"-- .., *:, '...6 •</p>
        <p>Zeppelin is a very wonderful invention. The German invention was what they called 'Fright- fulness,' but they have not frightened us yet. They have made us more "anxious to pay them back. The men that go up in them must have very strong nerves, and very daring, for the sensation must be very queer when they are out of sight of land. The best protection we have is always to keep our lights shaded, so that the town cannot be seen from the sky, and to prevent them having any object to aim at."</p>
        <p>Nephew Edgar Lehrle, 194, Battersby-lane (Beamont).—" The Germans say that they will not leave a brick standing in London, and they are building fifty Zeppelins for the purpose, but if they keep on comirig like they do 1 do not think that there will be much left of them."</p>
        <p>Niece Nancy Lehrle, 194, Battersby-lane (Beamont).—" People in Britain are very angry with the Government for not raiding German towns and doing to them what they have done to us. The first man to bring a Zeppelin down in Britain was Lieutenant Robinson, for which act he got the V.C." Niece Florence Tickle, B', Orford-avenue.-- "We are advised to keep indoors when there is an air raid, but people run about anywhere, and that is very dangerous." Niece Jane Gough, 5, Phillip-street.—" A- lthough Zeppelins have done so much damage they have not come hp to the Kaiser's exoecta- fiat', for he boasted that England would be -atterly wiped out by his Zeppelins. I think it is left to Count Zeppelin to invent something superior to Zeppelins to carry out the Kaiser's expectations."</p>
        <p>Niece Dorothy Wright, 68, Winv.ick-street (Wycliffe).—" All the streets in Warrington have to be darkened because of these Zeppelins, and though people think they will come over here, I do not think they will ever get the chance."</p>
        <p>Nephew Harold Freaney, 107. Cartwright- street (Sacred Heart School).—" Count Zeppelin calls them his night hawks. He thinks that they are blowing up London, but wakes to tha fact that London is blowing them out of existence."</p>
        <p>Niece Doris Whittaker, 62, Baxter-street (Evelyn-street School).—" The Zeppelins have been to the place *here I was born. They are lighter than air, and with being made of aluminium, they get about the air more easily." Niece Edna Fellows, 44, Bewsey-street (Evelyn-street School).—" Our aeroplanes, with the help of anti-aircraft guns, have succeeded in their attempts to bring to the earth some of the Zeppelins. I think that everybody will be very glad when this war comes to a conclusion and the Zeppelin raids are ended." Nephew Colin Campbell, 56, Lancaster-street (Evelyn-street School).—" Sometimes the men in the Zeppelin throw out a great deal of smoke to hide behind."</p>
        <p>Niece Mary V. ,Flynn, 26, Pierpoint-street (St. Alban's).--" When war was declared Ger- many thought of a very cruel way of using their Zeppelins by raiding unfortified -British towns and villages!! Niece Amy Campbell, 56, Lancaster-street (Evelyn-street School).—" To hide from view on a clear day, these machines throw out a thick vapour 'which forma a cloud. It must have been a keen disappointment to the inventor of the Zeppelin, as he vowed to destroy London." Nephew William Watkinson, Heaton-street (Hamilton-street School).—" Round about Ber- lin are many Zeppelin factories, some of which have been blown to atoms."</p>
        <p>Niece Elsie Johnson, 323, Lovely-lane (Wycliffe).—" Their cruelty is only laughed at by the hard-hearted Kaiser, who plans to kill the defenceless women and children of the Motherland. To do this it is costing a great deal of money, but he does not mind as long as he squeezes it out of his people, who are very nearly at the point of starvation. His heart is not softened by the pitiful cried of help from the nation."</p>
        <p>Nephew John Willie Peers (No. 979), 73, Lythgoes-lane (Wycliffe).</p>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>1017 Peter Appleton, 23, Heaton-street; age 9; Silver-street School.</p>
        <p>1018 James Stockton, 21, Ashton-street; age 12; Hamilton-street School.</p>
        <p>1019 Thomas Atherton, 50, Scott-street; Hamilton-street School.</p>
        <p>1020 James Forster, 62, Chorley-street; Hamilton-street School.</p>
        <p>1021 Stanley Rowlinson, 15, Hamilton-street; Hamilton-street School.</p>
        <p>1022 Gladys Ellen Masten, 20, Newfields, Lymm; age 12; Lymm School.</p>
        <p>1023 Harold Dutton, 4, Owen-street; age 11: Hamilton-street School. 1024-John Forsyth, 10, Heaton-street; age 12; Silver-street School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <p>IVEY husband has taken cocoa for years, and I used to laugh at him a little because he set such store by it. He's a big strong man, and I did not think he needed cocoa. It never struck me that his good health might have something to do with the cocoa.- But when I went into the factory I began myself to feel the strain. "Take a cup," he said to me one day when I was making his cocoa, "it will do you a power of good.", I did—and I felt a warm glow all over me. It put new life and strength</p>
        <p>into me. I wasn't nearly so tired when. I got to bed that night; and next day I started with another cup of cocoa; and the work seemed far lighter than before. I don't laugh at my husband any more—we have our cocoa regularly together.</p>
        <p>" I used to laugh at him a little."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>FARM.</head>
        <p>THE FARM.</p>
        <p>MAN POWER.</p>
        <p>Census Of Farm Workers To Be Taken.</p>
        <p>_Further efforts are .being made to adjust the difficulties involved in the distribution ,of man- power. _ _ . _</p>
        <p>The President of the Board of Agriculture and Fisheries, in a notice to farmers, announces that before the New Year a census will be taken of men of military age still engaged in farm- ing. Meantime, until January 1, 1917, and in certain,cases until April 1, 1917, no more men employed in farming will be called up except in return for unfit men released from service.</p>
        <p>Farmers aro warned to take advantage of the period of respite by making arrangements for replacing men who may be called to the Colours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>FARMERS HAVING A GOOD TIME.</head>
        <p>FARMERS HAVING A GOOD TIME.</p>
        <p>At the annual tenants' dinner for the Walton estate, yesterday week, Sir Gilbert Greenall, replying to the toast of his health, said he was sure it would be adiaitted .that farmers were having more prosperous times, and those pre- sent, in common with other farmers, were get- ting a bit of their own back. Some friend had remarked that this was, perhaps, an oppor- tune time for raising rents, but, although he admitted the contemplation was a pleasant one, he had no thought of doing anything of the kind.—Mr. Blackshaw, replying for the tenantry (proposed by Sir Gilbert), said, that although the past year lind been the most remunerative he had experienced, things were not so glowing as was often stated. People rarely considered farmers' losses, and were amazed at an increased price being demanded for milk, in spite of the fact that the price of a good milking cow was as much as £45.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>FARMER-SOLDIERS OF VERDUN.</head>
        <p>FARMER-SOLDIERS OF VERDUN.</p>
        <p>The heroic farmer-soldiers of Verdun, since the complete failure of he German assaults against the fortress, have been able to enjoy a well-won respite from the tempest of battle. On the relaxation of their duties practically the whole of the men possessing agricultural ex- perience volunteered to assist in the gathering of the crops in the vicimity, a considerable por- tion of which, by the way, was raised from seed corn given by English farmers through the Agricultural Relief of Allies' Fund, and had been sown as close to the firing line as military requirements permitted. An arrangement was made between the mili- tary authorities and the mayors of the com- munes in the neighbourhood of Verdun by whit,,h the corn crops belonging to the local peasant farmers might be cut and threshed by the farmer-soldiers. Thanks to the spontaneous offer (if• •these poilus—many of whom were themselves peasant-farmers when war broke out—crops have been reaped and threshed which otherwise woUld have been lost. The Agricultural Relief of Allies' Fund seeks to give, as oppoitunity occurs, Mrictiv agricultural help to all peasant farmers in allied countries who have suffered ruin through the enemy in vasion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>WHAT WOMEN CAN DO.</head>
        <p>WHAT WOMEN CAN DO.</p>
        <p>In a recent issue of the " Spectator " a corre- spondent (" Mabel Smith ") gave an account of her nine: weeks" experience on a farm. " What am Ito give her to do?'" So spoke my employer before my arrival at the farm. I. had -chosen agriculture for my war work for the following reasons: (1) Because there is a shortage of labour in this all-important in- dustry, and, having been asked to help in organising women's work on the land, I wished to find out from practical experience how much of such work women csin and ought to do; (2) because I think it is a mistake to allow chil- dren to leave school in order to work on farm&amp; before the school leaving age, and so I hoped to take the place of one such child; (3) because there seemed to be an idea that educated women could not undertake manual labour, and</p>
      </div>
