      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, OCTOBER 14, 1916.</p>
        <p>NO COMPROrAISE.</p>
        <p>If Germany squeals, or interested parties squeal for her, in the hope that some form of intervention may be brought about to save her from the fate she has deliberately courted, Germany must now ,know that such hope has been. very much misplaced. Recently Mr LLOYD GEORGE 'told America, and through America all whom it might concern,' that ~we should tolerate no peace-Mongering miSchief makers. We had, he said, taken our punish- ment; now Germany must take hers----or throw up the sponge. This week, on the re- opening of Parliament, Mr. Asu-tnr has orwe again given voice to the unalterable deter- mination of Great Britain and her Allies. "This long and sombre procession of cruelty and suffering," he said, amid applause that had a sinister meaning for Germany, "lighted up as it is by deathless examples of heroism and chivalry, cannot be allowed to end in some patched-up, precarious, dishonouring compromise masquerading under the name of peace." Germany knows she is defeated, however long she may contrive to draw out the agony for us and herself; she knows now that there is to be no escape from the effects of defeat by any "patched up" contrivance, however skilfully manipulated. With the ex- ception of the disturbing'factor ol Roumania, all goes exceedingly well for us on all the fronts. Mr. ASQUITH said so without empha- sising the position on any particular front. The best feature of the whole campaign, in fact, is that each, nation is going to work ,as though it believed its awn particular sphere was the decisive front. We believe that on the Somme bangs the issue of the day; the</p>
        <p>-777 i • A</p>
      </div>
