      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>Seeing that Harmer has recently joined the Army .it was a piquant coincidence that the jersey he wore on Saturday at the Warrington v. Swinton match should be No. 9.. Soldiers will understand the idea.</p>
        <p>The scores on the telegraph board on the Wilderspool ground are conspicuous by their absence nowadays. Is it that no one can be found to do a highly necessary job of this kind in the days of amateur—that is uhpaid--service?</p>
        <p>Was it shortage of petrol or sheer love of adventure which prompted one of our most eminent public officials to abandon his own car on Monday night and, under the cloak of dark- ness, take a high seat on the motor fire engine coursing through the town for exercise? A little of both, perhaps.</p>
        <p>I have received a field postcard this week, •signed "Joe Durham, sergeant," with the line "I am well" left undeleted. In boxing phraseology he adds: Another round and Mr. Bosche is out of the world.</p>
        <p>Another message comes from my friend, Mr. A. E. Spann, who is on ambulance duty at Salonika, where he is staff sergeant in charge of the surgical side of a hospital with 1,500 beds.</p>
        <p>He pays a glowing tribute to the medical men and nurses; and as to "the boys" who come down from the Front, they are decidedly hopeful that the Hun and his satellites will soon be de- feated.</p>
        <p>By his special request I convey his "kind re- gards to his many friends," who will be glad to hear he is cheerful and well in the midst of his good, if hard, work.</p>
        <p>A Penketh farmer, who, instead of putting the clock back a fortnight ago, put it forward and found that his milk next warning was short by several gallons, and many customers had to go unserved. - Cows should never be hurried.</p>
        <p>Passing a Nonconformist Church the other day I observed that the lady taxi driver, who was waiting for the wedding party to emerge, was reading "Home Chat." I don't know whether the newly-married couple said to her. "After you!"</p>
        <p>About the same date the wedding of a brick- setter took place in another Warrington chapel, where there is only a harmonium. Thinking that.. music should be rendered anti finding that the instrument was incapable of producing Mendelssohn's Wedding March as a voluntary —the organist played, what?—" Men oi Harlech " which, as every • Warringtonian knows, begins, "I'm the man for mixing mortar, • Four and twenty bob a quarter." Work even pursued the bricksetter to his wedding.</p>
        <p>Much discussion has taken place as to the meaning of the word "tank," Vie new weapon with. which our Army has surprised the in- ventors Of poison-gas. No one seems to ,be able to account for the meaning of the name of the mysterious arrival.</p>
        <p>If schoolboys nowadays are anything like the schoolboys of my young days they will be able to inform investigators as to the real meaning of the verb "To ,tank." The British Army is about ." to tank" the Germans way they will never forget, and it is fitting that our newest instrument to help the fight along should ill- spire the adoption of this unfamiliar but ex- pressive noun.'</p>
        <p>Two million signatures were attached ;to the memorial calling for total prohibition during the war, a very large figure indeed if the memorial were not " pushed " •elsewhere more vigorously than it was pushed in Warrington.</p>
        <p>The campaign authorities have called upon the Government to give effect to the memoljal, or give a referendum on the widest franchise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>IVEDDING OF MISS 11. CORDON.</head>
        <p>IVEDDING OF MISS 11. CORDON.</p>
        <p>At the Parish Church, Preston, there , took place by special licence a quiet wedding between Private John SelleY, Army Pay Corps, son of Mr. John Selley, contractor, London, and Miss Helen Gordon, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Gor- don, 46, Arpley-street. The bride, who was given away by her father, was attended by her sister,. Miss Florence Gordon, while the best Man was Sergt. Whitehouse, alsil of the Army Pay Corps. The quiet nature of the ceremony was due to a recent bereavement in the family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>STEALING A FUR NECKTIE.</head>
        <p>STEALING A FUR NECKTIE.</p>
        <p>At Monday's Borough Police Court an elderly woman named Florence Wilkinson, no settled abode, was sentenced to a month's imprison- ment for stealing a fur necktie valued at 16s. lid. from Mr. Robinson's stall in the Market. Prisoner said she was drunk when she took the tie, but the police denied this statement.</p>
      </div>
