      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 14, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 14, 1916.</p>
        <p>LADY JELLICOE</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Receives Navy Day Cheque For £2,150.</head>
        <p>Receives Navy Day Cheque For £2,150.</p>
        <p>TOWN HALL SCENES.</p>
        <p>Yesterday afternoon the cheque for £2,150 representing the splendid effort made by War- rington Navy Day was presented to Lady Jellicoe, wife of the Commander-in-Chief of the Grand Fleet. The ceremony took place in front of the Town Hall in the presence of a gather- ing of some five or six hundred citizens. The cheque was handed by his Worship the Mayor (Alderman Peacock) to Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., and the latter presented it to Lady Jellicoe.</p>
        <p>It was unfortunate that rain began to fall midway through the ceremony, and as a conse- quence the nice musical programme to be pro- vided by three hundred children representative of the various schools in the town, .had to be curtailed. The children stood within an en- closed space in front of the Town Hall steps, and those in the rear file carried the Union Jack, and the flags of our principal Dominions and those of our Allies. Their programme in- cluded "God Save the King," ":The Marseil- laise," the Russian National Anthem, "Mother land," a patriotic song, and "Rule Britannia." The children, who sang very sweetly, were selected from the senior and Secondary Schools. Mr. J. W. Parker, Heathside, was the conduc- tor, and the children had also been trained by Mr. I. Bowen, ArPley-street School; Mr. F. H. Longshaw, Hamilton-street f:;chool; and Mr. H. Bowen, Arpley-street School. Miss Ethel Bunch, assistant mistress Parochial Boys' School, was accompanist. Lady Jellicoe had originally intended to reach Warrington at 1.54, but a wire was re- ceived saying that she would not be able to come until an hour later. This somewhat up- set arrangements, and there was a mild flutter in "the Town Hall. Shortly before three His Worship the Mayor left in his motor for the railway station, and in a few minutes returned 'with his distinguished visitor and Mr. Harold Smith, M.P. Lady Jellicoe, who wore mourning for her late father, Sir Charles Cayser, smiled charmingly at the warm greeting she received on arrival. A brief delay, and she appeared be- tween the Mayor and the Member on the Town Hall steps, surrounded by a large number of well-known ladies and gentlemen. The Mayor was at his best, and in a splendid speech thanked Lady Jellicoe for coming to receive Warrington's tribute 'to the Navy, and ex- pressed admiration for her husband the heroic Commander-in-Chief of the Grand Fleet.</p>
        <p>_ The 'cheque for £2,150 was in a long pamr envelope, and this he handed to Mr. Harold Smith. The Member for the Borough, in the course of an appreciative and patriotic speech, put in. a little dramatic touch by opening the envelope and drawing out the momentous piece of paper.</p>
        <p>Lady Jeniece.</p>
        <p>In acknowledgment her Ladyship said :—" I am very glad to come to Warrington this after- noon to receive this handsome cheque, and I am also surprised to hear that it is the first inland town whose people have collected so much money for the Navy. They are very much to be con- gratulated. I also value the cheque very much, as you show how you value the Navy and realise how much they are doing. My hus- band asks me to express to you how sorry he is that he could not be here this afternoon, but there are other labonrs that claim his atten- ti:m. (Cheers.) He asked me to express to the citizens in Warrington and all who took part in collecting this money and who subscribed, how much he and all that are serving with him appreciate all you have done, and express their heartfelt thanks. The Gtand IFleet Fund was inaugurated by her husband to help all those who are invalided out of the Navy either by ,sickness or by being injured. You cannot realise that every time the Fleet goes to sea it faces the same risks from a dozen different quarters, and in a dozen different shapes. I ask you never to forget those in peril on the sea, and I thank you most warmly for all you have done for the Fleet in Warrington." (Cheers.) The Mayor mentioned that he had received apologies for absence from Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall, Sir Arthur and Lady Crosfield. Mr. J. J. Bleckly, Mr. W. P. Rylands, Canon Willis and others.</p>
        <p>The singing of "Rule Britannia" by the choir and the National Anthem, brought a very interesting event to a close.</p>
        <p>Her Ladyship was entertained in the Town Hall by, the Mayor, and accompanied by Mr. Harold Smith and His Worship, left in time to catch the 4A7 train for Edinburgh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>MASON IC INSTALLATIONS.</head>
        <p>MASON IC INSTALLATIONS.</p>
        <p>Bro. Samuel Pickton. Master Of the " Lodge of Rectitude."</p>
        <p>There was a large attendanoe of members and visitors, including grand and provinbial grand officers, at the Masonic Hall on Monday evening, when Bro. Samuel Pickton was in- stalled as Worshipful Master of the "Lodge of Rectitude," No. 3597. The visitors in- cluded W.Bros. H. V. Pigot, A.G.C., P.P.G.C.; F. Howard, P.P.G.D.C.; R. H. Pearson, P.P.G.O. ; J. C. Waldron, P.P.G.D.C.; D. Tin- nion, P.P.S.G.D.; R. Lunt, P.P.A.D.C.; T. I. Fletcher, W.M., 148; J. H. Day, F. Pearson, W. Hainsworth, J. W. Murray, W.M., 2963; J. H. Heywood, J. S. Hill, W.M., 2651; W. B. Cook, A. G. Guest. 2963; W. H. Tickell, and T. Cooper.</p>
        <p>The new Master was installed by the retiring Worshipful 'Master, Bro. Herbert Woods, who, later in the evening, was presented by the lodge with a Past-Master's jewel in recognition of his services during the .past Year. The W.M., assisted by W.l3ros. the. Rev. T. T. Lee-Jones, P.Pr.G.C. H. F: Greenough and J. Critchley,' invested ilia officers as under 1.P.M., W.Bro. Herbert Woods; S.W.; W.Brw H.E. Traiis; J.W., Bro. J. W. Bouchier; chap- lain, Bro. the Rev. E. Downham; treasurer, W.Bro. H. Woods;. secretary, Bro. T. F. Mathews; S.D., Bro. J. L. Davenport; J.D., Bro. J. B. Starkey; D.C., W.Bro. H. F. Greenough, P.P.G.G.B. ; almoner, W.Bro. W. Hainsworth ; organist, Bro. E. Davies; 1.G., Bro. H. L. Tay. lor ; stewards, Bros. J. Poyser, J. H. Teare, Ridgway, and G. J. Wild; tyler; Bro. G. S. Rice.</p>
        <p>The treasurer's statement of accounts, which was of a satisfactory nature, was adopted, W.Bro. Greenough was elected as charity repre- sentative and the W.M. as group representative. The usual banquet was again abandoned owing to the war. BRO. HENRY TAYLOR MASTER OF " GILBERT GREENALL " LODGE.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the installation took place at the Lion Hotel'Assembly Room of Bro. Henry Taylor as Worshipful Master of the "Gilbert Greenall " Lodge. of Freemasons, No. 1250. The installing .Master was W.Bro. E. Rose, who was assisted by W.Bros. H. Maddock, 1.P.M., T. Hill and F. Rose.</p>
        <p>The newly - 'installed Master afterwards proceeded to appoint and , invest the following officials: S.W.. Bro. H. Collin; Bro. T. Reddish; Chaplain, W.Bro. Rev. B. Per- cival; Treasurer, W.Bro. T. Hill; Secretary, W.Bro: E. Rose; Assistant Secretary, W.Bro. F. Rose; S.D., Bro. E. Allen; J.D., 'Bro. A. C. Binks ; Organist, Bro. T. A. Davies; 1.G., Bro. Jas. Gray; Stewards, Bros. E. Lunt, T. F. W. Dixon, W. S. Barker and A. J. Allen; Tyler, Bro. P. J. Dell. In addition to a inn meeting of lodge mem- bers, there were present_to witness the ceremony the following distinguished visitors: W.Bros. R. Verney Clayton, P.A.G.D.C. Eng.' Prov. G.S. East Lancashire; W. H. J..Jenkins.P.P.G.R., C. Holland, P.P.D.G.D.C., E. apse, P.P.D.G.D.C., R. Lunt, P.P.D.G.D.C., J. Lee, P.P.J.G.W. (Che- shire), T. Hill, P.P.A.O.D.C.' J. Waldron; P.P.A.G.D.C.. Rev. B. Percival, P.P.P.G. Chap- lain, T. I. Fletcher, W.M., 148; J. Moore Mur- ray, W.M., 2963; S. Pickton, W.M. 3597; T. S. Steel, 1.P.M., 3287; J. H. Heywood. P.M.. W. Hodgkinson, P.M.. W. H. Stansfield. P.M.. R. T. Smith. P.M., C. G. McDowell, P.M., and Her- bert Woods, I.P.M.</p>
        <p>The official business of the lodge having been thus brought to a happy and harmonious con- clusion, the brethren partook of light re- freshment. In these after proceedings the usual loyal and Masonic toasts were honoured. Among the toasts Was that of the Grand Lodge, responded to by W.Bro. R. Verney Clayton.</p>
        <p>HIPPODROME FULL VARIETY MATINEE, MONDAY, at 2.4s.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>ALIENS ACT "CATCH."</head>
        <p>ALIENS ACT "CATCH."</p>
        <p>"OUR DAY,"</p>
        <p>Mysterious Case of Elderly Man Next Thursday's Great Effort and Young Woman. of the Empire. Next Thursday will be "Our Day,' when an effort by the whole Empire will be made to raise funds to support the gigantic work that is being done in this war-blighted world by the minis- tering angels that are at work under the aus- pices of the Red Cross Society and the Order of St. John. At Warrington there will be 'a Cafe Chantant in the Parr Hall, opened by the Countess of Stamford, with a choice array of attractions—including a performance of the German Hymn of Hate by the, :and and the placing on sale of souvenirs in tile shape of por- tions of the Zeppelins that have been wrecked in England. Full particulars of this out-of-the- way and attractive entertainment appear in. an advertisement. Collections are being made in the works, banks, Szc.,., and we are informed that both Masters and men are contributing generously. Other efforts are being made; and it may inspire still further exertion when it is known that this Red Cross work in the war is being done at an expenditure of 51d. in the of the money received, or something/ less than 2f per cent. This world-wide effort is patronised by the King, the Queen, and Queen Alexandra. "Our Day" last year brought £1,400,000 to the Red Cross. It is the " day " of everyone who has the spirit and intuition to feel for the men who are cheerfully suffering, fighting, and falling for those who remain behind; of everyone, man or woman, who, from whatever cause, is debarred from a direct share in the contest of the nations. For those reasons, and no less for a host of others, the organisers of " Our Day" hope that this year will surpass the record, not merely of last year, but of .all other .days. It is for Britons, and the Britons beyond the seas, to en- sure that they are not disappointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>WHIST DRIVE AND DANCE FOR THE FUND.</head>
        <p>WHIST DRIVE AND DANCE FOR THE FUND.</p>
        <p>. On Thursday evening, in the John Crosfleld Memorial Hall, a most successful whist drive and dance were held in aid of the funds of the Warrington Branch of the British Red Cross Society. This social event, which was excep- tionally well patronised, was most successfully organised by Mrs. Osborne, an extremely ener- getic member of the committee, and cannot fail to add considerably to the funds of the War- rington branch. The drive and dance were specially arranged in connection with the great effort of " Our Day" on Thursday next. ' The proceedings were graced by the presence of Miss .Sylvia Broadbent (vice-president of the Daresbury Division, 8.R.C.5.), who distributed the whist prizes and made an appealing speech, and Miss Margaret Broadbent, commandant in Haddon Court Hospital. Whist occupied the attention of a hundred an 1 forty devotees of the game until 9.30, when re- freshments were served. A further batch of visitors then arrived and dancing began to the mellifluous strains of Mr. Starkey's band. Mr. T. H. Edwards (Grappenhall) was a capable M.C., while the M.C.'s for whist were Councillor Halliwell and Mr. Jowett.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Osborne was ably assisted by Mrs. G. S. Pendlebury, Miss Short, Mrs. Collin, Mrs. Per- cival, Mrs. Edwards and Mrs. Shaw, while Mr. Percival and Mr. Collin were invaluable in ar- ranging the whist tables, and the general duties of active service.</p>
        <p>Six valuable prikes were ofiered for competi- tion, theo donors being Messrs. Eustanee and Co., jewellers, Sankey-street; Councillor J. T. Locker, Mr. Garland, Bridge-street; Mr. Felix Maginn, Boots, Cash Chemists; and Mr. Davidson, Bridge-street. The winners announced were Ladies: 1, Mrs. Bibby, 140; 2, Mrs. Hankin- son, 139; 3, Miss Dee, 138. Gentlemen: 1, Alderman D. Tinnion, '147; 2, Mr. James Shaw, 142; 3, Mr. J. Thornley, 138. Miss Sylvia Broadbent, having presented the prizes, outlined the work of the Red Cross Society. "The very best we can do," con- cluded Miss Broadbent, "is not enought to re- pay our soldiers and sailors for what they are doing for us." (Applause.) Alderman Tinnion moved a vote of thanks to Miss Sylvia Broadbent and Miss Margaret Broadbent for coming to them that evening. They belonged to a family that took an interest in and encouraged everything for the good of their fellow men, and not only for the good of Warrington. but their whole country and Em- pire. (Applause.) There was vigorous applause, which Miss Sylvia Broadbent acknowledged. On her suggestion cordial acknowledgment was made of Mrs. Osborne's untiring and most suc- cessful efforts for the cause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>"GREAT CONFESSIONS OF FAITH."</head>
        <p>"GREAT CONFESSIONS OF FAITH."</p>
        <p>Cardinal Newman's Apologia And Religious Opinions.</p>
        <p>Preaching on the above subject at Cairo-street Chapel on Sunday morning, the Rev. ,Irvine Lister, in speaking of Newman's intense opposi- tion to liberalism in religion and his belief that there was no half-way house between Romanism 'and Atheism, said that this was, a matter well worth considering to-day, for many still held Newman's belief. If one can admit certain premises, for example, the Divine authority of the Church, infallibility; the Divine institution of the sacraments, certain doctrines of the Incarnation, original sin, fallen humanity and atonement, then logically one must end in the Church of Rome, admitting all her claims. But, if one (hies not admit these premises, but holds ,that there is no external authority in matters of religion, that reason is a sufficient guide in all spiritual affairs, that intellect is supreme, though not despising spiritual experience, and that there is progress and an upward tendency in mankind, then the logical end of such views is liberalism in re- ligious thought. In the 19th century one type of thought was represented by Newman and the other by Dr. Martineau. Newman's was a great and almost successful attempt to make one ten- dency predominant, but the work of Martineau and others with him has been too well done for it ever to succeed. But, because of the work of these men, the path is plainer, and it is easiel• for us all to find out what is right and ta abide in it.</p>
        <p>To-morrow morning the Rey. Tryire will speak on "The Autobiography of Mark Rutherford."</p>
        <p>Presentation to Belgian Soldier.</p>
        <p>Joseph Van de Vyvere, a young Belgian, who when war brgke out had been working for two months in the annealing department at the works of Messrs. Rylands Bros., andot once joined the Belgian Colours, has been over on a short furlough this week, and on Thursday was presented by .Rylands's wire drawers with a fountain pen as a mark of their respect. - Mr. W. Haigh, in handing over the gift, spoke of the admiration the men had for Vyvere, who had been doing his duty in the trenches for over two years. He took part in the fighting at Liege, Namur and before Antwerp, and for nearly two i,earS he has been helping to keep the Germans from getting beyond Dixmude. Two brothers have taken part in the war—one has been killed and the other has become a sergeant. Vyvere, who was known as "Joseph Clements" at Rylands's works, is 28 years ,of age, single, and a printer by trade. He knows several languages, and came to England •to add English to his accomplishments..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Death Of Mrs. Shaw, Of Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Death Of Mrs. Shaw, Of Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>We regret to record the decease of Mrs. Emily Shaw, wife of Mr. James Shaw, of 7, Alfred- street, Stockton Heath, who passed away on Wednesday. Mrs. Shaw had been in indifferent health, and the end came junexpectedly. The funeral took place yesterday at St. Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath, the service being im- pressively read by Rev. H. V. Pigot, rector of Grappenhall. Among the members of the famly and ingurners present were: Mr. James Shaw (husband). Mr. John Shaw and Mr. Birch Shaw (sons). Miss Beattie Shaw, Mrs. Saunders and Mrs. Birch Brown (daughters), Mr. B. G. C. Saunders (son-in-law), Mr. Bertie Brown, Mr. Horace Wilcocks, Mr. John Mainwaring and Dr. Joseph. Many handsome floral triliutes were received and expressions of sympathy. - -Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons carried out the funeral arrangements.</p>
        <p>HIPPODROME FULL VARIETY MATINEE, MONDAY, at 2.4s.—Advt.</p>
        <p>WI"</p>
        <p>' , • • /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>FINE OF £2O.</head>
        <p>FINE OF £2O.</p>
        <p>A case surrounded by curious circumstances was heard at the Borough Police Court on Wednesday, when a young female, whose name and address were not made public, and Basil Wilberforce Stothert, of Thrapston, Northants, a middle-aged gentleman of the educated classes, were charged with offences under the Aliens Restriction Act, 1914. The female defendant was charged with having registered in a false name and address, while staying at the ,Lion Hotel on the 25th. September, and also with registering in a false name when staying at the Patten Arms on August 24th. The male defend- ant was charged with -aiding and abetting on each occasion. Chief Constable Nicholls informed the magis- trates that he had handed to the Press the par- ticulars of the charge. He desired that the Court should hold the name of the young woman from being published. There were certain reasons for doing so, and he was sure they would acquiesce in the application he made. The Chairman (Mr. J. W. Hayward): Is it in the interests of justice that you wish us to keep this name private? _ _</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: It is in the interests of justice and more for the interests of the girl. Mr. H. Woods, who represented accused, said that the male defendant was quite willing to take the responsibility of the whole thing. Stayed at Two Hotels.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable explained that when the parties stayed .at the Patten Arms it was not necessary to register their addresses, the names alone being sufficient. It appeared, he said, that the. male defendant belonged to Thrapston in Northants. He went to the Lion Hotel and engaged rooms for himself and '..wife and the young girl attended on the 25th September and represented herself as his wife. The registration forms were placed in front of them, and the young woman apparently had no knowledge of tilling in the form. She asked .the male defend- ant how to fill it, and he suggested the names and address. She was a young woman of about 20 years of age, and had been engaged, at Thrapston! It seemed that there was a certain amount of familiarity between the parties, and she was removed from Northamptonshire to Lancashire. Some time ago, in August, they came to Warrington,- and went to the Patten Arms Hotel and stayed there approximately a week, and the same procedure as to registration took place there, only the address ha 4 not to be taken. The male defendant signed his name and put his nationality as British. The female defendant was with him at the time, and was observed by the bookkeeper to fill in' her name as Stothert. 'On the form at the Lion Hotel the address was filled in as Thrapston, Northants, and as far as the girl was concerned that was not correct.. The case in the eyes of the law was an exceptionally serious one, for the reason that the girl was 20 years of age, and that the male defendant had apparently been following her. He came there and without his guidance or without his help it would have been impossible for her to have committed the offence. • The male defendant was/a married man with a family, and held a position in the place he came from which certainly ought to have made him the last pos- sible individual to have acted in the way he had done. The defendants were both liable to penal- ties of ,2100 Or imprisonment for six months. Not Judging Morals.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woods said he wanted the Magistrates to be very clear that they were not in any way judging morality. They were ,not there to answer any charge of immorality, and the de- fendants had committed no act for which they• were charged, against the inoral laws or the criminal law of the country. They were charged with an act, the effect of which had only been brought about by statutory provision, which had been passed since the commencement of the war. They had not committed any aet which Feuld be in contravention of any Act of Parliament 11; which wak ,• . existence previous to the war. They were th British born subjects, and he submitted at the Act was not passed with tlp intention of catching, if he might use the ex- pression, cases of that deseription. As regards the girl defendant, he did not think he need say anything. The man was quite prepared and desirous of taking upon his own shoulders the responsibility of everything that was done by the girl. What they had done was .done in ignorance of the law, and without the knowledge of the consequences of the Act. To show they had no intention of doing anything against the security of the realm—the 'object of this Aliens Restriction Act—the male defendant had for thirteen years served in the British Army. He left the Army shortly before the war, and Since the commencement .of the war had voluntarily assisted every day in recruiting work. He had recruited no less than 1,682 men for His Majesty's Forces, and he had done this in a rural district, where it would have been impos- sible to have got such a large 'number without his assistance and influence.. He thought the cll.. would be met by a nominal fine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Consideration for the Girl</head>
        <p>Consideration for the Girl</p>
        <p>Th., Chief Constable Said the female defend- aht was between 20 and 'M. She had no father, and her mother lived a very great distance from here. She had really no guiding hand other than the guiding hand of the male defendant, and he (the Chief Constable) was quite prepared to withdraw the charge so far as she was con- cerned. Ile believed she was only getting a wage with a war bonus of a guinea a week, and she would possibly lose this if a conviction were recorded. They would not care for her to lose her situation on that charge. The magistrates having agreed to withdraw the charge against the girl, retired to consider .the case of the male defendant.</p>
        <p>On returning, the Chairman said the Bench were of oninion that the offence was a very serious one, aggravated by the fact that the defendant was a man of education.</p>
        <p>The- imposed a 'fine of ,210 in each case, or one month's imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>BOWLS AND BEER.</head>
        <p>BOWLS AND BEER.</p>
        <p>Fined 20 for Serving in Unlawful Hours.</p>
        <p>At the Newton Petty Sessions on Thursday, Emma C. Miller,. the licensee of the Oak Tree Aotel, Newton, was summoned for selling in- toxicating liquors during prohibited hours on the 28th September. Her husband, John Miller, was charged with supplying the same, and seven colliers from Ashton-in-Makerfield were charged :with consuming the same. All the defendants pleaded guilty. Mr. T. S. Steel, of Warrington, appeared to prosecute for the police, and Mr. J. W. Knowles defended the licensee and her husband.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel explained that on the afternoon of the date in question a bowling .match was taking place at the Oak Tree Hotel, which P.C.'s Smith and Lang visited. After being on the 'bowling green for some time, P.C. Smith went into the ;totel, and at the bar window .he saw two men standing there drinking what appeared to be beer. Whilst he was there each man called fer a glass of beer, which ,was .served by the licensee. -Later in the afternoon, , about 5.50; P.C. Lang was sitting near the bar, when he saw Mr. Miller come out of the room and heard him order "Two pints of pop and ale, one beer and two porters." These were supplied by the licensee and placed on a trey, which the husband took into ,the room. P.C. Smith came intolthe house at the moment, and both police officers went into the room and took charge of the, drink. They found that the seven defendants from Ashton had been supplied, with pop and ale and pop and porter. The defendants were all told that they would be reported, to which ,the licensee replied "All right." • ' Mr: Knowles, in mitigation on behalf of the licensee and .her husband, pointed out that they had held the licende since 1892 without a com- plqint, and the this ease was that they had received notice, to leave the premises. He submitted that if a penalty were imposed upon either the lieensee or her husband, the other defendant should simply be ordered tetpay the costs. - -</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the Bench regarded this as a very serious case, and fined Mrs. Miller £lO, her husband £lO, and directed that they should pay ,R 3 3s. towards the advocate's fee. The other seven defendants were each fined 40:4 . and ordered to pay an advocate's fee of £2 2s.</p>
        <p>IJIPPODROME FULL VARIETY MATINEE, MONDAY, at 2.43.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>ZUSti WAWAMM. _ . . - - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>•CAL, INTELLI: ENCE.</head>
        <p>•CAL, INTELLI: ENCE.</p>
        <p>Lights For Coming Week</p>
        <p>Light up Vehicles. ▪ 5.53 • 5.51 ▪ 5.49 ▪ 5.47 5.45 5.43 5.41 5.39</p>
        <p>Saturday Sunday Monday Tliesday Wednesday Thursday Friday Saturday</p>
        <p>Obscure Lights. 6.53 6.51 6.49 6.47 6.45 6.43 6.41 6.39</p>
        <p>‘Varrington Market Prices.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday7s Warrington Market the prile of wheat was Bs. 41d. to Bs. 6d. per 701 b., and 4s. 41d. to 4s. 6d. per 451 b. was realised for oats. Flour realised 475. 6d. to 525. 6d. per 2401 b., thirds 2s. sd. to 2s. Bd. per 201 b., while bran was is. Bd. to is 10d. per 201 b.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Infectious Disease.</head>
        <p>Infectious Disease.</p>
        <p>There are 18 local patients in the Fever Hospi- tal and 20 plithisis patients in the Sankey Hos- pital. There are ten cases of scarlet fever and four each of enteric fever and diphtheria. Noti- fications last week were: Measles 5, sca4atina 3, enteric 2, tuberculosis 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Births and Deaths.</head>
        <p>Births and Deaths.</p>
        <p>During the week ended October 7 there were 29 births and 22 deaths in Warrington: In the corresponding week last year there were 32 births and 22 deaths.</p>
        <p>The causes of death were: Phthisis 4, pneu- monia 3, diarrhea, enteritis, cancer, bronchitis, 2 eh; all other causes 7.</p>
        <p>Smokes for 2,300 Men.</p>
        <p>There are now 2,300 wounded soldiers at the Lord Derby Hospital, Winwick, and the officials are much exercised as to how the craving for a smoke by so large a body of leisurely men is to be satisfied when there are no funds available for providing the luxury. The only way out of the difficulty can be found by a generous public either sending tobacco and cigarettes or the money with which to buy them to the</p>
        <p>" Administrator, " Lord Derby Flospital, " Winwick."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Insurance Finance.</head>
        <p>Insurance Finance.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Finance *Sub-committee, which were confirmed at Thursday's meeting ot the Insurance Committee (the Mayor presiding), showed the following estimate of administra- tive income and expenditure for 1917 :—Esti- mated income: Credit from the Insurance Com- missioners for general administration, £505; grant from Commissioners towards the increased cost of pricing prescriptions through the bureau. £4O; transfer from Sanatorium Benefit Fund, £9O; estimated credit balance from 1916, £140; total, £775. Estimated expenditure: Remunera- tion of staff, £360; stationery, printing and postages, £135; office accommodation, £110; pricing prescriptions, £75; inoidental expenses, £25; total, £705; estimated balance at end of 1917, 270.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. There was a good attendance at the meeting on Sunday, presided over by Rev. T. T. James. The speaker was Rev. A. J. Chessum, who spoke upon "Dignity." True dignity, he said, was not in self-opinionation or speaking the mind impulsively without due care for the feelings of others, but in lowliness of spirit, with all humility, seeking to help by our words and actions, bearing and forbearing one towards another. A hearty welcome was given to Private A. Jones, and a letter was referred to by the Chairman from Sergeant J. Oxley, which was most cheering. The brethren stood and joined in silent prayer for the lads who were away. The Secretary, Mr. B. Farrar, referred to the death of Lieutenant Armstrong, who had given his life for his King and country and home, and said they • all sympathised with the mother and family in their heavy bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Commercials' Cot.</head>
        <p>Commercials' Cot.</p>
        <p>A very enjoyable whist drive, organised by the members of the Warrington and District Branch of the United Kingdom Commercial Travellers' Association, was held in the John Crosfield Memorial Hall on Friday evening last week. The Chairman (Mr. H. Walsh, who acted as M.C.), explained before play began that the proceeds of the drive would be devoted to the Warrington Cot Fund at the Pinner Schools. The Branch, he said, had promised to raise the sum of 100 guineas for this purpose, which was rather a large undertaking in war time, but he thought the worst was now over, and he hoped the fund would be completed this year. He referred to the good work being done by this institution, and mentioned that the children of any commercial traveller who falls in the war will be admitted without election, and be educated entirely free of charge. Thirty games were played, with the following result :—Ladies: 1, Miss N. Blackwell, 226; 2, Mrs. J. Leigh; 3, Mrs. Webster. Gentlemen: 1, Mr. W. L. Walker; 2, Mrs. Osborne; 3, Miss D. Leigh. Intermediate prizes were won by Mrs. Maddock and Mr. W. G. Hayes. At the close of play Mr. O'Brien thanked all present for their support, and said if it was necessary to hold another whist drive for the same purpose he hoped they would all come again. The prizes, which had been given by friends, were then presented to the winners by Mrs. Walsh. The refreshments were in the hands of a ladies' committee, who ably carried out their duties. The surplus was sent to the Soldiers' Home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>" 'Wood's College" Student's Successes.</head>
        <p>A " 'Wood's College" Student's Successes.</p>
        <p>We record a commendable list of commercial successes in recognised public examinations gained by Miss Kate Murtagh, during the twelve •months she has been in attendance in the Day Classes conducted by Mr. F. Wilson Wood at Wood's College, 15, Bridge-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>"What Next?"</head>
        <p>"What Next?"</p>
        <p>At the Friends' Meeting House, Buttermarket- street, to-morrow evening, Mr. T. Herbert Cross- land, of Manchester, will lecture on " Whdt Next?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>In Aid Of Thelwall Heys.</head>
        <p>In Aid Of Thelwall Heys.</p>
        <p>A whist drive and dance will be held in the Parr Hall on Thursday, November 2nd, in aid of the Thelwall Heys Military Convalescent Home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Rev. Harry Hands.</head>
        <p>The Rev. Harry Hands.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Hands, MA., LL.B., son of Mr. C. H. Hands, of Arpley-street School, was ordained priest on Sunday, October Ist, by the Lord Bishop of Southwark in his cathedral.</p>
        <p>Insurance Committee's New Representative. At Thursday's meeting of the Warrington In- surance Committee Mr. John Rymer Young was appointed a representative of the deposit CQII" tributors to fill the vacancy caused by the resig- nation of Mr. W. J. Rowland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>Special services will be conducted at the Boy- sey-road Wesleyan Church to-morrow (Sunday), when the preacher, morning and evening, will be the Rev. Tom Dring. In the afternoon Mr. T. S. Steel will preside at an organ recital, to be given by Private J. Worsley Harrop, Stockport.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Thanks To Warrington.</head>
        <p>Thanks To Warrington.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Broadbent, of The Hollies, Latchford, has received a letter from Mr. George F. She, secretary of the Royal National Lifeboat Insti- tution, thanking her for the effort made by Warrington on Lifeboat Flag Day recently. "You may well be proud of Warrington's effort on Lifeboat Flag Day," Mr. Shee wrote.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Music in The Park.</head>
        <p>Music in The Park.</p>
        <p>The R.A.0.8. Band will play in the Park to-morrow at 3.15, as follows :—Hymn, selected; quick march "Call to arms" (Greenwood); selection, "Mozart's A2th Mass" (Riiimer); overture, "The Golden Hive"' (Brepsant); selection, from Meyerbeer's opera (Hawke); selection, "Les Cloches de Corneville " (God- frey); "God Save the King." Robert Smith, conductor.</p>
        <p>Harvest Thanksgiving Services At Christ Church, Padgate.</p>
        <p>Harvest thanksgiving services Will be held at Christ Church Padgqe, to-morrow. The preacher in the morning will be the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson, and in th.. evening the Ber- n. W. Campbell Baugh, vicar of St. Bride's, Liverpool. A children's service will be held in the afternoon. Milling's and Early Closing.</p>
        <p>While the Government are hesitating-respect- ing the suggestion'that shops 'should close early during the winter months, Messrs. Henri Milling and Co. have decided to close on Mon- day, Tuesday and Wednesday at 6 p.m.; Thurs- day at 1 p.m.; Friday at 8 p.m.; and iaturdaY at 9 p.m. We hope this idea will spread, and that the public will assist the movement bY shopping early.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Harvest Festival At St. Paul's.</head>
        <p>Harvest Festival At St. Paul's.</p>
        <p>Harvest thanksgiving services were coin- menced on Thursday evening at St. Paul's Church, when the preacher . was the Rev. A• Wood, vicar of Appleton-le-Moors, Yorkshire. There was a good attendance and the chuvh was tastefully decorated. The choir rendered the anthem, "Let the earth rejoice." The services will be continued to-morrow (Sunday), when the preacher in the morning will be the Rev. E. Gunner, and in the evening the Rev. E. Downham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Late Dr. Adams.</head>
        <p>The Late Dr. Adams.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Warrington Insurance Committee on Thursday, the Mayor (Alderman Peacock), who presided, said that since their last meeting they had lost one of their members, Dr. Adams. The Clerk had already sent a letter of condolence to Dr. Adams' daughter, and he was sure the meeting would endorse his action. Dr. Adams had lost his wife a short time previously, and they were all shocked that a second bereave- ment should take place in the family. The members of the committee rose in their places in respectful sympathy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>God and the War.</head>
        <p>God and the War.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. T. James has arranged to give another series of sermons with a special bearing upon the war, in which he will deal with manY of the religious issues involved by the present abnormal conditions. The sermons will be de- livered at Wycliffe Church at the Sunday even- ing, service as under: October 22, "Is Go dead?"; October 29, " The\Angels at Mons "" ; November 5, "Where are the Fallen?"; Noveln: ber 12, "hall we know our Dead again?::, November 19, "The problem of suffering , November 26, "The Soul of a Private."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Alrycliffe Harvest Festival.</head>
        <p>Alrycliffe Harvest Festival.</p>
        <p>At Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sun- day, harvest festival services were held. Tlie church was tastefully dressed by a number of ladies. The preacher for the day was the Rev' T. T. James, who, in the morning took as hie subject "Standing Fast," and in the evening "The Mihistry of Common Things." Th(3 morning anthem was "Hearken unto this.: (Button), the solo parts being taken by Miss 19. Hewitt and Mr. J. Poole. in the evening the choir rendered Barnby's anthem of praise, "Sing to the Lord of Harvest." Mr. W. 1. Payton presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Warrington Chess Club.</head>
        <p>Warrington Chess Club.</p>
        <p>The opening meeting of the above club Was held at the Prince's Cafe, Sankey-street, a° Thursday. That the club should suffer in mei°. bership by the war ,is inevitable, but the meet- ing was unanimous in its resolve to continue to meet as in years past. The officers elected were:—President, Mr. P. F. Blake; President, Mr. A. Hunt; Secretary, Mr. R. P' Owen, 78, Ellesmere-road, Stockton Heath* References were made to those members 0° active service, and congratulations offered t° Sergeant R. E. Burtt on his discharge from lios" pital. The customary invitation was issued t° any who desired better acquaintance with th° historic game, to attend on Thursdays betwee° 7 and 10.30 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Women's Bible Class.</head>
        <p>Women's Bible Class.</p>
        <p>The Women's Bible Class of the Bank Qoa,l Working Men's Mission commemorated their eleventh anniversary on Wednesday by meass of a tea and concert, between 60 and 70 bell present. Mrs. Dale presided at the concert so gave a most haplay and encouraging addres6' She also gave a recitation. Numerous iters° were given by Mrs. Whitby and Mrs. Smith,' and a Lancashire sketch by Mrs. Gleave. S°T: were rendered by Mrs. Storey and Mrs. J. BireDi Mrs. F. Gray acted as Iccompanist, The rePor of the year's work was given by Mrs. Moore, who stated that the financial part was veil good, but the members had decreased durisg the summer on account of the war, menibe,rs having to give up their homes to live with reli tives in other towns. Nine new members ,11,111, joined the class during the year, wialc - brought the number of members up to 158.</p>
        <p>Independent Methodist Preacher Dead" By the death of Mr. R. B. Woods, of Latch- ford, the Independent Methodists lose a zealoua and valued minister. Mr. Woods had been fa depressed health for some little time, but Or' filmed his evangelistic work with great courage until the end, which came rather suddenly? On Tuesday evening. He was born in Warringtl nearly 70 years ago, the son of Peter and Riteee Woods, whose home was in Winwick-street• He followed the business of his father, fl 3 smith, but about 25 years ago devoted self - self to evangelistic work among the Indepeneen Methodist Churches. In this sphere he has beer! much appreciated, and has won the esteem 4): his colleagues in a remarkable way. Ile lia resided in Manchester and Southport; but f?r. the past twelve years has lived in the neje"</p>
        <p>bourbood of Warrington. The funeral takes place to-day. A service will be held in Stockton Heath Independent Metho- dist Church A 2 o'clock, and the interraellt will be at Hill Cliff() at 3. A memorial service arranged for 6-morrow evening at GrapPenhal Independent Methodist Church at 6 o'clock;</p>
        <p>HIPPODROME FULL VARIETY MAT' MONDAY, at 2.4l—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>MISS MIIRTAGH</head>
        <p>MISS MIIRTAGH</p>
        <p>is seventeen years of age, and is now a skilful shorthand writer and typist. Within a few weeks from the commencement of her training, she registered the following successes :- Ist Class (ciistinction)—Book-keeping. Ist Class (distinction)—Commercial Corre- spondence. Ist Class—Handwriting. The al:Wye certificates were issued by the National Union of Teachers (Elem. Stage). Miss Murtagh's further successes are as follows:</p>
        <p>• Primary Theory Shorthand Certificate. Intermediate Theory Shorthand Certificate. Advanced Theory Shorthand Certificate'. 80 words per minute Shorthand Speed Cer- tificate.</p>
        <p>90 words per minute Shorthand Speed Cer- tificate.</p>
        <p>100 words per minute Shorthand Speed Cer- tificate.</p>
        <p>120 words per minute Shorthand Speed Cer- tificate.</p>
        <p>Senior Typewriting Certificate (N.S.A.) _ _ _ _ . . _. . _ . •</p>
        <p>Mr. W. :1-1. Woodcock kindly acted in the capacity of Secretary Superintendent at the examination at which Miss Ifurtagh succeeded in qualifying at 120 words per minute.</p>
        <p>ITIPPODROME FULL VARIETY MATINEE, MONDAY, at 2.4s.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 14, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 14, 1916.</p>
        <p>DIED FOR THEIR eOUNTRY.</p>
        <p>Additional Local Casualties in the Various War Areas.</p>
        <p>SERGT. S. CHURM.</p>
        <p>Fell Leading His Men in a Glorious Charge.</p>
        <p>A fine tribute to Sergt. Sam. Churm, of the Canadian Forces, 'son of Mr. and Mrs. S. Charm, 2, Collin-street, Warrington, who has been killed in action, is paid by Private Stokes, a comrade, who conveying the sad news, said: "It is with the greatest regret that I have to tell you that your son Sam was killed last Tues- day morning. On that day you lost one of the best boys that any parents could have. I lost the best chum I ever had or want to have, and our regiment lost one of the finest sergeants. He fell leading his men in one of the finest charges the Canadians have ever made. It was carried through to a finish and every object gained. . . . . _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The bbys who came out of the fight all right tell me that he suffered no pain." Sergt. Churm, who was twenty-two years of age, emigrated to Canada in March, 1912, but immediately the war broke out he came back to England and joined the Canadian forces, and was only in England three weeks before being sent out. He was educated at St. Barnabas' School, and before emigrating was employed as a clerk in the office of Messrs. Movks, Hall and Co.'s works. He was actively connected with the St. Barnabas' Church, being a member of the Bible class, choir, and social club, and when he emigrated he was presented with a travelling rug and brush and comb. He was a very keen</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>KILLED.</head>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>SEC.-LT. C. M. ARMSTRONG.</p>
        <p>Recently Promoted Officer</p>
        <p>Killed In Action.</p>
        <p>Three weeks ago we were congratulating Mr. Charles McDonald Armstrong, stepson of Mr. Gordon, stationmaster at Bank Quay, and Mrs. Gordon, 46, Arpley-street, on havinc, been pro- moted to a second-lieutenancy in 'the King's Own Yorkshire Light Infantry. We now regret to state that he was killed in action on Septem- ber 25th. Second-Lieutenant Armstrong, who twenty-six years of age, had been in the Army eight years. He enlisted in the 16th Lancers, and transferred to the 17th Battalion, along With his step-brother, Edward A. Gordon, and they went to India together. They were drafted from India to France in November. 1914, and have served there since. His 4tep-brother is at Present sick in hospital. Second-Lieutenant Armstrong only had three days' leave dur- ing his whole Army career. He was educat?d at Wycliffe School, and attended the Sunday school and church. Before enlisting he was employed in the offices of Messrs. Greenall's dis- tillery, to which place he went from the offices of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd. At the morning and evening services at Wycliffe Church and the afternoon meeting of</p>
        <p>was</p>
        <p>SECOND-LIEUT. CHAS. M. ARMSTRONG. Killed.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. on Sunday, sympathetic refer- ences were made regarding the dead Lieutenant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>SGT.-MAJOR F. SWEENEY.</head>
        <p>SGT.-MAJOR F. SWEENEY.</p>
        <p>A Former Well-known Member of the Liberal Club.</p>
        <p>Regimental-Sergeant-Major Fred Sweeney, of the ,Cheshire Regiment, whose death in action we reported last week, was very well-knoWn in Warrington. He joined the South Lancashire Regiment in his youth, and belonged to the gymnastic staff, and while at Orford Barracks he held the rank of gyMnastic instructor. He was a member of the Liverpool Gymnasium, and instructed in gymnastics the Lymm Company of the 3rd Volunteer Battalion of the Cheshire Regiment, which was at that time under the command of Colonel J. Groves, who took 3 personal interest in Sergeant-Major Sweeney. During his period at Orford Barracks he held private classes in fencing and boxing,. of which</p>
        <p>sports he was an excellent exponent. having won several Army competitions, and also cora- : peting frequently at the Military Tournaments held at London. During his sojourn in War- rington he was one of the most popular mcm-</p>
        <p>ber; of the Liberal Club. On retiring from the Army he took up a position with Messrs. Brunner, )iond and Co.. Northwieh, and was eaptain of the works hockey team. On the out- break of war he joined the Cheshire Regiment,</p>
        <p>SERGEANT-MAJOR SWELNLY Killed.</p>
        <p>and was quickly. promoted Company-Sergeant- Major, and Eince going to France had been made Regimental-Sergeant-Major. He died from Wounds in the head, left wrist. anil left thigh..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>PRIVATE J. ASTII.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. ASTII.</p>
        <p>Brother Says He kne.w No Fear</p>
        <p>When 1 n The Trenches,</p>
        <p>Officially reported as wounded on August Bth• of this year, Private John Astil, South Lanca- shire Regiment, and son of Mr. and Mrs. James. Astil, 18, Hale-street, is now officially reported to have been killed; the news coming from the War Office. ,His parents also 'received the following letter from his brother, Private John James Astil, South Lancashire Regiment, who wrote: "I hope you are keeping your heart up about John, as you can 'always say that you had a son who died for his Country. He was a good soldier and knew no fear whOn in the trenches. He was always shouting across to the C4ermans, and even had a dog in the firing line. It is simply a hell hole out here. I have escaped death many times, but you get that `l7kly that you do not care whether you die or not</p>
        <p>_ . Private Astil, who was 23 years of age, enlisted February of this year, and had only been In France eight weeks. He was formerly employed at Longford Wire Works, and was educated at St. Ann's School. His father served for six months in the South Lancashire Regi- °lent, but was discharged on account of ill- health. As already stated, his brother. Private ohn James Astil, is at presmt on active service Rith the South Lanens:dre Regiment.</p>
        <p>.:1:1'..,4111 ' lift , t'...., , :ir' , l'''' . ~ ,</p>
        <p>been received saying he was buried in a little cemetery behind the lines. A son of Mr. James Leigh, 48, Napier-street, Private Leigh had been employed at the Mersey- street Gas Works \ for fourteen years, and was an old boy of the National School, Church- street. He enlisted on March 23rd, of this year, and has three brothers, Jack, Joe and Fred, also serving..</p>
        <p>thought will help to ease the burdens of your 6 sorrow."</p>
        <p>Private Guest, who had been in France for twenty months, twas 21 years of age. He for- merly worked for the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd.', at Grappenhall, and was educated at the Blue Coat 'School. His uncle, Mr. John Burrows, is serving with thesTrans- port Section.</p>
        <p>and brought to Manchester, that he saw Hall, badly wounded in the back, making his way to a dressing station. Clare was carrying a wounded officer on his back at the time, and a shell suddenly falling near by, killed the latter outright. Hall's sisters are naturally very anxious and hope that the pubication of his name and picture again will bring definite in- formation.</p>
        <p>Ernest Richards, of the King's Royal Rifles, who is in a French hospital wounded. "I have been wounded in the left leg," he wrote, "but it is nothing to worry about, as it isn't serious. I have had a good run for sixteen months. I have been admitted into hospital and it's like being in heaven to get in .a good bed again, but I would sooner be in one in dear old Blighty.' " =mon Richar4s3 who is twenty-six years of</p>
        <p>HIPPODROME FULL VARIETY MATINEE, MONDAY, at. 2A.--Ad.vt.</p>
