      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <p>billiard player, and played for the St. Barnabas' Club. He also played football for St. Barnabas' A.F.C. A brother-in-law is serving in the Army.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>PRIVATE C. DAVENPORT.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE C. DAVENPORT.</p>
        <p>First Man on Top of the Crest</p>
        <p>at Guillemont.</p>
        <p>Yo,terday morning Mrs. Olive Davenport, wire of Private Charles Davenport; King's Liver- pool Regiment, who resides at 9, Fairtield-road, Stockton Heath, received an nfficial .intiwatiob to the effect that her husband had been killed in action.</p>
        <p>Private Davenport was the youngest son of Mr. C. W. Davenport, Farrell-road. 'Stockton Heath, principal, of the well-known firm of Messrs. C. W. Davenport and Sons, building contractors. He was twenty-two years of age last month, and joined the Army shortly after the outbreak of war, and was sent to France after a very short period of eight or nine weeks' training. He was sent almost directly into the firing line, and had a wonderful experience, being present at a number of the fiercest engagements, Neuve Chappeile, Ypres, Loos, Festubert and Guillemont. In fact, this young soldier appears to have borne a charmed life for a long time. He had had many narrow escapes from death. Writing to his ,mother on one occasion, he said :—"I have been buried three times last week, the second time under three Or four men who had been killed. I lid</p>
        <p>PRIVATE C. DAVENPORT. Killed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>difficulty in digging myself out." On another occasion he was buried close to a box of star- lights, and a bomb which had been lying in the trench exploded practically beneath him and blew him out. He sustained severe burns and scorches, but was otherwise uninjured, and lie could afterwards- write to his parents that he was "not downhearted yet." This showed extraordinary strength and determination, as might only naturally be expected from a young man of such fine physical attainments. Re was a splendid athlete, and held the silver Champion- ship trophy of the Grammar School for swim- ming. Ile distinguished himself as a sharp- shooter, at sniping work and on dangerous sentry duties. A testimonial to his qualities as a soldier was given under rather peculiar cir- cumstances. It appears that Mr. Ebenezer Eng- land, who is an old friend of 'the family, was lunching in a Liverpool cafe one day and. sat at the table with an officer belonging to the Liverpool Regiment. They began to talk of 'Warrington, and the officer said, "Do you know a family named Davenport in Stockton Heath ?." Mr. England replied, "Very well." "Do you know a young fellow named Charles Daven- port?" "Yes. I knew him from his youth." "Well," said the officer, "lie has gone through a terrible time from the first. He is the finest man I have got in my regiment, and was the first man on top of the crest at Guillemont."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <p>Killed.</p>
