....., • i .</p>
        <p>LCE.-CORPL. ALBERT E. BRADSHAW.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <p>Bradshaw, of a Canadian Regiment, son of the late Mr. James Edward Bradshaw and Mrs. Bradshaw, late of Church-place, and brother of Mr. T. P. Bradshaw, secretary of the Warring- ton Rugby Football Club, and a well-known friendly society worker, has been killed in action.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Bradshaw, who was thirty_ seven years of age, was born in Warrington, and as a boy attended the National Schools, Church- street. He emigrated to Canada in 1904, and took up his residence at Toronto, where he leaves a widow and three young children. He enlisted on the very day the Lusitania was sunk, and came to England for training last year. He gave up his stripes in order to join .anothr division, and sailed for France on the 15th March this year. Lce.-Corpl. Bradshaw had had previous military experience; having been for some years in the Loyal North Lancs. Regi- ment, and had served in different parts of the Empire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>RIFLEMAN T. DICKENSON.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN T. DICKENSON.</p>
        <p>Soldier's Son Killed And Another Wounded. No further news having come to hand regard- ing Rifleman Thos. Dickenson, of the Rifle Brigade, who was posted as missing after an eneovomont on Sopt.mber 25th of last year,</p>
        <p>was wounded in July and is still in hospital. John James, the eldest, joined the Rifle Brigade in the early days of the war, but was discharged after a few weeks' service owing to deafnes„.</p>
        <p>„ . ... • . . „ . • • ' . , • • •• . : . - • 744444 • • • „ ' * 111.!e.i!.:e•s ,</p>
        <p>PRIVATE A. H. lIITCHINSON Killed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>PRIVATE W. JONES.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE W. JONES.</p>
        <p>" He Died Bravely And Fear- lessly," Says Comrade.</p>
        <p>Private William Jones, of the South Lancs. Regiment, son of Mr. and Mrs. W. Jones, 23, Tanners'-lane, has been killed in action. In a letter to Mr. Jones on October sth, a non- commissioned officer wrote :—" The parcel whia you sent to him arrived too late, for he was killed some days prior to its arrival. All his com- rades join with me in tendering such sympathy as we are able to give. Oui hearts go out to yourself, his mother, and all the family in this, your great, trial. May the knowledge that he died bravely and fearlessly help you to bear your loss with strength and fortitude. To him rightly belongs a measure of honour, inasmu,:h that he contributed to our huge successes of recent' date. You have sacrificed much, great will be your reward." Private Jones, who was nineteen years of age, and an "old boy" of Silver-street School, was in the Territorials prior to the outbreak of war. He was mobilised along with his battalion and went to France with the first draft. He</p>
        <p>RIFLEMAN ROSE. Killed.</p>
        <p>prizes for his prowess in that direction. He was also interested in bowling. His brother John, of the South Lancs. Regiment, was killed on the 10th June last year, a second, Charles, of the K.R.R., was wounded in October last year, while a third, William, has been serving at Salonika and is now in hospital suffering from fever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
