      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>PRIVATE W. BARKER.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE W. BARKER.</p>
        <p>" Will Be Very Hard To Re- place," Writes Quartermaster.</p>
        <p>Private W. Barker, East Lancashire Regi- ment, and son of Mr. Edward Barker, 29, Eldon-street, has been killed in action. His relatives being anxious about him on account of the stoppage of his usual letters home, wrote to Company-Quartermaster J. E. Cherry, who, writing on August 31st, said:—" I am sorry to say that Private W. Barker was killed instantly by a shell on July 25th. He was well liked by all his comrades, who express their deepest sympathy with you. His sergeants miss him, and he will be very hard to replace." Writing home a short time before he met his death, Private Barker said: "I am very pleased to tell you I have scraped through very nicely so far, but it has been very rough. You should have seen them run, they are no good at hand to hand fighting. They might be all right at long range fighting. Another thing I want to tell you, that they are still on the run, and we mean to keep them going." . . .</p>
        <p>Private Barker went to the Front about two years ago, having been in the East Lancashire Regiment ten or eleven years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>MISSiNG.</head>
        <p>MISSiNG.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE G. E. HALL.</p>
        <p>News Wanted Of Wounded And Missing Man.</p>
        <p>Miss C. Hall, of 15, Lilford-street, Warring- ton, is extremely anxious to get news of her brother, Private George Edward Hall, of the South Dines. In our issue of the 26th August we published the portrait of this soldier and the fact that he had been wounded on the Bth of that month. She has since received the following note from the War Office:—" In reply to your inquiry received 14th September, 1916, I am commanded by the Army Council to in- form you that the above soldier has been re- ported as wounded and missing since the Bth August, 1916." It seems that the family were informed by a young soldier named Clare, himself wounded</p>
        <p>ton, saying that he died in a base hospital on September 28th.</p>
        <p>Enlisting in November last year, Private Melvin had been on active service since March of this year. September 29th, the day on whizh the news of his death came to hand, was his 24th birthday. He was formerly engaged in the wheel shop at Dallam Forge. He was an old boy of St. Peter's School, and attended the Gospel Hall. Taking an interest in football, he played with the Longford United team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <p>ImsagormlaYlalMlligiorNaNg ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>LANCE-CORPL. F. W. GUY.</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPL. F. W. GUY.</p>
        <p>" A Splendid Fellow And Excellent Worker."</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. F. Guy, 38, Tanners'-lane, have been informed of the • death from wounds of their son, Lance-Corporal Frederick Wm. Guy, of the South Lancs. Regiment. Captain R. W. Crummack wrote under date October sth :—" ii is with deep regret that I have to tell you of the death of your son, Lance-Corporal F. W. Guy. Ho was wounded on the night of Septem- ber 25/26th, and died a day later in hospital. He was a splendid fellow and an excellent worker, very keen and always did his best." A letter of sympathy has also been received from a minister—the Rev. C. F. English, who interested himself in Lance-Corporal Guy when the latter was in training.</p>
      </div>
