        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL F. W. GUY. Died of Wounds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <p>age, joined the Army in eptember, 1914. and went to France twelve months last July. He was educated at Fleathside S.tliool, worshipped at All Saints, Mission, and was employed at Walker's Brewery since leaving sehool up to the time he enlisted. He is very well known in the town, and was good cricketer and football player, being a member of Walker's' geams. By ' his prowess he wen' a silver media. His. ibrotiter Josialt served' at the Front for a while., but was afterwards diaeharged from Ow Army on :mount of sickness.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>PRIVATE 11. IVAINTETIf.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE 11. IVAINTETIf.</p>
        <p>Hands OW Message While Passity . Through Warrington.</p>
        <p>Private Harold,Dainteth, Royal Fusiliers, has been wounded, in action. He now lies at W. wick, and while going through Warrington to. hospital on Thursday night handed a note th. the driver of the ambulance car, who'handedsit to a policeman, who afterwards handed' it. to" Dainteth's brother, and in that way the family became acquainted with the wounded solaieee whereabouts.</p>
        <p>Private Dainteth enlisted in February of this year, and had been eight weeks in France. rib formerly worked at the St. Helens Cable Co., Ltd., was an old boy of People's College, and is 20 years of age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>PRIVATE F. C. DALE.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE F. C. DALE.</p>
        <p>Burled by Shell Explosion Along</p>
        <p>With Five Others.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Stephen Dale, 264, Manchester.. road, have received news that their son, Private Findley Cecil Dale, of the Liverpool Scottish, has met with a mishap at the Front. He and five others were together when a high explosive shell burst about ten yards from them. All were buried, two being killed and three wounded, Private Dale being the only one un- wounded. Half his kit was blown off his back. He has since written home to say he has it come out of hospital, but :s still resting. Private Dale, who is twenty Years of age, wag before enlisting employed as a junior cl3.:k tc). the Warrington Board of Guardians.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>PRISONER OF WAR.</head>
        <p>PRISONER OF WAR.</p>
        <p>LCE.-CORPL. S. MOULD.</p>
        <p>Woke Up Only Twenty Yards</p>
        <p>From the Germans.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Samuel Mould, of the Royal Scots Fusiliers, son of Mrs. Mould, 37, Laira- street, is a prisoner of war in Germany. He was previously officially reported missing, but his mother was pleased to get the following letter, dated September 17th, from a hospital at Munster (Wesft.): "I am getting on all right and healing up rapidly. I got wounded at half- past ten on Saturday night, the 12th August. It was a narrow escape, as the shell which I got a splinter from killed our company com- mander and three more men, who were all within a yard of me. I crawled into the hole which the shell had made, and then the rain came on, but I hardly noticed it, and made the best of things by going to sleep. I got a sur- prise. next morning, when I saw the Germane only about twenty yards from me. They beck- oned me to come in, which I had to do the best way I cdild They bandaged Me, and gave me black bread,' meat, and a kind of salt water. At night time I had to go through our own shell fire along with some of them to a village behind their lines, where I got a fresh bandage and some more black tommy.' I was in two hospi- tals in France, and was finally moved to the one lam now in. We get vegetables for dinner, and with it either fish (uncooked), a small piece of meat, or two eggs. For the remaining meals we have a limited amount of bread and jam and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</p>
        <p>Parades. • To-day (Saturday), 3 p.m.—rieket Grouni,. Arpley, Company drill. Uniform. Monday, 8 p.m.—Headquarters, Platoons No. 1 and 2. Tuesday and Thursday, 8 p.m.—Headlimners,. Platoons Nos. 3, 4 and 5.</p>
        <p>Sunday,lOctoher 22nd, 3 p.m.—Company drill, Cricket Ground. ,Recruits.—Monday, Tuesday and Thursday,. 8 p.m.</p>
        <p>Fire Brigade Drill, Saturday, October 21st, a pan., Fire Station, Recruits for Fire Drill who cannot attend Saturday attend Thursday next, 3 Pau-</p>
