      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>. „..e,'' „ ~ ~„, f_.l ,f A I3TRICT itietw3 , L ..z__ .... .....</head>
        <p>. „..e,'' „ ~ ~„, f_.l ,f A I3TRICT itietw3 , L ..z__ .... .....</p>
        <p>14Y MM</p>
        <p>LOCAL FACTS AND FANCIES.</p>
        <p>By CRITIC.</p>
        <p>"I am sorry to say that I have not received any local papers just lately," writes a local soldier from Egypt.</p>
        <p>But his mother informs lee that she has been sending him his favourite paper, "The Examiner," quite regularly.</p>
        <p>Hard lines, isn't it?</p>
        <p>I can easily understand his disappointment. Once you get into the habit of reading it, you begin to look forward to it. And beyond all doubt, it has proved to he " ' Tommy's ' favourite." "Citizens' SUnday." Now then, all your citizens, get ready!</p>
        <p>And don't forget to be at the Cross by 2.15 p.m. to-morrow.</p>
        <p>• - Members of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment, tradesmen and Friendly Societies will march in procession to the Church, headed by an aug- mented band.</p>
        <p>What form the augmented band will take I am not at present in a position to state. Still, we have hopes.</p>
        <p>I do hear from private circles that a memorial for the late Sergeant Brookes, our Russian decoration hero, is now quite probable.</p>
        <p>I only hope that if the people of Lymrii do have the chance to recognise him they will do it thoroughly and rally round the organisers. He brought great honour to the village, and proved himself in every respect to be "A soldier and a man."</p>
        <p>Personally I have no doubt as regards the result. I think that it is the wish of every citizen.</p>
        <p>Harvest festival services were held at the Baptist Chapel, Higher-lane, last Sunday. The chapel was prettily decorated, and the congregations were fairly large. During the last few weeks our wounded at Brookfield have• been busily engaged settling billiard handicaps. Two have already been settled, and the win- ners have been subject to a great deal of chaff from their colleagues. "Is one wife sufficient?"—(" Daily Sketch" placard.) I should imagine so, but not knowing can't say, and not being interested, donk care a rap. The other day I overheard a man and his wife settling a very fine point. The man said he wished he was a woman, and the woman said she wished she was a man.</p>
        <p>The male said, "Aye! you only wish you were A man because you are over military age," whereas the lady wittily replied, "Yes! and you only wish you were a woman because you are under military age." I won't divulge the end of the argument, but I think it ended all right. Possibly hubby promised to get up and put the kettle on next time the clocks Pare put back. A fire broke out in one of the fustian shops early the other morning, and was detected and extinguished by a local constable. Constables are of use some times, although some people don't seem to realise it.</p>
        <p>Horse With Van Dashes Though The Vil- tage.—Considerable- excitement was caused in the village last Tuesday evening when a horse and van belonging to Burgons, Ltd., dashed through the village at a terrific rate, leaving its driver well behind. Luckily there was a clear course. When through the village it made for the Bridgewater Arms, then turned towards the back entrance to the Congrega- tional Church. This road, however, is very narrow, and after getting part way up one of the van's shafts got entangled in the iron rail- ings, which broiight the run to an end. It appears that the driver was unharnessing the animal and had just taken off its bridle when, without any warning, it galloped away. No one was injured, and bar slight damage to the van, everything turned out satisfactorily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>CRUEL RUMOURS</head>
        <p>CRUEL RUMOURS</p>
        <p>,Which May Cause a Lot of Trouble.</p>
        <p>"S. D. M." writes:---</p>
        <p>Why are people so eager to jump at conclu- sions? When some people get hold of a bit oi news they seem to delight in exaggerating it. Recently I have come • across many cases in which people have suffered great pain and anxiety through cruel rumours. Why should be? If people would only think and not let their imagination carry the too far, this evil would be avoided. I should like to meet the authors of these rumours face to face and tell them 'what I think about them and their treacherous methods. Is not the position of things bad enough without being made worse by unfounded gossip? Perhaps the authors of the stories do not realise that this evil is punish- able by law. If ever I can get to the founda- tion of a punishable rumour I shall see that the culprits are treated in the proper way. Some of the stories may originate through slight mistakes, but even these could be avoided by a little forethought; on the other hand some of them are so had that they must be pure inventions of an evil mind. It is in the interests of the entire community that these people should be brought to justice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>LYMM PLATOON ORDERS.</p>
        <p>Sunday, October 15th.--Church Parade (Citi- zens' Sunday). Fall in at Drill Hall 2.10 p.m. Tuesday, October 17th.-7.30 p.m., Route March; Uniform or Brassards. Thursday, October 19th.-7.30 p.m:, Shooting Competition, Miniature Rifle Association, Lymm v. Bradford; 8, Recruits' Drill. Saturday', October 21st.—Battalion Parade at Woodhey's Hall, Ashton-on-Mersey. Train leaves Lymm at 2.40 p.m. Details later. Orderlies.—Sergeant Wood, Privates Hartley and Hillier.</p>
        <p>G. R. WILLIAMSON, Platoon Commander.</p>
        <p>Try Cockle's Pills next time. Buy a box to-day and have tli•em handy.</p>
      </div>
