      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>PRISONER IN GERARNY.</head>
        <p>PRISONER IN GERARNY.</p>
        <p>‘N Ito Hopes To See His 11.4 mm Friends</p>
        <p>Soon.</p>
        <p>Writing from Lager Lamstedt, Hanover, Ger- many, to Mr. David Daniels, confectioner, Booth's Hill, Private E. Worrall, a prisoner of war states :—" On Monday last I received your kind and welcome parcel, and I am sorry to tell you that the cake and bread enclosed were uneatable. I guess it must have been a long time coming through; but never mind, it's tho first one that has happened like this, so we can- not grumble. When you send another parcel will you please send me card also,- also a list of articles the last one contained. I am quite well at present, hoping you are the same your- self. Remember me to all the friends in Lymm. Hoping to see you soon.—l remain, yours truly, Private E. Worrall." Private E. Worrall, prior to joining the Cheshire Regiment as a regular soldier, resided at Rush Green. He was taken prisoner during the memorable retreat from Mons, in which the Cheshire Regiment took an active part. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>PRIVATE JOHN PERCIVAL.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN PERCIVAL.</p>
        <p>Returns From Hospital To Trenches</p>
        <p>Only To Get Wounded.</p>
        <p>Private John Percival, the Cheshire Regi- ment, has been wounded in Ihe right leg with shrapnel. The news was received by his mother at Oldfield-road, Statham, last Sunday morn- ing, in a letter sent by Company-Sergeant- Major H. Clare, stating that the gallant soldier had been admitted into a base hospital. He had been in the trenches only a few days after being discharged from hospital, where he had been treated for shell shock. Ile was an old</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN PERCIVAL. Wounded.</p>
        <p>Territorial, but, however, was refused for ser- vice with the corps at the outbreak of war. He was eventually accepted and joined the Cheshire Regiment in March, 1915, and was drafted mit to France about four months ago. He is--21 years of age, and was educated at the Lymm and Statham Schools, and wor- shipped• at the Parish Church. Prior to enlist- ing he was employed in the engine sheds of the Manchester Ship Canal Co., at Partington. His late father was an Army pensioner. His brother, Private Peter Percival, of the Cheshires, was wounded while serving in the Dardanelles.campaign, and is at present train- ing in England.</p>
