      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>ABLE SEAMAN JIM JELLY</head>
        <p>ABLE SEAMAN JIM JELLY</p>
        <p>Home On Furlough.</p>
        <p>Able Seaman Jim Jelly, of H.M.S. a well-known local man, has this week been home on a short furlough. Talking to our representative, he informed him that he had been granted a short leave of four days while his boat was in dock for repairs. Life in the Navy, he said, though very healthy and sen- sational, is at times very strenuous and trying. This is the first time for six months that he has been ashore, but in the future the men are hoping to get four days' furlough every four months. That, of course, is if circumstances will permit. There are various advantages that colleagues ashore dpn't get; "for in- stance," he said. "we get cigarettes and to- bacco at half the price that you pay ashore. That, of course, is due entirely to the big duty they have to pay on tobacco before they can bring it into this country." "Have you seen much fighting?' our repre- sentative asked.</p>
        <p>"Well," lie said. "nothing to swank about, but I have -seen sufficient." Able Seaman Jim Jelly, who is the son of Mr. James Jelley, of Carlton View, Ileatley, rejoins his ship to-morrow (Sunday).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>ON EARTH.</head>
        <p>ON EARTH.</p>
        <p>Sergeant J. H. Moston's Opinion Of</p>
        <p>Prewilt Life In France.</p>
        <p>Writing to his relatives in Lymm, Sergeant J. H. Moston, the Cheshire Regiment, says:— "Excuse me not writing so often, for I have been very busy. What I saw when I was out here the first time was nothing compared with what it is now. It is simply H on earth. We are 'winning all right, and the sooner they realise it the better. You will be sorry to learn that Private John Percival, of Statham, is wounded. I don't think it will prove a serious wound. I hope not, at any rate." Sergeant J. H. Moston was mobilised with Cheshire Regiment at the outbreak of war, and went out to France in February, 1915. Six months ago he was sent over to Ireland suffer- ing from a general breakdown, and was there during the Irish rebellion. He went back again to France about three months ago,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PRIVATE JAMES JENNINGS</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JAMES JENNINGS</p>
        <p>Wants to Know Why He has not</p>
        <p>been Reeeh Ina any papers Lately.</p>
        <p>Private James' Jennings, Manchester •Regi- meat, who is at present somewhere in Egypt, writing to his mother at Moston-street, 'New- fields, says: "Before I go any further I must thank you for the P.O. you sent me in the last letter. It is very welcome, though not much -arse at present, for we are still at - (No Man's Land). We are miles out on the desert and liv- ing in dug-outs. It is not a bad way of living now that we have got used to it, and we don't half get some fun, especially over cooking our food, which, as you will imagine, we have to do ourselves. The food is not at all bad at present, but a little monotonous, and I shan't beat all sorry when we get near to a Y.M.C.A. hut so that we can have a little burst-up. We are now getting some very assuring bits of news from cablegrams. That just reminds me. How is it that you have not sent me any local papers just lately'? I have not received any,for the last two months. I have written a letter to Wm. Taylor, and if be is anywhere in Egypt it will find him. I am sorry to hear about Harry Jackson being reported missing, and I hope by the time you receive this letter they will have heard some- thing about him." Private James Jennings is a local lad, and is exceedingly well known to " Examiner " readers, and is looked upon as a gifted corre- spondent. The Wm. Taylor referred to is an- other Newfields' soldier, and had not been heard of for some time, but he has now turned up in Mesopotamia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
