      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Indispensable_To Business, But Goes Into a</head>
        <p>Indispensable_To Business, But Goes Into a</p>
        <p>Munition Works.</p>
        <p>A somewhat surprising case came up for hearing at the last meeting ,of the local Tribunal. The applicants were a firm of painters and decorators. They appealed for a member of the firm, whom they claimed to be a salaried partner, and indispensable to the working of the business. The man had already had one exeffiption, and this was a second appeal. It will be remembered that this case came up for hearing at the previous meeting, when no representative of the firm appeared, and their solicitor, Mr. G. L. Welford, apolo- gised for their absence. The case was found to be out of order and the Tribunal decided to postpone it for a fortnight and gave permission for the appeal to be pul in order. lirever, since that date the man had left his employ- ment and gone into munitions. Mr. G. L. Welford, their solicitor, at the last meeting, asked the Tribunal for permission to withdraw the appeal, but the Tribunal declined that re- quest, and dismissed the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Other Results.</head>
        <p>Other Results.</p>
        <p>Butcher: Exemption granted until February, 1917, subject to joining V.T.C. Coal merchant's son: Exemption granted until February, 1917, subject to joining V.T.C. Local confectioner, who appealed on personal grounds, chiefly on account of ill-health, adjourned for Medical Board's decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>The Late Commander Hut,chings.—At the Chester Consistory Court on Monday, before Sir Phillip Baker Wilbraham, chancellor, the Rev. Harry Vernon Pigot, rector, and Messrs. Henry Standish Leyland and Oswald Lofthouse, wardens of Grappenhall Parish Church, were granted a faculty to affix on the wall of the north aisle of the church a marble tablet to the memory of John Hutchings, commander in the Royal Navy, who lost his life in an ex- plosion in H.M.S. Natal on 30th December, 1915.* Mr. Charles Graham Hutchings (brother), of Grappenhall, will defray the cost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>PAINFUL DOMESTIC CASE.</head>
        <p>PAINFUL DOMESTIC CASE.</p>
        <p>Wife Breaks Domi While 6iNinu</p>
        <p>Evidence In Court.</p>
        <p>When giving evidence in support of ht‘r claim for a separation order against her hus- band, Joseph Starkey, of Ross Olen, Sunnyside Villas, Knutsford-road, Grappenhall, at Dares- bury Sessions on Tuesday, Mrs. Mary Ann Starkey bro.ke down so completely in the wit- ness ,box that she had th be assisted to a seat in the Court, and did not recover for some time. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Complainant was represented by Mr. T. S Steel and the defendant by Mr. A. Browne.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel said the summons for separation I was brought by complainant on account of the persistent cruelty of her husband. The com- plainant was 58 years of age, and the parties had been married over 35 years. There were Inine children living out of a family of sixteen. IComplainant had had a very unhappy life for a great number of years, and had had to com- plain of defendant's conduct from time to time. In June, 1914, an agreement for separation was made, but she afterwards went to live with him. His conduct, however, became so bad that she had to come again to the Court. The cases of cruelty were numerous and spread over a long period. During the past six months he struck her on scores of occasions and threatened her life. He swore he would swing for her, and said he would cut her -throat, 'at the same time using very filthy language, often in the presence of the children. Mr. Steel then detailed several instances of cruelty, one of these being when defendant ran at his wife . with his head and struck her in the mouth and loosened her teeth, while he also took her by the throat and dragged her about the kitchen. On the 19th September, in consequence of his conduct, com- plainant left him. The defendant is a flesher at Long's Tannery. Mrs. Starlgey then gave evidence in support of her advocate's statements, and said that hey husband was a heavy drunkard. He had made foul accusations against her, which, of course, had no foundation.</p>
        <p>Witness at this stage became quite faint and almost collapsed. She was assisted to a seat. Mr. Browne said he could produce evidence to show that the defendant had not missed a single quarter in two years and he worked from six in the morning until eight o'clock at night. The former separation allowance was ss. a week, and the circumstances had, he submitted, not changed. The advocates having conversed for a little,</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel told the -Court that Mr. Browne had offered 12s. 6d.. and that Mrs. Starkey wanted 15s. She had one child aged 14 living With her. The Court made an order for separation and allowed the wife 15s. weekly. They further ordered payment of a guinea advocate's fee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending Saturday, October 21st. Monday.—Musketry at range, 7.30 p.m. Range officer, Private F. W. Grounds.</p>
        <p>Tuesday.—Musketry instruction. Fall in at headquarters, 7.45 p.m. Thursday.—Squad drill .at headquarters,- 7.45 P.m-</p>
        <p>Friday.—Musketry at range for recruits, 7.30 p.m. Names of those required to attend will be posted at headquarters. Range officer, Section- Commander W. Trantom.</p>
        <p>Saturday.—Company drill. Fall in at head- quarters at 3 p.m. Orderlies.—Section-Corporal F. Fox land Pte. W. Cotterell. By order,</p>
        <p>F. A. FROST, O.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>69, LONDON ROAD, STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>69, LONDON ROAD, STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>BATCH OF FARMERS</p>
        <p>Summoned For Not Having</p>
        <p>Lists Of Men Of Military Age.</p>
        <p>Summonses of exceptional interest to farmers and other employers of labour were heard at Da resbury Sessions, Stockton Heath, on Tues- day. There .were about forty defendants— sixteen of whom were represented by Mr. T. S. Steel, solicitor, and each defendant was charged that being • a person employing male persons between the ages of 18 and 41, unlaw- fully did fail to make.and keep constantly ex- posed' in a conspicuous place in or about the premises a li4 of such persons in the form pre- scribed by the Defence of the Realm (Consoli- dated) Regulations Act, 1914. Mr. Steel said that all his clients would plead guilty to a technical offence, and as it wa, understood the other defendants would follow suit, the Chairman, Mr. James Hepherd, said that it might be sufficient to hear one case. Mr: Joseph Bolshaw, Brow Farm, Antrobus, was then called.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Owen said that notices had been posted throughout the district telling the people what they should do. It was found that especially in the agricultural districts it was not beinc, complied with. Mr. Steel said that in all the cases he repre- sented he offered a plea of ignorance. More proceedings might have 'been obviated if the village constable had in his daily round said to the farmers he met, "Have you got your notice up as regards the men employed by you?" Several defendants came from Antrobus. a very remote district, and he had been told that no notice had been posted there. Farmers generally were busy men, and the Bench knew the difficulties underwhich they had been work- ing. Their men had been taken away and they had to work -long hours. They did not see the daily papers—all they saw was a weekly paper, which they casually looked through on Sunday. Some of these men did not 'employ other labour than their own sons=--some of their sons had to go to the war and they had only one son at home. When the constable came to them on the 15th, 16th or 18th July they all at once sent to the local Post Office for forms, and he under.</p>
        <p>stood that the Post Office in Antrobus had no I forms. Some sent to Northwich, and then they could not get them. Mr. Bashaw sent all the way to Stockton Heath to get a form. All these men were respectable law-abiding citizens, and I had no desire to break the law. , He suggested that it would be rather unfair to record a con- viction against them. Publicity would be given to the matter and that would have the desired effect. Justice, he submitted, would be met by the payment of costs. Superintendent Owen said he could not accept the suggestion that a constable ought to go about to every farmer. The farmer ought to know the law. He came into town to sell his produce, and must have seen the ngtices. The remaining defendants were thell separately charged, and one man said that he got the notice up the day the constable called. The Chairman: Like a dog licence: it's got after the policeman has been. (Laughter.) He went on to say that the summonses were issued to bring this law before the defendantc. Every one.of them ought to have known about it. 'lt was passed in May, and this was October. The summons in each "case would be dismissed on payment of five shillings costs. The costs were paid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>PAD GATE.</head>
        <p>PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>, Wesley Guild.—The monthly devotional meet- ing of the Wesley Guild was held on Thursday evening, when an address was given by the Rev. A. J. Morton on the remaining days of the life of St. Paul. There was a good attendance. Accident to a Farmer.—Mr. Joseph Brimelow, son of Mrs. Brimelow, of Enfield Farm, Croft, whilst attending to his duties as teamsman in the stable, was kicked by one of the horses and very badly bruised about the face and body. Assistance was obtained and he was afterwards removed to the Warrington Infirmary. The latest report is that he is progressing favour- ably.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE.</head>
        <p>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—l read with interest the letter published in your columns last Saturday from Lieutenant Peters, drawing attention to the icy indiffer- ence of the Padgate people towards- the merits and work of the C.L.B. Cadets.</p>
        <p>The "appalling laxity" complained of is manifest not only in Padgate, but an apathy amounting almost to contempt for the move- ment is displayed by the public generally. Con- sidering that the openly avowed aim of the organisation is purely the production of zealous Churchmen, it is a matter for surprise to find that those in whom the welfare of the Church is reposed, should underestimate the immense possibilities the movement offers. If only by reason of its military constitution, the Church Lads' Brigade should commend it- self to the sympathies of the populace, inasmuch as it provides a body of youths, a large number soon to become eligible for service, ready dis- ciplined and partially trained—an embryo British Army! Lieutenant Peters' plaint is, I think, a genuine one, and it is to be hoped that it will be the means of bringing the virtues of the Cadets into due regard. Yours faithfully; SYMPATHISER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Trench Fever.—Sapper Richard Rigby, son of Mr. Richard and Mrs. Rigby, South-lane, has notified his parents that he,is in hospital in Manchester suffering with trench fever. His service with the Royal Engineers at the Front has extended for some considerable time, during which he has experienced the usual lot of our men who are so nobly upholding the traditions of British honour. An interesting incident occurred when one of our village lads, Walter J. Foden, R.A.M.C., helped him off the hos- pital ship to firmer ground. He is at present progressing favourably. Wesley Guild Social.—On Saturday the Wes- ley Guild -members held their first Social for this season, a particularly large number at- tending. A good concert was presented to the audience, who thoroughly appreciated the artistes, who were as follows:—Miss Bessie Scott, solo; Mr. J. Williams, solo; Miss L. Bates, pianoforte solo; Mr. B. Smith, violin solo; Miss Thomas, recitation. Following the musical portion refreshments were served, after which a variety of games were played to the amusement of all concerned. The Rev. F. Hudson, resident minister, fulfilled the duties of chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>PICTURISED ROMANTIC NOVEL BY ROY L.McCARDEff. ADAPTED FROM THE SENSATIONAL -FLYING A- SERIAL FILM BY ALEC J. BRAID. ,</head>
        <p>A PICTURISED ROMANTIC NOVEL BY ROY L.McCARDEff. ADAPTED FROM THE SENSATIONAL -FLYING A- SERIAL FILM BY ALEC J. BRAID. ,</p>
        <p>rectors in this story are purely imaginary, living</p>
        <p>and have no connection with or relation to ang personj</p>
        <p>by the doctor's orders. Believe me, lam your friend and mean no harm to you or him. On the contrary, I will do all I can for both of you." Later in the day Arthur roused from the mor- phine-induced sleep and demanded that Esther should be brought to him. Durand's wits were sharp: "You have been ter- ribly injured. The girl you speak of has.not been here. These are fever visions."</p>
        <p>"No, no; I saw her, spoke to her. I know she has been sitting by my side," replied the sufferer. In the library the Soul Stranglers spoke of The Diamond from the Sky and wondered when it would come into their hands. "He will recover," said Durand, "but we ;oust make him believe that his injuries have impaired his reason and that this girl, Esther, has never been here. I came out here to get the diamond," continued the •villain. "Ii will turn up, it always does. Meanwhile, here are fat pickings. You," and he turned to Blair, "get everything in your hands at the office that this poor fool upstairs has. I will take care that if he ever recovers in mind or body he will never cause us any trouble, and Durand held up a phial with a red label in which some small white tablets rattled. Blair, who had been fretting and fuming in sulki silence, sprang to his feet, his face contorted with anger and disgust.</p>
        <p>"I do not claim to be a saint. I hate Arthur Stanleigh, and I have always hated him. He stands in my way. But I will have no part in murdering his manhood with drugs with snailing faces." And Blair glared menacingly at both Durand and .De Vaux as though to spring upon them. "Yes, a fine bunch of cold-blooded cowardly mur- derers for money you two are," hissed Blair, as Durand and De Vaux regarded him in silent amaze- ment.</p>
        <p>" And as for you," Blair turned upon Vivian in her nurse's garb, and seized her. " Take off this masquerade. Let us kick out these vermin, and when Arthur is well and strong I'll kill him like a Southern man kills his enemy—man to man, face to face."</p>
        <p>• "You are a fine one to spout heroics," sneered Vivian. "Do you forget—" Then she checked herself, for though she sneered she admired He was a man for all his perversity. She had ne intention of taunting him now for his having mar- dered a weak old man—Dr. Lee—for The Diamond from the Sky. Blair.</p>
        <p>Vivian reflected that even this guilt of Blair's was a crime of sudden passion for possession of the diamond and panic at detection in the theft. After all, it was not the cold, insidious, slow murder--thel strangling of a soul—the others purposed. Her face softened, a look of admiration came into her eyes, she threw her arms around Blair and kissed him passionately. Durand and De Vaux slipped from the room, and Vivian held Blair in her em- braces and worked him to her will. His heart, however, never was in the dastard work the drug thugs planned to carry through.</p>
        <p>Hoardings for miles round told the wonderfu! stories of " Santleigh's Stupendous Circus,' Gorgeous posters in many colours pictured the sights to be seen within the canvas walls of the great tent. Jack Williams and Ben Burk,- paste - bespattered bill-posters, were engaged in spreading the intelligence. If the tent was not crowded every day the fault would not be theirs. The countryside glowed with the blaze of colours on the posters. That night they camped beneath a dead tree ill the woods.</p>
        <p>Both of them had a sweet tooth, and pancake with an abundance of syrup to give it taste WO a favourite dish of theirs. " Flap-jacks " made o'3 these were required syrup, and Burk was annoyed when he discovered that the sweet liquid had given out.</p>
        <p>"You needn't kick. That's no worry; we Passe(" a bee yard a quarter of a mile down the road. 0" and get some honey while I fetch water." To the beehive teudged Burk. grumbling all the way. On the alighting board of the hive, where it fell when the terrified little maid dropped it, lay The Diamond from the Sky. Here was a find more valuable than honey. It might be a rich gertil although found in an unusual place. The firelight would reveal the truth.</p>
        <p>When Jack Williams returned with the water his partner was bending over the fire examining a piece of jewellers. " Whatcher got theKe?" asked he. "A. real diamond; a whopper.," replied Burk. "You're daft. They don't, make diamonds that size., More likely it's a bit of fake." "No; it ain't fake," insisted Burk. "We'll test it and see," said Williams, drawing t' piece of glass from a lantern over the face of the gem. The deeply-scratched -pane of glass severed clear and straight.</p>
        <p>"It's a real diamond," said Williams, hoarse'''. " We're__ rich men, Ben."</p>
        <p>"We? You ain't got no claim on it. I foun4 it; it's mine alone," and Burk clasped the jewel to. him.</p>
        <p>Supper was forgotten by both—by one in the rage of disappointment and by the other in the glory °f possession—and the fire steadily gnawed its v 703" into the base of the dead tree. Beneath the dead tree Jack Williams woke frnin his fitful dreams of the diamond and the desire that obsessed him for it. He saw the tree was burnt almost through at the bottom and wavered. It was on his lips to scream a warning to his sleePing comrade—but the diamond?</p>
        <p>There was a crackle, a tearing sound, and then the tree bent over and crashed down, crushing the sleeping man beneath it. A shower of sparks rose in the air from the ruptured base where the fire had eaten until the tree had fallen.</p>
        <p>_ Ere yet his writhing companion died. Williams had despoiled him of The Diamond from the Ski' crushed against his breast by the fallen tree. Then with trembling hands the murderer, for such he was, hitched the horse to the waggon and drove off, in the night, holding the baleful diamond ever an? anon in the light of the lantern by the ,waggon seal' (To be continued.)</p>
