      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>CHAPTER XX</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XX</p>
        <p>THE SOUL STRANGLERS.</p>
        <p>Newspapers and financial circles had for months been interested in the doings of "John Powell." Such a meteoric career as his was bound to attract attention. Delighting in the intimate details of the life of a man who had suddenly sprung out of nowhere, his doings were made the subject of dis- cussion and speculation. All that was definitely known of the young man waa, the fact that from being engaged in sheep farming he had "struck it rich" in oil and had then invested in mines. His impulsively generous nature and his mag- netic personality, coupled with his youth and good looks, had made him a marked man in the small business world of oil and miring men in which he had in a few months become so important a factor. Hence it was the terrible injbries that had befallen him while inspecting his mining properties had been given much space in the newspapers. The reports were meagre as to just how his injuries had been sustained, save for the fact that his physician, Dr. Frank Durand, a stranger to Los Angeles, had by good fortune been with him at the time of the accident.</p>
        <p>Newspapers also stated that "Mr. Powell's " busi- ness affairs would be looked after by his cousin and eastern business associate, Blair Stanleigh of Richmond, who had been summoned to Los Angeles by Mr. Powell just previous to' his accident. The newspapers made a dranlatic story of the return of the young millionaire to his new palatial home, his first homecoming to it, a crushed, shattered, uncon- scious, and seemingly dying man. Then the accident ceased to be a three days' wonder, and other sensational happenings and events of interest supplanted it in the papers, and "John Powell" and his affairs gave no further con- cern save to those who were personally interested. "The King of Diamonds" was going to take a hand in the game now. Brought on the scene by the disappointed Abe Bloom, the glimpse he had of the diamond as the row started in Mammoth determined his course of action. Frank Durand knew a good thing when he set eyes on it, and he recognised in The Diamond from the Sky a supreme prize.</p>
        <p>Arthur's injuries were serious enough, but they were physical and not mental, and Durand realised that if money was to be-won as well as the diamond the young man's mind must bend to his will. "Rough work gets us nothing," he said to Vivian. "We are playing for millions and the diamond. Drug keep him under its influence, and that will help us along."</p>
        <p>Rebuffed at her first attempt to see Arthur, Esther waited tactfully until one morning she saw Durand and his accomplice, De Vaux, leave the mansion. Blair she knew was at the Pair Hope offices taking charge of the injured man's affairs. Esther was not afraid of them, collectively or singly, but she deemed it best to encounter Vivian Marston, who she learned was in charge of the sick room, alone. Esther had no desire to bring notoriety, and ruin, perhaps, upon the injured man by any prema- ture disclosure of his real identity, if she could help it. But she was deteiTnined to go even to the length were it necessary, and were she further denied access to the one in all the world she loved with every fibre of her brave and loyal little heart. Some intuitive sense of Esther's attitude must have impressed Vivian Marston, for she did not upbraid the manservant. Instead, she said, "I will see the young lady," and followed the servant, from the sick room where Arthur lay in fevered sleep. Vivian was in her costume as nurse, a masquerade she had assumed in furtherance of the plot against the helpless Arthur. She spoke as one having authority.</p>
        <p>" You cannot come in here," she said. " ' Mr. Powell' is at death's door. Any intrusion or ex- citement would be his death." Esther faltered. Then the Stanleigh spirit as- serteil itself. "Better he die with a true friend by him than live with such as you and your associates ministering to him," she said, scornfully. Vivian was cool and kept her wits. "That will be a matter for our friend to decide when he is able to decide it. Will you believe me if I permit you to enter and you can see for yoursblf that he is delirious?"</p>
        <p>Arthur lay with eyes closed tossing in a fe-...red sleep. '.Esther knelt by the bedside, and tier hond caressed the poor bandage'd arm nearest ler. Iler gate was upon the fevered anguisiled 'eonntenanee of Arthur. She paid no V:.rfan, xvlo pr.ssed by the foot of the bed I.nl t,sl; a swell inFtrument from the table. Then viviatt passed e..;tly to the back of the bed between the injured man and the window. She laid a hand as though soothingly _mon The free arm of the unconscious Arthur. It was in this manner that the two women—the one who loved him above all else and the who loved him not at all—waited in the silence of the sick room. Then his eyes opened and he saw her. "Esther," he murmured, "is it you, dear?" "Yes," she whispered, "it is Esther." He smiled, and was about to try to speak again. Then Vivian moved slightly. A film passed over Arthur's dark eyes; they closed and he lay still. The drui had lulled him.</p>
        <p>" You -see he is very weak," said Vivian, quietly. "This meeting had been a shock to him. Is your regard for him so selfish that you would cause his death to gratify your desire to intrude further? Don't you see he is in good hands? When he has recovered he can choose between us." Esther's fortitude gave way. Arthur was so wan she feared even now that the hand of death was on him. She rose to her feet and slowly left the room, followed by Vivian, who closed the door behind her. A heavy dread fell upon the heart of Esther. Callous as Vivian Mprston was, the grief and heartache that the deep blue steadfast eyes so poig- nantly expressed for a moment stirred some tender memory in the breast of the worldly woman. "•I am a trained nurse." she said, "and Dr. Durand is a notable physician. Our friend is hav- ing the best care and treatment. When he is well enough you shall see him. Till then we must abide</p>
        <p>inreeeceeceepeeee,</p>
        <p>_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <p>..0.00+0004 +000040004 ...P.00000.:.04.:•+•:•000.240000 0 0 0 .2* 0 0 • .:,.. 0 •:. •:. 0 000-:•-:•0 00 0 0 •:.•:••:••:••:••:••:. 0 0 •:. 0.,:•0•:.•:,..:••:.•:. 0 40 •:••:••:•0 0.:••:••:••:*0•:•0 •::. NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>and his promptitude in clearing he reminded me both of Jolley—whom, presumably, he is understudying—and Mason of Wigan. He did not lose any time at all, and that amused ex- pression invariably dominating his face, made him really entertaining to watch. When the weather is less boisterous and a greater oppor- tunity of direct play presents itself we shall be able to •see whether he can turn defence into attack. In the first half he tried hard; finding that he could not kick against the wind he ran with the ball, 131,1 t the atmospheric elements and the ponderousness of the opposi- tion, whose momentum and Power were strength- ened thereby, prevented his doing much in this direction. In the second half he had little to to—probably he handled the ball fewer- than half a dozen times—and, one could not judge whether he can make openings as Jolley does. I believe, however, that Horrocks will not be dis- appointing when the testing time comes.</p>
        <p>(Lancashire Section.)</p>
        <p>As for the three-quarters, one can hardly say much for them in the way of attacking, as, owing to the weather conditions, both teams laid themselv6s out to play the "spotting game," but, at the same time one must not overlook their very excellent defence in the first half. If they were not conspicuous in at- tack they amply made up for it in defence. The wind made it well-nigh impossible to keep Swinton out of the home quarters in the first half, and the try which we obtained was per- haps during the only time we really crossed the half-way line. In that successful effort Mills and Young were greatly conspicuous, and from an inside pass Potter finished it off beautifully, brushing aside half a dozen players to do the job. There were so many players in the ruck that various guesses were made as to who did the trick, but to Potter the debt is owed. I like this young man. His debut at home was exceptionally creditable, for, though he is somewhat slow in his movements, he always gets there. He is of that species of player which deceives his opponents. On one or two occasions he feigned passes with success, and when he was running those opposed to him never seemed to _size him up accurately. This apparent slowness of his may 'be an asset now, causing his opponents not to go so fast, but think he might cultivate getting off the mark a little more promptly. Perhaps a short term of track-sprinting with a pistol behind him would rub this habit out! But that he will prove of useful service to Warrington I have no doubt. Tranter was not •as conspicuous as on the last home match, but he worked hard. Perhaps the responsibilities of the captaincy weighed on him and, then again, there was the fact that he was tired owing to having just left work. It was only loyalty to the old club which persuaded him to put on his jersey. •</p>
        <p>It was a bad day for half-backs on Saturday, but Young and Douglas, in spite of the opposi- tion, managed to give their three-quarters a fair share of the ball. But I should like to see Douglas vary his play a little more. He is tre- mendously alive, but he ought to _transfer the ball oftener to his partner instead of pursuing the individualistic method. While his dash and capability are valuable in them- selves (he dropped a capital • goal), a scrummage half's -first mission is to set his three-quarters going, as the ultimate credit of a score usually originates in the half-back .who is quick around the scrum. How rapidly a man can get hi :p three-quarters on the run with the ball is a triie test of the merits of -a scrum- mage half. Irowever; in the first moiety his tactics were possibly the best—holding on or giving low kicks in the strong wind—but in the second half, when Warrington was in 'Swinton's territory practically the whole time, he euglit to have changed his style morel sharply and aimed at serving the three-quarters with the greatest expedition.</p>
        <p>• They had some heavy-weights against them, hut the ,forwards were on the apex of their form. There were several occasions when they literally pushed their opponents all over the field. What I said of the Broughton match seems to be confirmed every week—that is, these young forwards are playing with excep- tional skill. The leadership of Harmer and Green lends " old-headedness " to the pack, and at times when they get on the run they have a terrible momentum, which is not easy to stem.</p>
        <p>DETAILS OF PLAY. Coming to the details of the game, Warrington</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>"THE LIONS" WHACKED.</head>
        <p>"THE LIONS" WHACKED.</p>
        <p>Leave Wilderspool With Tails Between</p>
        <p>Their Legs.</p>
        <p>In the highest of spirits Swinton came to Warrington on Saturday intent on maintaining their unbeaten record, but they went back sadder and wiser men, the losers by 14 points to 9. .The turn of events greatly upset "The Lions," and as defeat seemed certain towards the close of the game they became desperate, and one man, Mee, was ordered off the field. It was a melancholy coincidence that the waggonette which brought them was drawn by ,a pair of funeral horses.</p>
        <p>Knocking about the little wooden hut where Warrington was dressing just before the match, I had a chat with Harmer, who was taking off his khaki. "Yes, it is very good of my officer to let me come down to play for the old club," he said, "but I don't know when I shall be here again, as our battalion has now got a team of its own, and I shall be ex- pected to play with them. We are due at Birkenhead next Saturday, where we play the Cheshires." Young came strolling in with hii usual nonchalance. "When are you joining up, Stan," Harmer asked. "During the week,;" he replied almost with gusto. "You'll have to get your hair cut," rejoined the for- ward, stroking his own head, which showed signs of the barber having recently gathered in the harvest. "Aye, I suppose I shall," said Stanley, "but it isn't that that's troubling me. I don't like getting up early in a morn- ing!' There was some sympathetic laughter, shared by Chesters, who, in wounded soldier's garb, had walked in on his sticks.</p>
        <p>Now for the match. The encounter had been looked forward to with a great deal of enthusi- asm. It was unfortunate that a. gale •raged and militated against a high-class exhibition of handling, but, in spite of this, the match Proved extremely interesting, being conducted at a rare pace, and the narrow difference of</p>
        <p>Points always -seasoning it with excitement:</p>
        <p>The home team had a hard task in the first half. Facing a strong yvind and occasionally a blinding shower severely handicapped them, and very seldom were they able to enter enemy territory. A feeling of depression came over us in the first ten minutes, when Dawson, a fleet-footed Swinton three-quarter, scored a try. From a scrum on the right near, the line a Swinton player broke away, and, having the ball knocked out of his hands behind the line, a rush was made for it, Dawson. who beat Horrock.s 'dropping on to it. When Ryder, with a sure kick, did the necessary, there was a chilly feeling, accentuated by Ryder scoring a penalty goal shortly afterwards. What fol- lowed, however, stimulated hopes, and a warmth of optimism which remained nnabted. There was some rapid scoring. No sooner Itad Ryder converted the try than Warrington got away on the left, and some brisk passing be- tween Young and Mills—one of the prettiest and swiftest movements during the game— culminating in Potter rushing over and notch- ing a try, which .4i ills converted in splendid style. Right from the kick-off Swinton were awarded a penalty for obstruction, and Ryder was up to form for the goal, making the game 9 points to 5-14 points in about fifteen minutes. •</p>
        <p>' I was rather struck by the resourcefulness of young Horrocks, who came up from Rylands's to play at full-back in place of Ben Jolley. During the first half he had a trying ordeal, for the high wind resulted in play being mostly confined to the home quarters. Even when he kicked forward the ball swerved round—picturesquely, but annoyingly—and to make progress on the touch-line was extremely difficult. 'Milder these circumstances not a little cunning had to be irvelzed to heln him out of the impasse in which lin found himself time and time again, and be rose to the occasion very well. Once when the Swinton forwards were on top of him he drooped hugging the ball like a fond mother her child. It saved the situation, and the way he tricked the opposition. though it cost a scrimmage, was laughable. Generally, the defence he .set. would have done credit to a more experienced player. In his methods of gathering up the ball</p>
        <p>Said everywhere In boxes, labelled is-3d and as-Od.</p>
        <p>WELCO FREE GIFT PURE BRITISH ,CPCOA. .SAVE THECOUPONS</p>
