      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>FOOTBALL FIELD.</head>
        <p>THE FOOTBALL FIELD.</p>
        <p>lost the toss and were put to play against a strong wind and an occasional glaring sun. They, how- ever, by close play forced operations well into the Swinton half. Swinton looked like breaking through, but Horrocks fielded well. He, however, spoilt a bit of good play by kicking into touch., Swinton livened up, and several exciting passages saw them carry the play right up to the home line, and in the desperate struggle that ensued Dawson forced his way through several men and planted a try right behind the posts. Ryder had no difficulty in adding the goal. Swinton continued to keep up the pressure, but a free kick brought relief. Hall and Dean on the visitors' right raced away and looked dangerous, but Warrington tackled earnestly and Young saved once in miraculous manner. Off- side ag ist Warrington gave Ryder another chance to score. but the wind carried a well-directed shot just wile. Still the visitors pressed, and still the home lot put in all they knew to stave off dis- aster. Considering the weather conditions play ruled of high order. Swinton generally being the aggressors. Swinton were Persistent, and some splendid passing among the threequarters looked promising, but Winstanley let the ball fall and it was scooped up by Horrocks. A free kick to Swin- ton near the twenty-five line was taken by Ryder, whose shot was carried to the left of the posts by the high wind. With heavy wind and rain hamper- ing them, Warrington were unable to get outside their own half for over twenty minutes, but eventu- ally Tranter and Mills got away. Dawson, how- ever, checked the movement, and, along with Win- stanley, got away, but passed forward to Crudden and Spoiled the effort. Play proceeded .fast and furious, and points were added by both teams in a very few minutes. Ryder having scored a penalty goal, Warrington got away on the left, and there was some brisk passing between Young and Mills, culminating in Potter obtaining a try, which Mills converted in fine style. Ryder immediately added his third goal from a peaaltV. Half-time: Swinton. 3 goals one try (9 points); Warrington, one goal one try (5 points). Better for Warrington.</p>
        <p>Warrington started the second half short of Mills, who had had his head injured, but he was able to resume in a few minutes. Warrington, with the wind, took up the running, and Dawson distin- guished himself by stopping a very ugly rush. Tranter nearly got through under the Swinton goal. but Ryder kicked away in fine style. A minute later Young raced over and Mills added the goal, thus putting the home team ahead. Winstanley raised 'a most violent siege, and play for a period went into Warrington quarters, but only for a short time, and Mills scored a penalty goal and Douglas dropped a goal. Final: Warrington, 4 goals 2 tries (14 points); Swinton, 3 goals one try (9 points). Warrington: Horrocks; Baker, Potter, Tranter, Mills; Young, Douglas; Harmer, Green, Cunliffe, Matthews, Glegg and Ward.</p>
        <p>_ Swinton: Ryder; Hall, Dean. Dawson, Winston-. ley; Kilkariff, Talbot; Holden, Moss, Crudden, Row- ley, Blower and Mee. •</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. A. Lee, of Leigh</p>
        <p>Warrington go to Runcorn to-day on the 2.14 train from Bank Quay. Their team will be selected from:—Horrocks; Mills, Tranter, Potter, Griffiths and Broadhurst; Young, Brookes. Douglas and Dainteth; Harmer, Green, Cunliffe, Matthews, Glegg, Ward and Dumbell.</p>
        <p>" Stan " Young, the popular half-back, has joined the Royal Field Artillery, and is now stationed at Preston</p>
        <p>Warrington met Runcorn at Wilderspool about three weeks ago in their third match of the seasdn. and drubbed tkem by twenty points to nil. We are hoping for and expecting another victory to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>TO-DAY'S MATCHES.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE. Heathside v. St. Thomas'. at Wilderspool; kick- off, 3.30. Referee, Mr. G. Crozier.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>(Lancashire Combination.)</p>
        <p>THE TOWN</p>
        <p>Take Three Points From The Borough.</p>
        <p>After having beaten Eccles Borough at War- rington on the previous Saturday, Warrington Town travelled to the "cake" town last Satur- day, and succeeded in. making a goalless draw in the return game, so that they take three of the four points provided by the home and home matches. It was a creditable performance and should do the Town "gates" some.servick. .</p>
        <p>The weather was exceedingly bad, the ground being very treacherous and rainsoaked. In, spite of this the teams played fast, clever, vigorous (at times too vigorous), most interest- ing football, but,the match was only enjoyed by about 500 spectators. It was easier to make mis- takes than to do the right thing on such a ground, especially by a visiting side.</p>
        <p>At the last minute•the Town were called upon to play Banner; one of their reserves, at inside left, and Ditchfield at left half, owing to Pte. Hey-wood being unable to leave his military duties early enough for the kkk off. It was a pity he could not play, as he- seems to revel under such conditions as those of Saturday, and he had played so well the previous Saturday against the Borough team. Eccles also had a debutant in their ranks at right full-back, named Roberts, who assisted the Town club in many of their games last geason, and previous to that had seen considerable service with the Manchester United Club.</p>
        <p>Most people anticipated that the Borough Would defeat the Town, which is perhaps the reason why they did not do so. Instead, they came perilously near 'being beaten, and were very lucky indeed to make a draw. It was a curious game, in a sense, for it was full Of good football, yet contained any amount of bad play. But this may be excused owing to the state of the ground, which was very slippery.</p>
        <p>The Town, with their half-backeswinging out beautifully-judged passes to either wing, opened out as though they were going to carry all before them, and for some time the Borough forwards were simply spectators, while their half-backs were puffing and blowing, and the full-backs were tackling and kicking in front of the net. Befo.re long it was ruite• evident the Town for- wards were a weak set in front of goal. Quite erratic And haphazard in their shooting in mid- field they were splendid, but +.1147.re was no "bite" in their attack. Off-side decisions were fre- r,,lentlr given against them. The only decent attempt at goal-getting for some time, was a superb low drive by Aspimill, but Howe fielded it cleverly and coolly. There was never a dull moment from beginning to end, and whilst the pace was at time terrific there was some excel- lent football displayed.</p>
        <p>Both teams were playing a sound game; they played mechanically, the ball flitting from M £l,ll to man with wonderful precision.' The ccles men were not so well balanced as the Town, though at times they made some splendid at- tacks and occasionally had some good oppor- tunities to obtain the lead.</p>
        <p>Within five minutes Aspinall onght to have given his side the lead, for as Siddall switched the ball out to the right, Midge made such an inviting centre that it seemed all the world to the proverbial "pinch o' salt"" on the centre- forward scoring: But he did not quite expect the ball, and as he was about to shoot was brought to the ground by Walker. Again, at the end of 15 minutes' play Siddall- had a chance from Owen, and this time it looked an even bigger certainty, but, alas! he shot and the posts were not "there." About this time a fine piece of work was displayed by Mitchell, the ex-Blackpool _player. He got ,the hdll at the half-way line from a return by Leah; and after some sidestepping past Stephens and Whitfield, dodging the backs, centred, and the opportunity was hashed by a bad shot: Just before the inter- val in f,t rousing assault the Town twice struck the framework of the Borough fortress.</p>
        <p>Restarting, the Town seriously , threatened danger, but there was a perceptible slackening in their efforts, and this gave the -Borough an opportunity of which they availed themselves to the full. They came right into their most dash- ing and vigorous game, sweeping their oppo- nents aside in their cavalry-like charges : This started the vigorous tactics on both sides, which</p>
        <p>threatened to spoil the game, but it was enjoyed; the vigorous "attacks" being made so "neat." On a few occasions a few of the players in- dulged in rather Unpleasant work and had to be cautioned by the referee, who it was satisfactory to see kept a firm grip on the game, and stopped what looked like the beginning of a " fistic " duel.</p>
        <p>The game, glorious as it hut been up to now, was contested with even more keenness, and the combatants fought h.arder for a lead, but all to no purpose, though Howe made a brilliant save from Siddall, but only partly cleared when Sid- dall followed up, and Howe. was slightly in- jured by the "contact." No one could wish to Sep a better game or one fought in a better spirit. The temper of the players throughout was admirable, notwithstanding the terrific pace at which it was fought: One must congratulate the Town on the splendid manner in which they acquitted themselves. As a team they were much more clever than their ojiponents, and they worked with much more whole-heartedness.</p>
        <p>The defence was immense, especially Leah, Millward and Ditchfield. The last-named is essentially a ". worrier "—in fact, would worry a "nest of rats." Kite is a grand goalkeeper, but had not very mach .to do. The forwards played splendidly, but are yet weak in "front." Aspinall and Siddall were very prominent, Illidge and Banner only moderate, but will do better. Perhaps the conditions were against them. The extreme left winger was put out of action in the early part of the game, but man- aged to last the game out, and was not so effec- tive as he might have been if properly fit.</p>
        <p>On the Eccles side, Howe, the ex-Manchester City goalkeeper, played a sound game, and was well covered by a pair of backs who worked hard and played a determined game. Thanks are due to the latter that no goals were scored against their side. Their half-backs were ex- ceedingly good, Walker, the centre-half, in par- ticular. being a "team" inlimself. His passing was magnificent. He is a :valuable asset to the Eccles. Club, and his loss would be keenly felt. Of the forwards, the extreme wingers, Leigh and Mitchell, are "class." They get the ball and they know, exactly what to do with it. The former is the old Aston Villa player, and. the latter is the Blackpool man: Both these men played excellent football, and it was a pity to see their efforts spoiled time after time</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>JUNIOR LEAGUE NOTES.</head>
        <p>JUNIOR LEAGUE NOTES.</p>
        <p>Last week the Howley United, who at present are the leaders of the League, paid- a visit to the Highfield enclosure. There was a good crowd present, and they were hopeful of seeing the home team gain the .first points of the season. The visitors were not at full strength, owing to Walters and Humphries missing their train at Bank Quay, and substitutes, Winstanley and Challinor; were played.. The home team was quite representative. After Fitzgerald ltad gained the lead for the visitors,- the home' team had several good chances to equalise the scores again, but their final efforts had no sting, and were easily cleared. A fine goal by Boyle changed the aspect of the game for the visitors, and at the of time the United were easy winners.</p>
        <p>the home team were best represented by Sad- ler, Dunne, Ford, liankey and Moulsdale. The visitors, although finding the net on five occa- sions, did not show championship form, and played in a too confident manner.</p>
        <p>The third goal, scored by Boyle, was the best goal of the match. He gained possession just inside the visitors' half, and beat two opponents and then shot well out of Sadler's reach. Boyle would greatly improve his play if he would make the best use of his speed.</p>
        <p>Fitzgerald made his first appearance for the Howley Club, the committee securing his trans- fer from the Orford Club during the week. He signalled the occasion by scoring three goals for his new club. On this form he Should prove a good catch for the leaders 'of the League.</p>
        <p>• The m.atch between the Cadets and the Orford. Juniors was not so one-sided as the score 5-2 against the Cadets -would appear to indicate. Buckley was in goal-scoring humour, and on four occasions put the ball past Miller, and Gibson responded twice for the Cadets.</p>
        <p>Buckley is better known as a St. Elphin's player, but he recently transferred his services to the Orford team, and has now played twice for them. He had a big share in the victory obtained by the Orford team on each occasion.</p>
        <p>A very keen game was witnessed between the Recs. and the Adults in their return fixture in the Park, the Recs. holding the lead at the interval. The second portion- was of a ding- dong character, and the scores were equalised, and before the close of play each team .scored again, the points being shared. The goalkeePers and backs were all seen to advantage when playing against the wind, but owing to the con- ditions,of the weather, it was difficult to control the ball and attempt combination.</p>
        <p>The best "match of the day was seen at Steel- street, where St. Elphin's and Penketh were in opposition. There is keen rivalry between the teams, and their games are always strenuously contested. The Saints won by the odd goal in seven, and each team gained the lead in turn. It was owing to the dashing play of the Saints' forwards in the closing stages of the game that the winning goal *as secured.</p>
        <p>Jones, in goal for the Saints, did all that was required of him in a very 'satisfactory manner, and Tun Stall and Furness showed good judgment in their defensive play.</p>
        <p>• Watson was the best 9f the half-back line. A. Jones, England and Lee were the most promi- nent forwards, the latter giving much satisfac- tion on his first appearance for the Saints.</p>
        <p>For the visitors, Thomasson was the better of the backs, and Sankey the pick of the halves, and Glover and Pearson were seen .to the best advantage amongst the forwards.</p>
        <p>This week the new members of the League are all engaged in the. Competition, and their per- formances will be keenly watched.</p>
        <p>There should be a keen struggle on the High. field Juniors' enclosure between them and the Cadets. Neither have yet scored any points, and both teams are anxious to get from the bottom of the League table.</p>
        <p>' Buckley, who has been transferred• from the 'Elphin's, and Fitzgerald, who got his transfer from Orford Juniors, are displaying good form and scoring goals for their new clubs.</p>
        <p>Last week Buckley scored four goals against the Cadets.</p>
        <p>Fitzgerald scored three goals against High- field. -</p>
        <p>Boyle scored the last goal of the match, Ether giving a poor display in the first half.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. F. A. P. Howley United St. Elphin's • 5 4 1 0 19 8 8 Penketh Juniors Orford Juniors Latchford Recs. Bewsey A.S Highfield Juniors Latchford Cadets</p>
        <p>ST: ELPHIN'S V. PENKETH JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>On the Steel-street ground</p>
        <p>St. Elphin's:V. Jones; Tunstall, Furness; Wat- son, Miller, Gleave; Chisholm, England, A. Jones, Lee and Holden.</p>
        <p>Penketh: J. Atkinson; White, Thomasson; Swift, Sankey, Boyle; Magee, Hughes, Glover, Pearson and Winstanley. Referee, Mr. H. Berry. _</p>
        <p>The visitors, aided by A strong wind, made the first attack, which was repulsed.; then they' gained a free kick, from which White opened the score. The home forwards combined better, and Lee placed the ball grandly, for Watson to equalise with a splendid shot. The Saints continued to show the best judgment, and England increased ,their lead. Just after this A. Jones got to close quarters with the visitors' backs, snd was brought dnwn insidp the penalty area. Miller was entrusted with the</p>
      </div>
