      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 14, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 14, 1916.</p>
        <p>Motor Vehicles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>PRIVATE tILI ES' the.m.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE tILI ES' the.m.</p>
        <p>Dramatic Story Of Disobedience To Offers Coaintands.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>PLEADS GUILTY AT COURT-MARTIAL.</head>
        <p>PLEADS GUILTY AT COURT-MARTIAL.</p>
        <p>A remarkable statement concerning his ex- periences since he left Warrington under mili- tary escort on Friday, August 18th,• waA told bY Private Charles Dukes, ex-secretary of the Warrington Trades Council, at a District Court. Martial—a D.C.M.—held .at Birkenhead on Thursday and yesterday. With Private Dukes were Private Stanley Billingsbrough Bodle, of The OaklandS, Hunt's- lane, Latchford Without, and five other con- scientious objectors, who were all found guilty of disobeying their superior officers. 'The punishment decided upon will be an- nounced later. •</p>
        <p>The writing room of a soldiers' recreation hut was transformed into a Court, and throughout Thursday Private Dukes stood at ease in the ante-room with other conscientious objectors and an escort, and was allowed to converse with with' ,his friends, including Mi. A. E. Seabury. As each prisoner's trial concluded their number gradually dwindled until only Privates Dukes and George Benson (the latter .of Stockport), remained in view. Their cases not having been reached on the rising of the Court at about six o'clock, they were then marched off to join their fellows in the guard room until yesterday morning, when they were aw.-.7.n marched into position near the Court, Dukes' case being dealt with first. PROTESTS FROM PRISONERS.</p>
        <p>Incidents in Thursday's proceedings must be recorded first, howeVer. The Court consisted of Major W. B. Beamish, 3rd Garrison Battalion Royal Welsh Fusiliers (president, Captain A. Davidson, 3rd South Lancs. Regiment, and Captain P. G. Villiers-Stuart (Military Cross). Privates Dukes, Beardsworth, Benson and Hopkins all objected to Captain Villiers-Stuart being a member • of the Court on the ground that he belonged to the same regiment, and as liable to be prejudiced. Benson added that his objection was a stronger ime, ;is he had been in the company which Captain Villiers-Stuart commanded, and the latter refused to " crime " him (Benson) for an insubordination. This had been the general policy of the Cheshire Regi- ment with conscientious objectors. "Acting on behalf of Dukes, Benson and Beardsworth," broke in Mr. S. R. Dodds, soliei_ tor, of Liverpool, "I may say that it comes as a considerable surprise to us that one of the members of the Court is an officer of the 3rl Cheshire Regiment. This matter will have to be, taken to another Court, because there are certain points in it which may be considered, irrelevant now. I, .as their legal adviser, can only go on under strong protest. I suggest that a fresh Court be convened for the trial of these men.</p>
        <p>The President: An officer is hot bound to bd of another regiment. Mr. Dodds: I don't deny that, but under tha particular circumstances it would be irregular. There is a movement for an</p>
        <p>in which this particular officer may be a witness.</p>
        <p>Captain Davidson: This man is not the com- pany officer of the man.</p>
        <p>_ _ Mr. Dodds: He does know something about the case. He has been brought into touch with it. The President: He is only one man, and he has not investigated the case. It is not as if he had. Mr. Dodds: It is not as if these were usual cases. I am not suggesting that he would allow the circumstances to prejudice his mind, but there is the point that these cases may have to gp to another Court. Privates Dukes, Hopkins, Benson and Beards- worth were then marched out, the othef three, including Private Bodle, who did not object, remaining.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dodds inquired for the decision of the Court regarding his objection. The President: We are not taking them now. Mr. Dodds: I am sorry to be so personal, but I want to make arrangements for other work if they are not coming on yet. _</p>
        <p>The President: When we come to them we will investigate the cases. Mr. Dodds: I should like to know whether our objection is upheld. The President: Your cases will not be dealt with before this afternoon, at any rate.</p>
        <p>In the afternoon it was announced that the objection to the constitution of the Court had been over-ruled, except in the case of Benson. Captain MeHinson, of the same regiment, but of another cempany, would take Captain Villiers- Stuart's place.</p>
        <p>the Tuesday he stayed in the barrack room when parade was sounded and the orderly cor- poral came in and forted .him out. When placed in the ranks he (Dukes) shouted, "I refuse to obey orders," and he was told to stand out. The officer isaid, "It is all right. You continue to refuse. I do not care a Continental so long as you know what you .are doing." :lle (Dukes) then asked whether he was notzmtitled to a court-martial for refusing, and he got the reply, "You have no right at all under military law." On being ordered to fall in to march to the park, he refused, and two N.C.O.'s took hold of each arm and he was marched to the park with his arms behind him. He resisted as hard as he could, and was not particularly hurt. When in the park he refused to drill, and was handed over to another N.C.0., who told two men to stand behind and see that be (Dukes) obeyed orders. The N.C.O. II marked, "If he does not move kick his feet." That was done in order to make him stand at ease and then at attention. He continued to resist, and another man was called. Two held his .arms, and the other held his clothes between the shoulders. On being told to " double " the one behind put his head in his (Dukes') back and he was run about in the, park in that fashion. At the end of every five minutes the men were ordered to bang him on to the ground, and one man sat on his legs while the others raised his body, bending it forward and _forcing his head down. Afterwards they held his head down and doubled his legs over from the hips several times.</p>
        <p>dry y aS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>DUKES PLEADS GUILTY.</head>
        <p>DUKES PLEADS GUILTY.</p>
        <p>The Court having assembled yesterday morn- ing, Dukes' name was called, and from the outer room could be heard the command of the Sergeant to Dukes and the.escort: " 'Tention— Left turn !—Quick march !—Left wheel !—(into the room, Charles being revealed in khaki; cap- less, and head unadorned by the glossy dark waves of hair of former days)—Halt!—Right turn!"</p>
        <p>And there he stood before his judges. He immediately pleaded guilty to a, charge of fail- ing to appear on the place of parade on the 19th September; and then stood "Easy," as did his escort. His protest against Captain Villiers- Stuart being a member ow the Court was noted by the President. The evidence of the prosecution was then given. Second-Lieutenant Tommis stating that he told Dukes the contents of the battalion orders, detailing that Dukes' battalion was to parade for a route march, and he must attend. Acting-Sergeant John Jones deposed that Dukes was absent from the parade and, on *rid- ing him in the billet, he (witness) ordered him to the guard room.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>HIS ALLEGATIONS.</head>
        <p>HIS ALLEGATIONS.</p>
        <p>Private Dukes then read a long and remark- able statement in mitigation of his offence, as his solicitor said. In the course of this lirivate Dukes said that on arrival at Chester with his escort on August 18th, he was asked by a Ser- geant to sign attestation papers, but he refused. The first night he shared a plank bed with seven others. Two were drunk and vomiting. Later, when he was asked if he desired to make any allotment, he replied that he did not in- tend to' soldier; and, therefore; the necessity did not arise. He refused to take the money accepted by other recruits, in that case, 38. 3d. He then returned to the guard room and on the Saturday afternoon was taken to the stores and served with a .pair of boots and shirts. . On being asked to sign for them he refused. The sergeant in charge of recruits said to the cor- poral.in charge of the stores, "You know what to do. Sign for him." That the corporal did. On leaving the stores he refused to carry the kit, and another sergeant came up and swore at him. He was then asked to remove his coat, but refused, and it was forcibly removed by two privates on an order from the sergeant, and an Army coat was put on. "My resistance to this was more than technical," he com- mented. He was asked several times to put on the clothes, but refused and, in the cells at. tached to the guard room, the sergeant ordered the men to force him into uniform. They be- gan by removing his waistcoat and his collar and tie. He resisted up to that point, and afterwards stripped, voluntarily, as resistance was useless. During the morning a com- missioned officer came into the guard room and they were marched into an adjoining room. He (Dukes) had his hands in his pockets and was ordered to stand at attention. but he refused. The officer then ordered two soldiers to take his hands from his (Dukes') pockets and force him to attention. That they did. The officer swore at him and •told the sergeant to take him to the guard room.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>"HAIR TORN OFF."</head>
        <p>"HAIR TORN OFF."</p>
        <p>Soon after arriving at Birkenhead on tha following Monday he was told that he had got to have his hair cut. On objecting, he was taken hold of by four men and put astride a form. The barber was called and the orderly corporal took the clippers and tore off his (Dukes') hair at the back with such force that the clippers were put out of gear. The barber then put them right and completed the job. He was abused by pretty nearly every non- commissioned officer" in the camp. They re- marked, "We can tame Army. We have already broken in others." On</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>FELL AT THE WATER JUMP.</head>
        <p>FELL AT THE WATER JUMP.</p>
        <p>I Continuing, Dukes said he was next taken to a horizontal scaffold pole, three feet high, and, because he would not jump over it, was thrown over twice. He was then run to th,b, water jump and, owing to being pushed from behind, was compelled to jump to avoid falling in. As he went an attempt was made to kick his feet from under him as he jumped, and on the third occasion they succeeded and he fell into the water. Afterwards, he was again thrown to the ground and a senior officer came and asked him why he refused to drill, and he replied it was because of being a conscientious objector. He also said he wanted a .court- martial, but the officer rejoined that they could break his (Dukes') resistance down. The doubling process was then repeated, and he was thrown to the ground. Two or three officers gathered round and he asked for a drink, whereupon one said, "He is only shamming. Give him some more." The treatment was 're- peated for some time, and he became semi- conscious. He was given ten minutes to con- sider his position and, on again telling them that he refused to drill, the treatment was re- commenced. He refused a week-end leave be- cause he declined to go home in khaki, and also because it would have been dishonest of him to accept the railway pass or any privilege when he did not intend to render any service. Following his ill-treatment on August 22nd he I drilled under threat of physical violence. He did that because he was repeatedly warned that I unless he drilled the abuse would continue and become more severe. He always refused to accept any pay. Instead of going on parade on Tuesday, the 19th ult. he sat on his cot until noon, when he was placed under arrest and put into the guard room. He had been there ever since. He had always said he did not in- tend soldiering, and did not intend to do so. In an eloquent address, Mr. Dodds, Dukes' solicitor, said it would be a mistaken act of kindness for the Court to pass only a light sen- tence, for it would only mean that holding the views he did he would have to face the ordeal over again. Dukes wished to be placed in such a position that the War Office could deal with his case as they had those of other conscientious objectors. He would then have a chance of being allotted to work of national importance under civil control when the War Office had dealt with his case.</p>
        <p>Having been found guilty, Dukes was ordered away to the guard room. He gave au revoir handshakes to his friends as he went.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE BOOLE'S LIFE OF CONSECRATION.</p>
        <p>Bocile's case was heard on Thursday. When charged with disobeying his superior officer by failing to fall in with a squad of recruits when ordered to do so, he replied, "Legally, I am guilty."</p>
        <p>_ _ The evidence of Captain Cotton, the adjutant, was that he saw the accused and personally warned him of the serious consequences in- volved by any disobedience, and asked him whether he fully realised what those conse- quences would be. In reply, the accused said "Yes."</p>
        <p>Acting-Company-Sergeant-Major Arnott de- posed that shortly after ten o'clock on the morn- ing of the 2:ith September' he ordered Bodle to fall in with a squad of recruits, but he refused to do so, saying, "I am very sorry, but I can- not do it." Witness then gave him a direct order to fall in, and he again said, "I am very sorry, but I cannot do it." The accused re- mained standing, and made no attempt to move. Witness then ordered that he should be taken to the guard room, and he was taken thither. '</p>
        <p>The President asked whether Bodle had any- thing to say in mitigation of punishment, and he replied that he had. Bodle then read a state- ment, which he afterwards handed, in to the Court. In it he stated that he pleaded guilty to the charge. Being a. conscientious objector on religious grounds, he could not allow himself to become a soldier, and, therefore, was in duty bound to disobey all military orders. He was arrested at Latchford Without against his will, and had signed no papers. Having been brought up from childhood in the International Bible Students' Association, he was pledged to conse- crate his life to God. The whole teaching of Jesus Christ was in direct opposition to warfare, and he intended to remain true to his principles at all costs. It would be impossible for him to be a faithful follower of the Prince of Peace and be a, soldier also. He had done all within his power to attain the exemption to Which he was legally entitled by appealing to the Local and Appeal Tribunals, but met with an unjust judgment. He had previously stated his will- ingness to engage in work of national import- ance. His desire was to be a law-abiding citizen like his Master was. He did not want to be a nuisancei but preferred that his energies should be used to the public good. The President: How long have you been with the International Bible Students' Association?— All tov life.,</p>
        <p>Do you wish to give ev;finw." yourself or to call witnesses as to your character?—l have no witnesses here, but I have about twenty letters at home; these testify to my character from childhood.</p>
        <p>It was stated that Bodle had been in military custody for eighteen days. There was a convic- tion against him by a Civil Court for failing to report himself for service. The Court then considered their decision in private, and, without announcing the sentence (they found him "guilty ") ordered Bodle to be taken to the guard room. Soon after he had gone the youth's mother presented herself at the Barracks, to find to her great disappoint- ment that her son's case had been dealt with. She went away regretfully.</p>
        <p>- --- - THE LATCIWORD CADET CORPS.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening a very enjoyable hot- pot supper and social was .held in the Cairo. street Schools by the members of the above carps. A short musical programme was pre- sented. Mr. W. Tynan gave.a fine rendering of "Bonnie Mary of 4.rgyle," and Lieut. H. Kitchen played some very pleasing pianoforte solos. Mr. Gordon B. Starkey gave two humorous sketches, which were loudly ap- plauded, "The 11.69 Express" proving the favourite. Miss G. Starkey was an able accom- panist; her greatest success was a selection of the favourite songs, the boys joining heartily in the choruses. Various games were indulged in.</p>
        <p>ORDERS.</p>
        <p>By J. B. Starkey, Commanding Officer.</p>
        <p>Parades.—Friday.- Fall in at 7.3-0 prompt. Orderlies.:-Corporal W. Rigby and Cadet J. Higham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>OUR CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>OUR CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Should Boys Have Men's Wages?</p>
        <p>EDUCATION REVOLUTION?</p>
        <p>Greatly augmenting the members of ' the Literary and Philosophical Society at their inaugural meeting of the session in. the Town Hall on Tuesday evening, was an array of teachers and members of the Education Com- mittee assembled to hear one of England's fore- most educationists, Professor J. J. Findlay (Professor of Education, Manchester Univer- sity) open a discussion on " Radieal reform in school practice." Dr. Naden, the president of the society, occupied the chair. , CHANGES PAST AND TO COME.</p>
        <p>The Professor was, of opinion that between the ages of nine and 13 years every boy and girl wanted to work and should work. The trouble was that in the past the adult had exploited the idea too much, made them work too inordinately, and not for the joy of work, but for wages. On the other hand schools had tended to take them away from work. At that age children were interested in doing things rather than in learning from books. "Am I talking heresy?" inquired the Professor amid the laughter which followed astonishment. The garden, workshop, the needle, brush—the doing of things of a prac- tical nature—were the things congenial to children's development between the ages of nine and 13 years. The teacher for those years should be the craftsman rather than the scholar. It was a finer thing for that teacher to be an artist or craftsman than learned in mathematics or science. He (the Professor) was not advocating the half-time system. Ile did not want to take the children away from public control and hand them over to industry at that age, but he was sure that if they re- fused to let children be engaged in some useful and practical employment for a part of their day they were doing them a wrong. Later in his address, the Professor, speaking of freedom without discipline as applied to the child, asked whether it was right for a boy of 14 or 15 years to earn £2 a week as .some were doing, and be economically inde- pendent? Was it fair to the boy himself? No: it was not right to say to him, "Go and be a wage-earner at a high rate and if you lose your balance there are the police waiting for you." (Applause.) The control of boys be- tween 14 and 18 years was necessary. They must attend continuation schools. The reason they had let go control of adolescents was be- cause the people found they could make money out of them. There must, however, be a re- assertion of that control. Right from the earliest times until the industrial revolution adolescents had never been allowed so much so- called liberty as now.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>DISCUSSION.</head>
        <p>THE DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>An interesting discussion followed. We have, however, only space to give the points of the speeches, including those in a letter from Mr. Harold Smith, M.P.: I hope we shall learn to imbue our boys find girls with a greater knowledge of, and, consequently, a greater pride in, our great Empire. We must instil into them the great traditions of a great people, and we must try to open their eyes to the small weaknesses in our character and habits, which this war has, I think, brought to light. We mu4st try to cease being quite so insular, and must awaken to the competitive power.of the great races of the world. Further, we should, I think, try to increase the earning capacity of our children. If our boys are going into business, into a shop or into a trade, can- not we teach them a little more which will use- fully prepare them therefor? In short, young Briton must more and more realise the Imperial and commercial value of citizenship. Individuality.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Roberts (chairman of the Educa- tion Committee): If we are going to make the best of the child's individualities it is going fo cost the ratepayers a Dickens of a lot of money, but I hope we shall face the music in a reason- able fashion. This war has shown up a good many of our deficiencies. It is true that we are not a disciplined people. We have had an ex- ample of that carried to extremes in Germany. There is a happy medium at which we should aim. There is a danger of the commercial ex- ploitation of child labour, and it makes one hesitate .before consenting to anything which might be used in that direction, yet we might have schools for training in general principles. Mr. A. J. Jolley (vice-chairman of the Educa- tion Committee): One great need is that the leaving school age should be highered. If that is not done by local authorities, Parliament should step in and compel it. Canon Morley Stevenson: What we must aim at is the equipoise between freedom and dis- cipline for the children. The desirability of keeping control over the young until 18 years old we should all admit, but the practical work- ing out of the scheme has yet to be done. Alderman Bennett: I have the grtatest possible admiration fer the teaching profession in War- rington, yet we do not get the results we should like. I doubt whether the curriculum is satis- factory. The' children are abysmally ignorant of literature and know about as much physiology as myself. Last week I had a pain and did not know whether it was lumbago or a chill on the liver. Picture Shows in Schools.</p>
        <p>Mr. Holman Kingdon : The picture show might be a great factor in the education of children, but it is not. I believe that no child at an elementary school should go into a picture show. If you debar them I would support the introduction of the cinema into the school. The way the cinema is done now is a prostitution of its possibilities. Mr. J. Moore Murray (Director of Education): I am not sure that the revolution proposed generally in education is going to be built on a right foundation, that the object is not just pounds, shillings and pence. In the last few years there has been a tremendous advance in ourt schools and schooling. They are not the failure that certain critics make out. The immediate necessities of education are smaller classes, higher leaving age and more money.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>STAGE AND KINEMA.</head>
        <p>STAGE AND KINEMA.</p>
        <p>Royal Court Theatre. When I went to see Mr. Edmund Kennedy, who had .just concluded a brilliant rendering of Dr. Napier in "The Case of Lady Camber," now being presented at the Royal Court Theatre, he was plackag his •ini maculate. morn- ing coat on the hanger. I flung a compliment. at him.- " Fine I" I exclaimed. "What, the coat?" he asked..</p>
        <p>That was his wit and modesty. As an actor with the iustinct of an artist, Mr. Kennedy is highly esteemed. In Vachell's "'The Case of Lady Camber," he has splendid opportunities, and makes the most of them. He did not entrain as the doctor without seriOus study and observation: Iris father-in-law and two brothers-in-law happen to be in the medical pro- fession, and besides taking open-eydd walks along Harley-street, he studied them for exter- nals. He has succeeded: he is the ideal Harley- street specialist.</p>
        <p>Mr. Kennedy has had a varied career, usually attended with Feat success. Looking at him as a .doctor, one would not. think of him as having 'been an office boy in Birmingham, but not until he entered the theatrical profession did he become comfortable; and it is rather singular that he should start as a local acting- manager at Camberwell. Thence he went to the Prince's Theatre, Manchester, also as acting- manager</p>
        <p>His career, as stated, has been varied. At the beginning of the year he had a short spell in Robert Courtneidge's "Light Blues," a famous musical comedy, in which he had a speaking part. His singing abilities—he was a sol) chorister at a Birmingham Church when a boy —were nct called into requisition. Mr. Kennedy never rests. He has acted in all sorts of plays in many character parts. The lot of the barrister is the nearest to that he enacts at the Royal Court Theatre this week. When be went to America in 1914 with "Change," he wa, the only Englishman in the charming Welsh piece; moreover, his part was that of a low Cockney. Who would have believed it? On his first visit to America in 1908, he went with his brother's play, "The Servant in the House."</p>
        <p>Mr. Kennedy has been to the Royal Court several times before, during a continuous run of - five years in Wilson Barrett's "Lucky Durham."</p>
        <p>I may say that the whole company is a first- class one. Mr. Bernard Lopping (Lord Camber) and Miss .Dorothy Dewhurst (Miss Esther Yorke) are married. Miss Dewhurst will be remembered for her splendid acting in "Hindle Wakes." •</p>
        <p>Patrons of the theatre are ,promised a rare treat next week, when that extraordinarily suc- cessful drama, "The Silver King," will occupy the boards at whole-night performances. No ingenuity could reduce this fine production into a " twice-nighter," and it is pleasing to note that the play is to be presented in its entirety. Mr. C. W. Somerset appears in the title part. This notable drama has now been before the public for a quarter of a century, and its fascinating power is still undiminished.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-10-14_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>Palace and Hippodrome.</head>
        <p>Palace and Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>Like the poet, a ventriloquist is born not made, and •so with. Tom Franks, who has amused audiences at the Hippodrome during the week in his exceptionally clever, humorous and up-to-date ventriloquial act.</p>
        <p>Mr. Franks early discovered his natural gift, and after careful training and persistence de- veloped his talent in this direction, with the result that he now occupies a leading position in his most enjoyable class of entertainment.</p>
        <p>He soon discarded the ancient fakes of the older generation of ventriloquists, and found that a single mannikin skilfully handled gave greater facility for a really original and catch- ing piece of patter work, and better oppor- tunity for a display of ingenious vocal effort.</p>
        <p>As he went on he developed the mechanical possibilities of his puppet, with the result that now " Billy " walks on to the stage with his master—gives back answers in daring form— works his mouth, eyelids, arms and legs, and even elongates his neck. Furthermore, at One stage of the programme he gives his employer a severe pummelling and lands him a knock down on the point. The turn is vastly amus- ing, and although, for some time at least Tom Franks has not appeared before all the crowned heads of Europe, he has made hosts of admirers in every place of notability in the kingdom, not excepting Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Four Holloways give a marvellous exhi- bition of skill on the tight wire as trick cyclists and comedy acrobats. The Brothers St. John supply a clever instrumental and patter act, and the Sisters Brookland are charming comediennes, whose dancing is most attractive.</p>
        <p>"A Girl in Every Port" is a farce in which three young persons have a battle royal for the possession of a certain member of His Majesty's Navy whose predilection for the fairer sex gets him into rather warm quarters. The farce was well produced by Charles H. Hughes, Marie Sadie, Marie Lyndon and Jessica Black.</p>
        <p>Mdlle Cameo presents a beautiful series nf living marble statue studies. Her appearance in the classical pose of the "Venus of Milo" was a fine artistic study, assisted by a remarkably clever arrangement of the back curtain.</p>
        <p>A popular programme in every respect is an- nounced for next week at the Hippodrome. Fyne and Hurley in a burlesque, "Oliver Twist Twisted," will secure many laughs, and War- wick and Ciss appear in a similar class of enter- tainment. Harry Brown, America's popular comedian, is well known in the halls, and Mr. and Miss De Brean, ' fancy Japanese artistes, introduce a novel turn. Billy Howard and Kathryn Harris are bound to claim applause in "The Adventures of a Happy Tramp"; while Pauline Travis, male impersonator; Edwin Meier, reciter; and The Doves, sensational gym- nasts, complete an excellent bill.</p>
        <p>The Kinemas. "The Spider," a fine drama featuring Pauline Frederick, is at present drawing crowded houses to the Star Kinema, Church-street. Next week we are to have a great Triangle feature, ".Let Katie Do It," during the first half of the week, and the magnificent play, -"" The Butter- fly on the Wheel," during the latter half.</p>
        <p>At the Grand Electric Theatre, Bridge Foot, a Famous Players Company production, "Prince and Pauper," now occupies the screen, along with the second episode of "Peg o' the Ring." For the first half of next week "Working It Out" and the twentieth episode of "The Dia- mond from the Sky". will be the " star " pic- tures, and during the latter half a fine metro drama, "Soul Market," and the third episode of "Peg o' the Ring."</p>
        <p>That great war film, "The Battle of the Somme," is now being screened at the Pavilion, Lovely-lane, and the first episode of "Peg o' the Ring." "The Price of Her Silence," a fine exclusive. picture, and the twentieth episode of "The Diamond from the Sky" will be shown during the first half of next week, and an excit- ing detective drama, " Ultus—the man from the dead," during the latter half, along with the secbnd episode of "Peg o' the Ring."</p>
        <p>FOYER.</p>
        <p>Death Of " Owd Toss."</p>
        <p>One of the best-known professional bowlers in the North of England in the person of Mr. William Taylor, familiarly known as ". Owd Toss," has died at the age of 73 at Little Hul- ton. 'Whilst playing in the Talbot Tournament he caught a chill, which was followed by pneu- monia. Three weeks ago Mr. Taylor took part in an extremely interesting bowling match be- tween Lancashire professionals and Warrington amateurs, on the Rylands's Recreation Ground, when', after an exciting game he was beaten 21-19 by G. Ball, sen., after holding the green for fifteen minutes. Ten years ago he beat Gerard Hart 21-20 in a championship at Blackpool.</p>
      </div>
