        <head>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED</head>
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED</p>
        <p>YOUNG DOMESTIC SERVANT</p>
        <p>COMMITS SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>On Friday of last week about 8.30 p.m., wh,il6 a young man named John Mousdale was vfe-LD- ing along the road at Stanney Lunt Bridger over the Bridgewater Canal at Grappenhall,r heard moans coming from the water-- looked, but could not tsee anything at first the moonlight was very faint. He went do'it for a closer view and then saw the top ofulle woman's head and her arms moving as if s",t were struggling in the water. She was rigi'id in the .middle of the canal, and, as he Oil' not swim, he ran for help. When he returne3 with two men the woman had disappeared. Tire police were informed, and Police Constab Kelford, having got assistance and the use of 2: boat, dragged the canal with boathooks. body wal recovered after a half hour's search,. Mr. H. C. Yates held an inquest on Monaa' in the Large Room, Grappenhall, on the bodY.:3 which was that of a young woman named 1311 , Summerfield, otherwise Sumner, a doines'ijitl: servant, who was in her nineteenth year. .7 verdict of "Suicide whilst temporarily insane. was returned. Mrs. Eleanor Rogers, of "Warrington-road' Bartington, said she knew that she was courting_ a young -man who was now in France, but the?, were not engaged. "I am afraid it -was tbici way," she added, "he did not want lier,al she wanted him. I think he told her he dia '- want to marry her." Mrs. Gibbs, who resides at Rosedale. La 11- tc d ford Without, stated that the dead girl 11% been. ra iservantt for mother's iother'eeven slservicueloils:o; She had been an excellent servant, and her she cheerful. They liked her immensely- 321er she liked them. She got a letter from ps adopted mother telling her to give a weerr,r notice as another place had been found her.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Yes, ctit and dried. id Witness further told the jury that she sa if she (Lid not leave there would be. troubll at home, and added that her sister soid„ir would be .better to give notice, otherwise '-e . , mother was a hard woman end would On% with a horse and cart to fetch her. She wa very miserable after getting the letter. In summing up, Mr. Yates tle,, the woman that wrote that letter should o: ashamed of herself to write that waY to 911. girl. ..</p>
