        <p>would be of no use if they did, and I wanted to disprove this. ' My experience of farm life leads me to believe (1) that much, even if not all, farm work can be done by women, and that they can be trusted to go on doing even a monotonous task con- scientiously without supervision; (2) that farm life is not suitable for young children, and that it is in the interests of the nation to keep them at school; (3) that educated women are at no disadvantage. If taught to use their tools oroperly, so as to produce the greatest effect vith the smallest effort, they will soon achieve -ood results, and the intelligent interest they take in their work will enable them to appreci- ate the use and value of tasks which to others might appear needless and monotonous.</p>
