        <p>RIFLEMAN THOMAS DICKENSON. Killed.</p>
        <p>the War Office has informed his mother, who resides at 77, Haydock-street, that they have been regretfully constrained to conclude that he met his death on that date or since." Rifleman Dickenson, who was twenty-three years of age, had been in the Army two years, and at the Front 'about sixteen months. He was formerly employed at Dailam Forge. PRIVATE S. LEIGH. • Buried in Little Cemetery Just Behind the Lines. Mrs. Leigh, who resides with her two little children at 57, Birehall-street, has received offi- cial news of the death of her husband, Private Samuel Leigh, South Lancashire Regiment. He was killed on September 16th. A letter has</p>
        <p>f PRIVATE S. LEIGH. Killed.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE A. HITCHINSON. One Brother Killed And Another Wounded. Missing since an engagement in Gallipoli on August 10th last year, Private Arthur Hollins Hitchinson, of the South Lancs. Regiment, son of Mr. and Mrs. F. Hitchinson, 67, Priestley- street, is now officially reported to have met his death on that date. Private Hitchinson, • who was formerly employed as a galvaniser by Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd., was educated at the Heathside and Factory Yard Schools. He enlisted soon after the outbreak of war, and was twenty years of age. There are two other brothers serving in His Majesty's Forces, Seaman Herbert Hitchin- son, seventeen years of age, has been in the Navy about six months, and Private Edward Hitchinson, of the Machine Gun Corps, who</p>
