        <p>was formerly employed by the Whitecross Co., Ltd. PRIVATE J. GUEST. " A Gallant Son," Says Officer In Letter. Mr. and Mrs. Guest, 26, Kerfoot-street, have received a letter intimating to them the news of the death in action of their son, Private John Guest, South Lancs. Regiment. The letter is from Lieutenant G. H. Linden:— "On behalf of Myself and the other officers or B Company I wish to offer you our deepest sympathy in the sad loss of your gallant son. He was a brave soldier, and while you grieve over him you Must be proud of him, for he gave his life for his country. . . I hope this</p>
        <p>• • %o:ttlistl.4.teAt.•• • -.• • • ,e4c. ~.• .•</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. GUEST. Killed.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE A. CLARE. Wounded &amp; Missing A Month Ago; Now Reported Killed. Reported as wounded and missing about a month ago, Private Alfred Clare, South Lancs. Regiment, is now stated to have been killed. His wife, who resides at 39, Wellington-street, has ieceived intimation to this effect. Private Clare enlisted in November, 1914, and had been at the Front for fifteen months. He was formerly employed as a bricksetter's labourer by Mr. Daniel Cooper, Alexandra- street, and was educated at the National Schools, Church-street. He leaves a widow and five children. RIFLEMAN S. ROSE. Two Brothers Killed And Another Wounded. , "Whilst in charge of a burying party in the trenches I came across the body of Rifleman S. Rose, and in the execution of my duty I took his papers and identity disc from him and buried him decently. . . I found him on the 22nd September and I should say he had been killed about six or ten days. Death must have been instantaneous, due to a bulleethrough the head.... You and your dear children have my deepest sympathy." So wrote Corporal S. Ward from a hospital at Norwich, where he had been admitted wounded, to Mrs. Rose, 14, Clegg-street, inform- ing her of the fate of her husband, Rifleman Samuel Rose, of the King's Royal Rifles. Rifleman Rose, who was thirty-one years of age, and an "old boy" of St. Ann's School, was formerly employed as a furnaceman by Messrs. Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd. He joined the Army two years ago last September, and had been at the Front fifteen months. He attended St. Ann's Church, and was the father of two children. An excep- tionally good billiard player, he had won many</p>
