        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL S. MOULD. Prisoner of War.</p>
        <p>coffee, minus sugar, and at half-past five at night we get barley water. . . . I will be glad when the parcels start coming." • • On a card, receited previous to this letter, Lance-Corporal Mould was wounded in the back. Lance-Corporal Mould, who is eighteen years of age, enlisted in June last year, and has been at the Front three Months. He was educated at Beamont School, attended Elmwood-Avenue Sunday school, and was formerly employed at Bradley's, clothiers, Bridge-street. His •brother David is with the Royal Garrison Artillery, having been called up on Saturday, after having worked on munitions in town.</p>
        <p>BRONCHIAL COUGH. Mother and Baby both Cured by Veno's Lightning Cough Cure. Mrs. Russell, 1, Osset-road, Grays, Essex, says: "I first used Veno's Lightning Cough Cure for my little baby when he was suffering with severe bronchial cough. Other things had failed, but Veno's reli6red at once, and soon baby was as well as ever. Since then I have taken Veno's myself, and have found it splendid for a very troublesome cough !" Trust Veno's to cure coughs, colds, asthma, bronchitis, influenza, catarrh, and whooping cough. Prices 111 d., Is. 3d., and 3s. everywhere. A Difficelt Case. Physician (to wife, upon receiving invitation to join three fellow practitioners in a rubber of bridge) : "Here I am, dear, called away again. Appears to be a difficult case, too. There are three other doctors on the spot already."—" Punch."</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH. Let Not Your Light So Shine.—At Daresbury Sessions, held on Tuesday at Stockton Heath. six or seven residents in Stockton Heath ind Grappenhall vicinity were prosecuted by the police for not having lights in their dwellings properly shaded. In a couple of cases the de- fendants had light-coloured window blinds, which failed to reduce the glare. Fines of ten shillings were imposed. CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT. Stockton Heath Detachment. Orders for week commencing 15th October :-- Sunday.=N.C.O.'s Class, Vhelford. Meet Victoria-square, 9 a.m. Monday.—Company and Recruit Drill, Head- quarters, 7.45. Wednesday.—N.C.O.'s Meeting, Mulberry Tree, 8 o'clock. Thursday.—Recruits' Drill, Headquarters, 7.15 Friday.—Class Firing, Drill Hall, 7 o'clock. Sunday, 22nd.—Route March. Fall' in, Vic- toria-square, 9.45 a.m. One ration to 'be carried in haversack, and great coats rolled. Dismiss 5.45 p.m. Saturday (to-day).—Shooting Competition, Appleton Rifle, Range, 2 o'clock. FRANK WARBURTON, Officer Commanding. PRIVATE H. WEBSTER. Missing Twelve Months; Now Reported Killed. Missing since September 25th last year, Pte. 11. Webster, of the South Lancashire Regiment, son of Mr. and Mrs. William Webster, 20, Mitchell-street, Stockton Heath, is now officially reported to have met his death on that date or since. Private Webster joined the South Lancashire Regiment in September, 1914. He was engaged as a bomb-thrower, and previous to enlisting was employed by Mr. John Taylor, greengrocer, Walton-road, Stockton Heath. Had he lived he would have celebrated his 24th birthday this month. A member of the Stockton Heath R.F.C., he showed considerable ability in this particular class of sport. He nttended St.</p>
