        <p>kick, but shot Straight at the goalkeeper, and the ball was got away. The visitors now improved and good play by Glover enabled them to make an attack on the home goal, and Winstanley made the scores equal. Even 'play followed up to the interval, the • scores being: Elphin'i, 2 goals; Penketh, 2 goals. • On.resuming the Saints played strongly, and Lee and Jones were very prominent, but failed to pierce the, visitors' defence. From a breakaway Sankey gained the lead for the visitors. The Saints now played keenly, and with only ten minutes to go Lee equalised for the Saints. The game was how strenu- ously contested, Jones and England being very prominent for the Saints, and just before the close of play Lee gained the lead from close quarters. Result: St. Elphin's, 4 goals; Penketh, 3 goals. HICHFIELD v. HOWLEY. The teams representing the above clubs were as follows: Highfield: Sadler; Dunne, Bell; Barnea, Jepson, Wishlade; Tomlinson, Ford, Hankey, Moulsdale and Peattee. Howley: Winstanley ; Rowlinson, Foster; Hind, Hoarty; Boyle, Fitzgerald, Davies, Wood- house and Challinor. The home team made the first advance and Win- stanlerhad to kick out. The visitors retaliated, and forced a corner kick, from which Fitzgerald shot on the wrong side of the uprights. The home team gained ground by the aid of a free kick and attacked strongly, and a good long shot was put in by Tom- linson, and Foster, in attempting to clear, put the ball through his own goal, thus opening the score for the home team. The visitors replied strongly, in midfield, but their final shots were weak. The home team tried hard to break through, but failed Fitzgerald equalised for the United with a shlt that beat Sadler' all the way. Half-time: One goal each. The visitors restarted strongly and soon had the home defenders in difficulties. Fitzgerald gainvd the lead with 'a good shot. The I.:.;ne. team Pla.,ed with more vim and on several occasions were within an ace of making the scores level. Boyle gained .possession near the half-way line and outpaced his opponents and 'scored the third goal, and a few minutes later added a fourth. Fitzgerald scored the fifth, making his third goal for the match. Result: Howley, 5 goals; Flighfield, 1 goal. LATCHFORD CADETS v. ORFORD JUNIORS. In Victoria Park. Cadets: Miller; Jones, Neilliams; Shaw, Plumb. Massey; Bradshaw, Davenport, Gibson, Sankey and Blackmore. Orford: Walters; Doughty, Gibson; Clare, Bel- shaw, Hirst; Buckley, Griffiths, Austin, Witton and Va,rley. Referee, Mr. P. Gosling. The Cadets won the toss and playing ,againkt cross wind, they led off with an attack on their opponents' citadel, Gibson shooting over the bar. Even .play in midfield ensued, and both teams ap- peared to be evenly matched, but at the end of twenty minutes' play the Juniors' forw'ards ad- vanced on the Cadets' goal b- good combination, and were successful in opening their score. After this reverse the Cadets attacked strongly. and Brad- shaw centring nicely from the right, Gibson made the scores equal. From now to the interval the Juniors had the best of the play. and Buckley scored twice in quick succession. Half-time: Orford, 3 goals; Cadets, 1 goal. The Cadets forced the pace in the second half, and Were soon in an aggressive position. A centte from Davenport missed the goal by- inches only. From the goal kick play was transferred to the Cadets' quarters, where Miller cleared well, but a little later Buckley put the Juniors further ahead. The Cadets now showed better form, and were at- tacking strongly. and after several attacks had been repulsed by the Juniors' defence, Gibson scored the Cadets' second goal. Eveh play followed for a While, but, as the time was approaching the Juniors made another attack or. the Cadets' goal, and Beck• ley scored again. The Cadets made several strong attempts to reduce the arrears, but were unable to to break through the Juniors' defence again. Re- sult: Juniors, 5 goals; Cadets, 2 goals. LATCHFORD RECS. V. BEWSEY A.S. In Victoria Park. Latchford Recs.: Lockhart; Dutton. Willcock ; Bancroft, Areen, Trainor; Heesom, Warburton, Brown, Cooper and James. Bewsey A.S.: Stephens; Mains, Perks; Dickenson, Kent, Worrall; Schofield, Payton, Bradburn, Ry- lance and Flassall. -Referee, Mr. A. Holt. The Recs. won the choice of ends, and had a strong wind in their favour. Braclburn set the ball rolling and advanced towards the Recs.' goal. Willcock cleared and play was quickly transferred to the Adults' end without result. Even play. fob' lowed, and Lockhart and Stephens were prominent with good saves. Attempts at combination were spoiled by the wind, but the Recs. had the best of the exchanges, and after Stephens had sa,ved several good shots, Brown managed to send one past him and opened the Recs.' score, after twenty minutes' play. The Bewsey lads responded in fine style. but could not get past the home defence. Half-time: Latchford Recs., 1 goal; Bewsey, nil. The second portion opened in favour of the Adults. who attacked strongly, but in vain. After a spell of even play the visitors returned to the attack, and from a scrimmage Hassall equalised. Both teams now played keenly and eventually the Recs, gained the upper hand, and James gained the lead with a fine shot from near the touch line. The Adults again took up the offensive, and Lockhart put a grand shot over the bar. The ensuing corner kick saw the ball well placed, and Rylance headed throur,h for the Adults, equalising the scores again. The re- mainder of the game was in favour of the Recs., but the visitors' defence prevailed, and at the call of</p>
        <p>time the result was: Latchford Recs.. 2 goals; Bew- sey A.S., 2 goals. MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. • Grappenhall y. Penketh Juniors; Mr. Burgess. Orford Juniors v. Appleton Hornets; Mr. P. Gos- ling. Howley United v. Bewsey A.S.; Mr. Berry. Highfield v. Latchford Cadets; Mr. Essenhigh. St. Elphin's v. Widnes St. Mary's; Mr. Wright. Widnes Juniors v. Latchford Recs.; Mr. Marsh. TEAMS FOR TO-DAY. WARRINGTON TOWN V. WALKDEN. At Hood-lane. Warrington Town: Kite; Leah, Millward; Siddall, Whitfield, Bunting; Illidge, Heywood, Aspinall, Stephens and Banner. ST. ELPHIN'S v. WIDNES ST. MARY'S. At Steel-street. St. Elphin's: W. Povey; W. Tunstall (captain), V. Furness; D. Cooper, H. Miller, H. Gleave; W. Wat- son, G. England. W. Lee, A. Jones and J. Holden. Reserves: J. Chisholm and J. Barton. GRAPPENHALL v. CROSFIELD'S JUNIORS. At Hood-lane. • Grappenhall: Bartcin (captain); Willis, Clayton; Ormerod, Howells, Cameron; Harris, Dolan, Daven- port', Leigh, Solan. HOWLEY UNITED V. BEWSEY A.S. At Victoria Park. Kick-off 3.15. 1 / Howley: Walters;. Foster, Rowlinson; Hoarty, Miller, Hind; Humphries, Woodhouse, Davies, Fitz- gerald, Hibbert. Reserves: Boyle and Challinor. LATCHFORD CADETS v. HIGHFIELD JUNIORS. At Runcorn. Latchford Cadets: S. Miller; S. Williams, F. San- key; J. Mayers, F. Plumb, S. Jones; J. Porter, W. Massey, S. Gibson, W. Davenport arid A. Bradshaw. Reserves: S. Cartwright and E. Shaw. Team to meet at Warrington Bridge at 1.45, and travel from Bank Quay by train at 2.14. " HEARTS' " A.F.C. v..PENKETH FRIENDS' SCHOOL. At Penketh. Kick-off 3 p.m. Hearts' Team: P. Farrington; G. Crannigan, W. Forster; L. McGeer, R. Hope, G. Reay; J. Clarke. E. Bate, S. Lunt, T. Paton and G. Appleton. Reserves: Hinton and Cummings. Team to meet at Milner-street at 2 p.m. prompt. For the last two seasons "The Hearts" were a Rugby tea&amp; under the name of the Sacred Heart. They have now decided to form an Association team under the name of "the Hearts." There are open dates for teams, average age 17-18. Mr. George P. Crannigan, 30, Milner-street, is the secretary.</p>
